upply-chain anchors; both hash-anchored. **No semver bump.** Per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\", `conflict-gate.yml` is not in the public-surface enumeration (workflow_call inputs on `policy-check.yml`, schema contracts, rule IDs); it is a SCP-self-only workflow that adopters do not consume. Therefore no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry, no version-manifest bump, no release notes — internal hardening | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-038 (slice 020M hash-pinning of policy-check.yml + release-gate.yml). The conflict-gate is the third Python-installing workflow in the SCP repo; closing the unhardened install completes the Python supply-chain hardening across all three SCP-self workflows that pip-install at runtime. Threat surface (PyPI compromise of conflict-gate's deps) was acknowledged at 020M R1 safety as MIN-class because conflict-gate is SCP-self-only (not adopter-facing); a compromise would degrade SCP self's policy-evaluator quality but not directly compromise adopters. The user's directive to clear all outstanding TFs (rather than defer to v1.1 maintenance) raised this from forward-compat to in-flight closure. The `--no-deps` editable install of the local SCP package is the tradeoff: pip refuses `--require-hashes` on editable installs, so the local source tree is installed without re-resolving (its PyPI deps are already hash-verified above). The `ct-auth` vendored wheel is similarly outside `--require-hashes` (pip refuses for local file: URLs); chain-of-trust is the `vendor/**` CODEOWNERS coverage + the wheel being in-tree (no network fetch). Identified at 020M R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 closure |\n| D-040 | 2026-05-02 | Amend the rule-RFC process (`docs/reviews/rule-proposals/README.md` §Process step 2) so that the 48h wall-clock review window is a CEILING (auto-defer trigger) rather than a FLOOR (minimum wait time) **in single-operator mode** (D-031 default at v1.0.0). Rationale: in single-operator mode the proposal author IS the only `SCP-CODEOWNERS`