ner_reviews=false` in single-operator mode. CODEOWNERS still serves as documentation of who SHOULD approve when a second maintainer arrives. Forward-looking discipline preserved: when a second maintainer onboards, flip count=1 + codeowner=true + non-author=true in one operation. Both corrections are landed in `scripts/configure-020d2-required-check.sh` (idempotent re-run reproduces the live state) + `docs/security/branch-protection.md` + WP-SCP-020 §8 risk row. The 2026-07-21 quarterly escalation review re-evaluates the personal-account shape | ACCEPTED | First-PR-through-enforcement is the operational test of the spec; the test surfaced two assumptions that didn't survive contact with GitHub's actual behaviour: (1) the bare context name from 020B(xi) was incomplete (didn't anticipate Actions' `<workflow> / <job>` rendering for reusable-workflow callers), and (2) \"self-approval is the only option\" in 020K's wording was operationally impossible (GitHub forbids PR self-review). Both corrections are operational closure of the spec gap; neither weakens the security posture. The 4-layer protection (signed commits + required check + no admin bypass + tag protection) remains intact. The review-count=0 acceptance is the explicit bus-factor-1 cost; review enforcement returns the moment a second maintainer joins. Identified by USER-GATE-A signoff PR #64's failed merge attempt 2026-04-30 |\n| D-034 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020F renovate-tag-protection (parallel ruleset to D-030's `v*` ruleset, targeting `refs/tags/renovate/v*`). Closes 020F R1 safety review CRIT-SAFE-001: `renovate/v*` tag series unprotected — an attacker with push access could silently re-point `renovate/v1.0.0` to a malicious commit and cascade adversarial Renovate config to every estate adopter on their next scheduled run. The 020J ruleset matches `refs/tags/v*` (literal v-prefix at start) only. New ruleset `scp-tag-protection-renovate-v` applies same protections (deletion / non_fast_forward / update blocked, `enforcement: active`, `bypass_actors: []`). Configured via `scripts/configure-020f-renovate-tag-protection.sh` (idempotent, jq-based JSON construction to eliminate heredoc-interpolation injection surface, multi-property post-creation verification). Documented in `docs/security/branch-protection.md` \"Tag protection — renovate/v* pattern\" section. Symmetric with D-030 in posture and rationale; the only material difference is the tag-pattern segment. The `renovate/v*` series carries equal supply-chain weight as `v*` because every adopter that extends the preset cascades changes from the renovate-pattern tag to their own gate config | ACCEPTED | The 020J ruleset's `v*` literal-prefix pattern is by-design narrow; broadening to all release-track tags would require a separate ruleset per pattern. Renovate's preset shorthand `github>jrnb2024/standards-control-plane//renovate/default#renovate/v1.0.0` is the standard Renovate adopter onboarding shape; without ruleset protection, the entire preset trust chain is rooted in mutable tag refs. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged identically to D-030: the same admin who owns @jrnb2024 is the one publishing tags; the protection guards against accidental force-push, not against malicious admin action. Quarterly review cadence (2026-07-30 in STATUS.md \"Open scheduled follow-ups\") covers both rulesets together. Identified by 020F R1 safety review (workflow run dispatched 2026-04-30 evening) — surfaced under the no-silent-descope governance invariant after fix-round-1 closed the substantive R1 findings |\n| D-035 | 2026-04-30 | Adopt the WP-SCP-020 slice 020G adopter-helper invocation procedure as estate doctrine. The required-check context name `policy-check / scp/policy-check` traces to D-033 (the rendered Actions check-run name established for the SCP self-dogfood gate). Adopters of the SCP federation primitive enable this required status check on their default branch via `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` from the SCP repo. The script applies branch protection in two API calls (unified PUT for status-checks/admins/reviews + dedicated POST to `required_signatures` sub-resource per the GitHub API shape), refuses to bypass admin enforcement without an explicit `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` acknowledgement flag, refuses to run in CI (CI=true / GITHUB_ACTIONS=true env-var guards), preserves any pre-existing `required_pull_request_reviews` shape rather than nulling it, and emits an invocation-log block (operator, timestamp, script SHA256 + git SHA, target repo+branch, before/after API JSON) for paste into `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`. The log commit is part of the invocation procedure: an apply without the log entry is considered unrecorded. Estate cascade rollout (WP-SCP-024) drives this script across PIM, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower; FLA pilot (WP-SCP-020.1) is the canary first invocation | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-030 (020J `v*` tag-protection), D-031 (020K CODEOWNERS personal-account), D-032 (020D2 SCP-self required-status-check), D-034 (020F `renovate/v*` tag-protection): every operational procedure that mutates third-party repository state ships an idempotent applier + ratifying D-NNN + invocation log convention. The bootstrap-only / no-CI guard reflects that this script writes to a third-party repo's branch protection — an automation tool that ran it unattended could mass-mutate the estate via stolen credentials before any operator noticed. The `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` confirmation reflects that `--no-enforce-admins` defeats the whole point of the federation primitive on the target repo and so should require explicit intent. The `required_pull_request_reviews` preservation reflects that adopters with multi-maintainer teams have legitimate review-shape state that 020G must not destroy. Identified at WP-SCP-020 §4 020G + 020G R1 review (3 MAJ + 5 MIN + 2 nit on safety, 2 MAJ + 4 MIN + nit on completeness, 3 MAJ + 2 MIN + 2 nit on correctness — fixpoint via fix-round-1) |\n| D-036 | 2026-05-01 | Adopt `policies/VERSIONING.md` MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH semver contract on the SCP federation primitive's public surfaces (`workflow_call.inputs` on `policy-check.yml`, `schemas/policy-check-summary.schema.json` + `schemas/rule-config.schema.json`, `SCP-R-NNN` rule IDs) WITH a one-release deprecation ramp before any breaking change AND `docs/reviews/rule-proposals/` RFC-lite process (quorum=1 SCP-CODEOWNER per D-031 single-operator mode; 48h wall-clock window with weekend-counts; 0 approvals = auto-defer with `defer` label; bypass-introducing proposals require explicit §5 enumeration and a non-waivable 48h window per 020H.1 R1 SAFE-MAJ-001 closure) as estate doctrine for SCP federation primitive versioning and rule governance. Closes WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 (i)+(ii)+(iii) and BS-5. Forward-compat to 2-of-N quorum when a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation path, 2026-07-21 review). Internal-only surfaces (`lib/policy_check_invocation.sh`, `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock`, helper scripts in `scripts/`, Renovate preset internal shape, OPA Rego helpers in `policies/scp_common.rego`) are explicitly NOT covered — they may be refactored without notice | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-035 (020G adopter-helper invocation): every new governance procedure binding adopter behaviour ships a ratifying D-NNN row to make the doctrine searchable in the canonical decision-record home rather than buried in a slice DISPATCH-NOTE. The one-release deprecation ramp is the MINIMUM version-skew adopters must tolerate before a surface vanishes; combined with the Renovate preset's automated SHA-pin bumps, this gives every adopter at least one full PR cycle's worth of warning annotations before a breaking change lands. The RFC-lite process is intentionally lightweight at v1.0.0 because the SCP estate is single-operator; the auto-defer mechanism is currently a manual close + label step. As estate scale grows (WP-SCP-024 cascade), the auto-defer mechanism may be lifted to a GitHub Action that runs on a schedule. Identified at WP-SCP-020 §4 020H.1 plan row + 020H.1 R1 completeness COMP-MAJ-003 closure |\n| D-037 | 2026-05-01 | Grant `issues: write` permission to the `release-gate-violation-issue` job in `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` (per WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1, closing TF-020H3rg-003). Privilege scope is the JOB level (not workflow default); the release-gate evaluation job continues to run with `contents: read` only. The new job fires only on `needs.release-gate.result == 'failure' && github.event_name == 'push'` — workflow_dispatch dry-runs and clean push:tags runs never reach it. The job's only mutation is `gh issue create` / `gh issue comment` / `gh label create --force` against the SCP source repo's own Issues namespace; no other write surface is unlocked. The auto-opened issue assigns `@jrnb2024` (single-operator mode per D-031); forward-compat env `SCP_RELEASE_GATE_ASSIGNEES` for 2nd-maintainer escalation. Symmetric with D-029 (020C.1 `statuses: write` for read-back commit-status) — a privilege-write expansion ratified by an amending decision row that names the precise threat-surface and bounded behaviour | ACCEPTED | The release-gate's last-line-of-defense alarm needs SOME write surface to surface beyond the workflow log; opening a GitHub issue is the lightest-weight signal that survives operator absence (the workflow log is ephemeral; the issue persists + assigns + sends a notification). Privilege-of-least-privilege is honoured by job-level scoping rather than workflow-level — the gate-evaluation steps (pip install, schema validate, ramp check, expired-config check) cannot accidentally open issues because they run in the release-gate job which has only `contents: read`. CODEOWNERS coverage on `.github/**` (per WP-SCP-020 020K) gates any change to release-gate.yml itself. The forward-compat env mirrors `SCP_REGRESSION_ASSIGNEES` from canary-replay.yml. Identified at WP-SCP-022 020H.3.1 R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 + SAFE-MAJ-001 closures |\n| D-038 | 2026-05-02 | Adopt hash-pinned Python supply-chain installs across the SCP federation primitive's two YAML/JSON-validating workflows (`.github/workflows/policy-check.yml` × 4 install sites, `.github/workflows/release-gate.yml` × 1 install site) via `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/policy-check.txt`. Top-level pins (`pyyaml==6.0.2` + `jsonschema==4.23.0`) match the prior inline pins exactly — no version bump on the public Python deps; only the install mechanism is hardened. Lockfile generated by `pip-compile --generate-hashes` from `requirements/policy-check.in`; full transitive closure (`attrs`, `jsonschema-specifications`, `referencing`, `rpds-py`) hash-pinned with all-platform wheel SHA256s. Closes WP-SCP-022 TF-020H3rg-002 (filed at 020H.3 R1 safety MIN-SAFE-006 — a PyPI compromise of either pinned version could substitute a malicious package on the next install; `--require-hashes` causes pip to refuse install when the served wheel's SHA256 is not in the lockfile). PATCH bump (v1.0.0 → v1.0.1) per `policies/VERSIONING.md` PATCH category (\"internal refactors of helpers/lib\"); no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry required (no surface deprecation, no behaviour change for adopters). Lockfile regeneration command captured in `requirements/policy-check.txt` header AND `requirements/policy-check.in` documentation block | ACCEPTED | Symmetric supply-chain hardening with the pre-existing `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` (OPA + Conftest + Regal binary SHA256 verification) and Sigstore attestation soft-warn for OPA (TF-007). All three layers — Python deps, OPA-family binaries, GitHub Actions SHA pins — are now hash-anchored at SCP source. The lockfile + `.in` source pair mirrors `scripts/.tool-versions` + `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` (input + lockfile pattern). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged for lockfile regeneration: an operator who regenerates without re-reviewing the pip-compile output could introduce drifted transitive versions; mitigation is the `requirements/policy-check.in` source-of-truth (pins only the top-level deps; transitive resolution is automatic but reviewable in the diff). Future upgrades to `pyyaml` / `jsonschema` itself follow the same path: edit `.in`, regenerate `.txt`, review diff. The Sigstore re-tightening (TF-007) is parallel work on a different supply-chain dimension. Identified at 020H.3 R1 safety MIN-SAFE-006 closure |\n| D-039 | 2026-05-02 | Extend the slice 020M hash-pinned Python supply-chain install pattern to the SCP-self conflict-gate workflow (`.github/workflows/conflict-gate.yml` \"Install Python dependencies\" step) per WP-SCP-022 slice 020N, closing TF-020M-001. New `requirements/conflict-gate.in` (top-level pins matching `pyproject.toml` `[project.dependencies]` + `[project.optional-dependencies.dev]`: fastapi, uvicorn, httpx, jsonschema, pydantic, pyyaml, pytest, ruff, mypy) + generated `requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (pip-compile --generate-hashes under Python 3.12 to match `setup-python: python-version: \"3.12\"`; full transitive closure with all-platform wheel SHA256s). Install pattern: (i) `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements/conflict-gate.txt` (PyPI deps); (ii) `pip install --no-deps vendor/python/ct_auth-0.8.0-py3-none-any.whl` (local vendored wheel — not on PyPI; CODEOWNERS-protected via `vendor/** @jrnb2024`); (iii) `pip install --no-deps -e .` (editable local SCP package without re-resolving deps already hash-verified in step (i)); (iv) post-install version-pin assertion (`assert yaml.__version__ == \"6.0.3\"; assert jsonschema.__version__ == \"4.26.0\"; assert fastapi.__version__ == \"0.136.1\"; assert pydantic.VERSION == \"2.13.3\"`) defending against future-PR drift (mirrors 020M SAFE-MAJ-002 pattern). Deliberate pyyaml-version drift accepted: conflict-gate.txt resolves pyyaml==6.0.3, policy-check.txt pins pyyaml==6.0.2 — independent workflows with independent supply-chain anchors; both hash-anchored. **No semver bump.** Per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\", `conflict-gate.yml` is not in the public-surface enumeration (workflow_call inputs on `policy-check.yml`, schema contracts, rule IDs); it is a SCP-self-only workflow that adopters do not consume. Therefore no `policies/deprecations.yaml` entry, no version-manifest bump, no release notes — internal hardening | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-038 (slice 020M hash-pinning of policy-check.yml + release-gate.yml). The conflict-gate is the third Python-installing workflow in the SCP repo; closing the unhardened install completes the Python supply-chain hardening across all three SCP-self workflows that pip-install at runtime. Threat surface (PyPI compromise of conflict-gate's deps) was acknowledged at 020M R1 safety as MIN-class because conflict-gate is SCP-self-only (not adopter-facing); a compromise would degrade SCP self's policy-evaluator quality but not directly compromise adopters. The user's directive to clear all outstanding TFs (rather than defer to v1.1 maintenance) raised this from forward-compat to in-flight closure. The `--no-deps` editable install of the local SCP package is the tradeoff: pip refuses `--require-hashes` on editable installs, so the local source tree is installed without re-resolving (its PyPI deps are already hash-verified above). The `ct-auth` vendored wheel is similarly outside `--require-hashes` (pip refuses for local file: URLs); chain-of-trust is the `vendor/**` CODEOWNERS coverage + the wheel being in-tree (no network fetch). Identified at 020M R1 safety SAFE-MIN-003 closure |\n| D-040 | 2026-05-02 | Amend the rule-RFC process (`docs/reviews/rule-proposals/README.md` §Process step 2) so that the 48h wall-clock review window is a CEILING (auto-defer trigger) rather than a FLOOR (minimum wait time) **in single-operator mode** (D-031 default at v1.0.0). Rationale: in single-operator mode the proposal author IS the only `SCP-CODEOWNERS` member; there are no external reviewers for the wall-clock pause to enable. The recursive 3-lens R1/R2 adversarial review (per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`) IS the adversarial-review surface; the wall-clock pause was vestigial theatre that produced no additional safety. The operator MAY merge a proposal as soon as adversarial review reaches fixpoint (no new CRIT/MAJ on a complete cycle) AND the proposal PR is open with the `scp-rule-proposal` label applied. The 48h CEILING remains in force: zero approvals by 48h still triggers auto-defer (`defer` label per slice 020L label-provisioning). When a second maintainer onboards (D-031 escalation, 2026-07-21 review), the 48h becomes a mandatory MINIMUM to give the second maintainer time to review — the CEILING-not-FLOOR posture is single-operator-only. Bypass-introducing proposals (`Bypass-surface non-empty: true`) retain their non-waivable 48h MINIMUM regardless of operator-count posture, because the additional adversarial-review value of a wall-clock pause IS material when a new bypass surface is being introduced (the operator may catch their own bypass-introducing PR's flaws on second look more reliably than for a non-bypass additive rule). Surfaced by 020L dogfood (the first RFC actually walked through the process). Closes WP-SCP-022 020L post-R2 process-gap | ACCEPTED | The 020L dogfood surfaced this gap operationally: the operator (single-maintainer) reached R1+R2 fixpoint on a non-bypass proposal and was about to wait 48h before merging — at which point the wait was identified as theatre rather than safety (no second pair of eyes to chime in). Theatre-vs-safety is the doctrine question the dogfood is designed to surface cheaply. The amendment narrows the discretionary posture to single-operator + non-bypass + post-fixpoint, leaving every other shape of the 48h window unchanged: bypass-introducing proposals keep the non-waivable minimum (the threat-surface justifies the wait); the post-2nd-maintainer-onboard 48h becomes mandatory minimum so the second maintainer has time to review; the 48h CEILING for auto-defer remains so abandoned proposals are surfaced. Symmetric with D-033's \"operational reality must amend the spec when the spec encounters a constraint that wasn't anticipated\" pattern. Identified at WP-SCP-022 slice 020L post-R2 fixpoint by operator (`@jrnb2024`) when reviewing the merge-readiness of PR #89 |\n| D-041 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard data shape (`schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` v0.1) + opt-in adopter participation model per WP-SCP-023 023B (this PR). The schema defines aggregated per-rule counts (raw_findings, denies, waived, rule_config_disabled), waivers aggregate (active_count, by_rule_id, expiring_within_30d), rule-config aggregate (disabled_rules, expiring_within_30d), repo / ref / commit / scp_version / RFC3339 emitted_at, and a verdict (allow/deny/warn). The schema EXPLICITLY DOES NOT include waiver content (`reason`, `approved_by`, `waiver_id` strings) — invariant 2 of WP-SCP-023 plan-doc binds this. The reusable workflow gains a `scorecard-emit: bool` `workflow_call` input (default false) so existing adopters see no behaviour change; opt-in adopters set `with: scorecard-emit: true`. Schema versioning: MAJOR-pinned per `policies/VERSIONING.md` D-036 + WP-SCP-023 plan-doc §10 Q1 — aggregator drops emits with unknown `schema_version`. Verification: GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation (OIDC-based) via `actions/attest-build-provenance@v4.1.0` (SHA `a2bbfa25...`) at emit time; aggregator verifies via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow` (per D-042 to be filed at slice 023C). v1.2.0 cut on this slice's merge per VERSIONING.md MINOR category | ACCEPTED | This is the first slice of WP-SCP-023's implementation chain (023A plan-doc landed at PR #97, commit `7075234`, 2026-05-03 R4 fixpoint). The data shape was specified at plan-doc level; this slice ratifies it as code surface. Default-false on the workflow input is the load-bearing safety property: existing adopters cannot have new behaviour spring on them; opt-in is explicit per invariant 4. The waiver-content exclusion (invariant 2) is enforced by the schema's `additionalProperties: false` constraint on every level + the explicit field enumeration. The MAJOR-pinning posture means schema breakage requires v2.0.0 + adopter migration; v0.1 prefix on `schema_version` reflects the freedom to amend until first adopter opts in. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-041 reservation |\n| D-042 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard aggregator pipeline trust model per WP-SCP-023 023C (this PR). Mechanism: per-emit verification via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow <expected_workflow_ref>` (the GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation OIDC pattern, mandatory per the D-042 reservation note above + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure); index trust rooted in git's required-signed-commits (slice 020J) + `output/scorecards/** @jrnb2024` CODEOWNERS coverage (slice 023A) + branch protection on main (slice 020D2), NOT a separate Ed25519 key layer. The aggregator runs as `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` on a weekly cron (Monday 06:00 UTC) + `workflow_dispatch`; two-job structure (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped via job-level permissions, mirroring the 023B `attest-scorecard` job split); reads `docs/scorecards/opt-in-registry.yaml` (CODEOWNERS-protected at `docs/scorecards/**`), pulls each adopter's latest green policy-check run's `scorecard-emit.json` artifact, verifies attestation, schema-validates emit, aggregates into `output/scorecards/index.json` (schema: `schemas/scorecard-index.schema.json`), and opens a PR for operator review. Failure modes (verification_failure / unreachable / no_emit) record loudly per-adopter — never silently dropped. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by schema `additionalProperties: false` at every level). Internal-only workflow per `policies/VERSIONING.md` §\"Scope\" (mirrors 020N conflict-gate.yml posture); no semver bump. Verifies WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 6 (\"Aggregator output is Ed25519-signed\") in spirit — Sigstore/Fulcio uses Ed25519 under the hood, managed by GitHub rather than by an SCP-side private key, eliminating the private-key-distribution surface | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 (per-emit data shape + opt-in model) — D-041 ratified the EMITTER side of the trust model; D-042 ratifies the AGGREGATOR / VERIFIER side. The mandatory `--signer-workflow` constraint was already pre-committed at the D-042 reservation note above (closes 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001) — this slice ratifies it as workflow code. The Ed25519-via-Sigstore framing eliminates the chicken-and-egg of \"where does the SCP private key live and how does the aggregator hold it without exposing adopters\" — GitHub manages the keys via OIDC + Sigstore. The two-job permission split (aggregate read-only + commit write-scoped) is identical to 023B's `policy-check` + `attest-scorecard` split (closes the analogous threat surface where workflow-level write permissions could be exploited by a compromised aggregate-side step). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-042 reservation + 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001 closure |\n| D-043 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard exposure surface — markdown report (`docs/scorecards/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`) + MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` — per WP-SCP-023 023D (this PR). Markdown generator at `scripts/generate-scorecard-report.py` produces deterministic golden-file-tested output reading `output/scorecards/index.json`; aggregator workflow `.github/workflows/scorecard-aggregator.yml` extended in the commit job to invoke the generator + add the rendered markdown to the operator-review PR alongside the index. MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard` (registered in `src/standards_control_plane/mcp_server/tools.py`): read-only contract per WP-SCP-023 plan-doc invariant 5; request shape `{repo_filter?, since_emitted_at?}`; response shape excludes waiver content (`reason` / `approved_by` / `waiver_id` — invariant 2) by Pydantic model construction (extra=forbid + explicit field whitelist). The MCP method reads `output/scorecards/index.json` from the SCP repo working copy at call time; staleness is observable via the response's `aggregated_at` field. ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 documents the adopter onboarding workflow. NEVER carries waiver content (invariant 2 enforced by Pydantic models + schema + dedicated pytest scans on both markdown + MCP responses) | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-041 + D-042 — D-043 ratifies the CONSUMER side: markdown for operators/auditors, MCP for agents/tooling. Read-only contract + Pydantic-enforced field whitelist + golden-file pytest together preserve invariant 2 across both surfaces. The MCP method does NOT introduce a new authentication primitive — it rides on WP-SCP-021's existing Ed25519 receipt mechanism (consult-class semantics). The markdown report is informational and CODEOWNERS-protected via `docs/scorecards/** @jrnb2024`; operators can hand-edit before merging the aggregator PR if needed (regenerator overwrites on next run). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the MCP server runs at acc.brokapps.ai; if down, agents lose `consult_scorecard` access but the markdown report + git index remain consultable. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-043 reservation |\n| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs while recommending the v1.0.0 pin; `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. The CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient: workflow-side hardcoding of `--tooling-slice-id` and omission of `--allow-not-applicable` in 024B-extras is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\n\n> D-044 limitation note: the CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient. 024B-extras must hardcode `--tooling-slice-id` per workflow file and omit `--allow-not-applicable` from cohort workflows; that workflow-side hardcoding is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
