{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_98","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"bash -lc 'cd /Users/amplience/Projects/standards-control-plane && rg -n \\\"enable-required-check\\\\.sh\\\" tests scripts docs | sed -n \\\"1,120p\\\"'\"","aggregated_output":"scripts/enable-required-check.sh:129:Usage: enable-required-check.sh --repo OWNER/NAME --branch BRANCH [flags]\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:7:RESTORE_SCRIPT=\"${REPO_ROOT}/scripts/enable-required-check.sh\"\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:83:| D-035 | 2026-04-30 | Adopt the WP-SCP-020 slice 020G adopter-helper invocation procedure as estate doctrine. The required-check context name `policy-check / scp/policy-check` traces to D-033 (the rendered Actions check-run name established for the SCP self-dogfood gate). Adopters of the SCP federation primitive enable this required status check on their default branch via `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` from the SCP repo. The script applies branch protection in two API calls (unified PUT for status-checks/admins/reviews + dedicated POST to `required_signatures` sub-resource per the GitHub API shape), refuses to bypass admin enforcement without an explicit `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` acknowledgement flag, refuses to run in CI (CI=true / GITHUB_ACTIONS=true env-var guards), preserves any pre-existing `required_pull_request_reviews` shape rather than nulling it, and emits an invocation-log block (operator, timestamp, script SHA256 + git SHA, target repo+branch, before/after API JSON) for paste into `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`. The log commit is part of the invocation procedure: an apply without the log entry is considered unrecorded. Estate cascade rollout (WP-SCP-024) drives this script across PIM, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower; FLA pilot (WP-SCP-020.1) is the canary first invocation | ACCEPTED | Symmetric posture with D-030 (020J `v*` tag-protection), D-031 (020K CODEOWNERS personal-account), D-032 (020D2 SCP-self required-status-check), D-034 (020F `renovate/v*` tag-protection): every operational procedure that mutates third-party repository state ships an idempotent applier + ratifying D-NNN + invocation log convention. The bootstrap-only / no-CI guard reflects that this script writes to a third-party repo's branch protection — an automation tool that ran it unattended could mass-mutate the estate via stolen credentials before any operator noticed. The `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` confirmation reflects that `--no-enforce-admins` defeats the whole point of the federation primitive on the target repo and so should require explicit intent. The `required_pull_request_reviews` preservation reflects that adopters with multi-maintainer teams have legitimate review-shape state that 020G must not destroy. Identified at WP-SCP-020 §4 020G + 020G R1 review (3 MAJ + 5 MIN + 2 nit on safety, 2 MAJ + 4 MIN + nit on completeness, 3 MAJ + 2 MIN + 2 nit on correctness — fixpoint via fix-round-1) |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:92:| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs while recommending the v1.0.0 pin; `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. The CLI guard is necessary-but-not-sufficient: workflow-side hardcoding of `--tooling-slice-id` and omission of `--allow-not-applicable` in 024B-extras is the canonical closure path for the operator-supplied not-applicable bypass surface (see FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001). Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:93:| D-047 | 2026-05-10 | Adopt WP-SCP-024 024B-extras operational contracts: `scripts/enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>` restores branch protection from the captured prior-state log entry by transforming GET-shape JSON into the PUT body, stripping envelope fields recursively, converting `enforce_admins.enabled` to a boolean, and replaying `required_signatures` through the dedicated sub-resource; restore-mode posture-degradation flags `--i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement` and `--i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks` are mandatory when the target removes those protections; forward-mode now performs a path-pinned workflow lookup for `.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml`, queries workflow-runs without a branch filter, validates `--expected-wrapper-sha` against actual release tags via `gh api repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/git/ref/tags/<tag>`, and treats the working-tree diff of `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` as part of prior-entry detection; the new CI workflow `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` wires the CLI gate into PR checks with `paths-ignore: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[AB]*/**`; ADOPT-001 gains §12.8 break-glass procedure; STATUS.md and the DISPATCH-NOTE placeholder for the step-7 operator demo are updated in-slice. | ACCEPTED | The 024B split left the restore / break-glass / workflow-integration surface as the deferred half so the review load stayed bounded. This decision ratifies the operational contract that lets operators disable and re-enable the federation primitive safely without widening the adopter-facing API surface, while preserving the bootstrap guard that prevents tooling slices from self-blocking their own PRs. The release-tag SHA requirement is intentionally anchored to actual git tags, not arbitrary commits, so Gate 3 can re-arm against a concrete release artifact rather than against moving main-head. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: the helper remains operator-run, and the restore demo evidence is still an operator-interactive step outside Codex dispatch. Identified at WP-SCP-024 024B-extras authoring time. | \ndocs/security/branch-protection.md:80:  adopter helper is `scripts/enable-required-check.sh`; its\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:848:./scripts/enable-required-check.sh \\\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:869:**Required-check context name.** The required status check MUST be configured to the **check-run** context `policy-check / scp/policy-check`, NOT to the **commit-status** context `scp/policy-check-readback`. The check-run is the authoritative gate (driven by GitHub Actions); the readback is an informational commit-status forge-able by any runner with `statuses: write`. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper defaults to the correct check-run context per D-033.\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:904:- **Multi-maintainer adopters.** Configure branch protection with `required_approving_review_count >= 1`, `require_code_owner_reviews: true`, **AND `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`**. (GitHub enforces non-self-approval at the platform level — there is no separate REST API field for that constraint; the WP-SCP-020 plan corpus uses `require_review_from_non_author: true` as shorthand for this platform behaviour, but no API field needs to be set.) Gate 1 is **machine-enforced** — a bypass PR cannot merge without a CODEOWNER review from someone other than the author. The `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` setting is non-optional in this mode: without it, an approving CODEOWNER review on an initial commit persists across subsequent commits in the same PR, so an attacker who obtains a clean review can later push commits that add `.scp/rule-config.yaml disable: true`, change `scp_bypass: true`, or weaken the wrapper's fork-PR `if:` — and merge without re-review. Dismissal fires on ANY push to the PR branch regardless of which files the new commit touches — it is not path-selective. Stale-review dismissal is the enforcement complement to `require_code_owner_reviews`. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper preserves your existing review-shape verbatim and does NOT set `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` for you; configure it explicitly via the GitHub branch-protection UI or API. (As of fix-round-5 of this slice, `enable-required-check.sh` emits a stderr WARNING when the preserved existing review config has `dismiss_stale_reviews: false` — see also the \"verify the gate is live\" block in §12.7.3.)\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:934:2. After merge, remove the required-check from branch protection. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper does NOT have a removal mode — invoke the GitHub API directly:\ndocs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md:1200:scripts/enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:29:1. Defines the **adopter cohort** for cascade: `{pim, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower}` per D-035 (= the 5 estate repos enumerated when D-035 ratified `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` as canonical onboarding mechanism). FLA is explicitly a separate track per operator direction (2026-05-03 — \"FLA-independent implementation slices\") + the WP-SCP-020.1 canary continues on its own cadence.\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:30:2. Defines a **per-adopter onboarding contract** that each cascade slice satisfies: (a) caller wrapper PR'd into the adopter repo with SCP federation primitive SHA-pinned per VERSIONING.md (D-036); (b) `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` invocation per D-035 with invocation-log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` — **conditional on the slice's `cascade-status:` declaration per §5.2 + invariant 2** (only `onboarded` and `onboarded-operator-bump` slices add a log entry; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` slices MUST NOT modify the log file); (c) post-bake observation window of ≥1 calendar week before cascade declares the adopter onboarded; (d) opt-in scorecard-emit added as a follow-up sub-slice once TF-023E-002 closes AND the adopter's repo visibility allows OIDC artefact attestation per TF-023E-001.\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:31:3. Defines a **canary-first sequencing** that minimises blast radius: smallest cooperative adopter first (PIM target), control-tower (high-traffic flagship) second, then mapp-doc-agent + recommender as a paired slice, then shopify-app last. The sequencing is reversible per slice — any adopter can be removed via PR to the adopter wrapper file + a parallel `enable-required-check.sh --restore` invocation (added at slice 024B).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:44:2. **Required-check enablement is operator-run + invocation-logged + CI-enforced.** `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` per D-035 runs from the operator's local environment (the script refuses CI=true / GITHUB_ACTIONS=true per D-035), with an invocation log entry under `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` (the D-035-canonical estate-cascade audit trail; the file's existing comment \"Estate cascade rollout = WP-SCP-024\" reserves it for this WP) that captures operator, timestamp, script SHA256, target repo+branch, before/after API JSON. An apply without the log entry is unrecorded. **CI enforcement (lands in 024B as `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh`)** parses DISPATCH-NOTE `cascade-status:` and `slice-type:` fields. `slice-type:` is `tooling` or `cohort`; tooling slices are 024A, 024B-core, 024B-extras, and 024G, while cohort slices are 024C, 024D, 024E, and 024F. **Fail-closed default (closes 024A R6 R6-NEW-MAJ-003 safety):** if `cascade-status:` is absent OR contains a value not in the enumerated set `{onboarded, onboarded-operator-bump, blocked-on-adopter-conflict}`, the script exits non-zero (CI fails). Recognised values map to check-paths:\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:47:   - **`cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict` (invariant-10 path):** PR MUST NOT modify `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` (no `enable-required-check.sh` invocation occurred — closing the bypass surface that R3 safety surfaced) AND MUST reference the filed `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` TF in DISPATCH-NOTE matching the standardised prefix **with meaningful content** (format spec below; bare-prefix stub fails CI per the same rule above).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:70:7. **Per-adopter rollback is single-operator-doable.** Each cascade slice ships a rollback procedure: `enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>` reverts branch protection to the captured before-state, and the adopter wrapper PR can be reverted via standard `git revert`. Rollback time SLO: **<30 minutes from operator decision to merge-restored state — claimed only after slice 024B closes with a demonstrated real-repo round-trip on a throw-away test repo (not a dry-run mock).** The SLO is aspirational at 024A merge; 024B's acceptance criterion in §6 makes the round-trip a hard predecessor for 024C. Documented per cascade slice. Closes 024A R1 MAJ-SAFE-003.\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:98:| **T-024-01** | Operator runs `enable-required-check.sh` against the wrong adopter repo, mass-mutating branch protection on a non-target repo. | `enable-required-check.sh` invocation. | D-035 + script's CI=true / GITHUB_ACTIONS=true refusal + script's `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` confirmation for `--no-enforce-admins`. Cascade slice's invocation log captures target repo+branch BEFORE invocation, surfacing typos for review. |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:133:     - **`onboarded`** — success-path via Renovate; an `enable-required-check.sh` invocation occurred + the log entry is in this PR + bake observed a Renovate-issued SHA pin bump cycle clean.\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:134:     - **`onboarded-operator-bump`** — R-024-07 fallback path; an `enable-required-check.sh` invocation occurred + the log entry is in this PR + bake satisfied via operator-driven SHA pin bump (NOT Renovate); STATUS.md gains a `TF-024X-renovate-<adopter-slug>` row gating Threshold A credit (per R-024-07).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:142:3. **`enable-required-check.sh` invocation** — operator-run from local machine after the adopter wrapper PR merges AND at least one green `policy-check / scp/policy-check` CI run is observed on the adopter repo (satisfying §5.4's safety check), AND BEFORE the post-bake observation window begins. Invocation captured in the branch-protection log per (2). **Steps (3) and (4) are not concurrent — (3) enables the gate that (4) observes.** Closes 024A R1 CMP-nit-001.\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:164:D-044 ratifies this script's operational contract: bootstrap-only (refuses CI=true / GITHUB_ACTIONS=true per D-035 symmetry), validates `--scp-sha` against the SCP main HEAD at invocation time (warns if it's not main HEAD; refuses to emit if it's a non-existent SHA), and emits a manifest file `MANIFEST.json` listing every file emitted + its SHA256 for audit. The local manifest provides the equivalent forensic-traceability surface without coupling to the branch-protection log (reserved for `enable-required-check.sh` per D-035, with the log entry remaining the operator's responsibility in 024C+).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:168:### 5.4 `enable-required-check.sh` — already exists, extended at 024B\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:170:Per D-035 the script already exists at `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` from WP-SCP-020 020G. WP-SCP-024 024B adds:\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:206:| 024B | 024B-core: `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` + `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` + `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` + scaffolder unit tests | Scaffolder emits a known-fixture wrapper that schema-validates against the canonical wrapper shape from ADOPT-001 §12; `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` parses DISPATCH-NOTE `cascade-status:` field and implements all four CI behaviours per invariant 2 — **fail-closed default** (absent or unrecognised value → exit non-zero); for `onboarded`: requires log entry + target match; for `onboarded-operator-bump`: requires log entry + target match + `TF-024X-renovate-<adopter-slug>` STATUS.md row matching invariant 2's **regex format spec literally** (file-agnostic; `\\S` non-whitespace anchor at description start; ≥20 char total); for `blocked-on-adopter-conflict`: requires `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference (in DISPATCH-NOTE) matching the same regex spec literally AND **asserts log file was NOT modified** (closes the R3 safety bypass + R4 safety stub bypass + R6 safety regex/whitespace + file-scoping bypasses); D-044 filed. **024B-extras is deferred**: `enable-required-check.sh --restore` round-trip, ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass, workflow wiring, and step 7 operator demo move to the sibling slice. **024B-core is a hard predecessor for 024C; 024B-extras must also merge before 024C opens per the split decision.** | Codex executor | Per WP-SCP-022 dispatch contract |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:223:| **R-024-03** | Cascade slice onboards an adopter whose internal review shape differs from SCP's (e.g. requires 2 reviewers). | The adopter's existing `required_pull_request_reviews` is preserved by `enable-required-check.sh` per D-035 (\"preserves any pre-existing `required_pull_request_reviews` shape rather than nulling it\"). No cascade-side override. | 024B + each cascade slice |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:224:| **R-024-04** | Operator runs `enable-required-check.sh` against the wrong adopter. | T-024-01 mitigation: invocation log + script `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` + script's CI-refusal. Cascade slice's R1 correctness lens verifies log target matches DISPATCH-NOTE target. | 024B + each cascade slice |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:235:| **R-024-15** | An adopter accepted into the cascade then later wants to opt out. | Invariant 7: per-adopter rollback is single-operator-doable + <30min SLO. The adopter's `enable-required-check.sh --restore` invocation reverts branch-protection; the wrapper PR can be reverted via `git revert`. Documented per cascade slice. | Each cascade slice |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:271:3. **`enable-required-check.sh --restore` test surface** — the rollback mode lands at 024B. How is it tested without actually mutating an estate repo? **Resolution path (closes 024A R1 MAJ-SAFE-003):** Q3 closes only when 024B-extras step 7 acceptance criterion is met — i.e. a real-repo round-trip on a throw-away test repo (NOT a dry-run mock). Until that demonstration is complete, invariant 7's <30-min rollback SLO is aspirational and 024C MUST NOT open. (024B was split into 024B-core + 024B-extras on 2026-05-09; the round-trip demo lives in 024B-extras per docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md.)\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md:96:020G  scripts/enable-required-check.sh with branch-protection-log.md\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md:123:| 020G | **Branch-protection automation.** `scripts/enable-required-check.sh`. Acceptance: (i) header asserts `gh --version >= 2.40`, `jq` on PATH, required fine-grained PAT scopes (`administration:write` on single target repo only); (ii) refuses without `--repo` and `--branch`; `--plan` flag prints PUT payload without applying; `--enforce-admins` flag defaults true; (iii) logs invocation (SHA of script, operator, timestamp, target, before/after API response JSON) to `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` — the log commit is part of the invocation procedure; (iv) bootstrap-only header declares script is not run unattended. |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md:236:- **U-arch-3** → **CLOSED**. `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` `--branch` required arg.\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/adversarial-review-round-1.md:41:- **[MINOR | in-scope]** 020G's `enable-required-check.sh` is \"human-invoked, audit-logged\" with no definition of what \"audit-logged\" means. Fix: write the log target into the slice deliverable.\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/adversarial-review-round-1.md:84:- **M5 [in-scope slice 020G]** `enable-required-check.sh` token scope + audit undefined. Plan must specify: fine-grained PAT with `administration:write` on single target repo only; script refuses without `--repo`; logs invocation to `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md`; bootstrap-only header.\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/adversarial-review-round-1.md:163:- **#8 [in-scope]** `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` (020G) should declare required `gh` version, token scopes, `jq` dependency; `--plan` flag.\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md:3:This log captures every invocation of `scripts/enable-required-check.sh`\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md:31:<!-- new entries appended below this line by `enable-required-check.sh` operators -->\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/plan-fixpoint-log.md:40:| M-sec-5 | `enable-required-check.sh` token scope + audit | Slice 020G acceptance rewritten: fine-grained PAT `administration:write` on single repo, refuses without `--repo`, `--plan` flag, invocation logged to `branch-protection-log.md`, bootstrap-only header. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/plan-fixpoint-log.md:60:| m-devex-8 | `enable-required-check.sh` robustness | Slice 020G acceptance: gh version check, jq dep, fine-grained PAT, `--plan` flag. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r2-package.json:15:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3.json:32:    \"result\": \"{\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"SAFETY_BYPASS\\\",\\\"reviewer_id\\\":\\\"r3-safety-bypass-020h3\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-022/020h3\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"docs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md \\u00a712.7; .github/workflows/policy-check.yml; scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh; scripts/enable-required-check.sh; renovate/default.json; docs/DECISIONS.md; docs/security/branch-protection.md; STATUS.md\\\",\\\"self_reported_concerns\\\":[],\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R3-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4 Gate 1 uses 'typically `services.yml`, `output/findings/waivers.json`, or `policies/**`' to describe which paths an adopter should CODEOWNERS-protect. The word 'typically' leaves open the reading that services.yml alone is sufficient. In fact `output/findings/waivers.json` is the minimum-necessary coverage for Gate 1 to fire on every bypass PR: verify-bypass-pairing.sh line 28\\u201331 machine-enforces that waivers.json must be in the bypass diff (Gate 3). An adopter who protects only `services.yml` (not `waivers.json`) AND separately misses \\u00a712.7.1's MUST wrapper-CODEOWNERS requirement creates a Gate 1 gap: a bypass PR that adds a D-NNN row, a waivers.json entry, and sets scp_bypass=true in the wrapper \\u2014 but the wrapper change is itself CODEOWNERS-uncovered and services.yml is untouched \\u2014 passes through Gate 1 without any CODEOWNER approval.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4 Gate 1 wording (post fix-round-4 COMP-R2-MIN-002 closure): 'Approving review from a member listed in your repo's CODEOWNERS for the paths the bypass affects \\u2014 typically `services.yml`, `output/findings/waivers.json`, or `policies/**` if you mirror policies locally.'; verify-bypass-pairing.sh line 28\\u201331: `if ! grep -Fxq \\\\\\\"${waivers_path}\\\\\\\" <<<\\\\\\\"${changed_files}\\\\\\\"; then echo \\\\\\\"${waivers_path} must be part of the bypass diff\\\\\\\" >&2; exit 1; fi` \\u2014 machine-enforces waivers.json is always present in bypass PR diff; ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.1 'Multi-maintainer adopters MUST protect this file [the caller wrapper] with CODEOWNERS'; the two requirements (\\u00a712.7.1 wrapper + \\u00a712.7.4 bypass paths) are defence-in-depth layers that both need to hold.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An adopter configuring CODEOWNERS who reads \\u00a712.7.4's Gate 1 description and covers only `services.yml` \\u2014 reasoning it is the canonical SCP-related path \\u2014 while omitting `output/findings/waivers.json`, AND who also misses the \\u00a712.7.1 MUST requirement for the caller wrapper, has a Gate 1 configuration where no CODEOWNERS-covered file appears in a bypass PR. In that state `require_code_owner_reviews: true` is satisfied by any review (no CODEOWNERS file matches the PR diff), collapsing Gate 1 to a plain review-count check. The three-gate model's machine-enforcement claim then holds only if `required_approving_review_count >= 1` is set and the review count is satisfied \\u2014 without CODEOWNER specificity. The COMP-R2-MIN-002 fix introduced the 'typically' qualifier while adding the examples; 'typically' was not present in the pre-fix-round-4 text.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace 'typically `services.yml`, `output/findings/waivers.json`, or `policies/**` if you mirror policies locally' with language that makes `output/findings/waivers.json` the minimum-necessary path: 'at minimum `output/findings/waivers.json` (required: every bypass PR adds a waiver entry per Gate 3, so CODEOWNERS coverage on this path guarantees Gate 1 fires on every bypass PR); extend coverage to `services.yml`, `policies/**`, and any other SCP-governed paths in your repo.' This wording removes the ambiguity introduced by 'typically' and ties the minimum-necessary requirement directly to Gate 3's machine-enforced invariant.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R3-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.5 step 0 instructs operators to 'close any open Renovate-bot PR that is bumping the wrapper SHA pin (label: `scp-federation` per the preset's packageRules)'. However `scp-federation` is a ROOT-LEVEL label in renovate/default.json (`\\\\\\\"labels\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"scp-federation\\\\\\\"]` at the top-level config), meaning every Renovate PR generated by this preset \\u2014 not just SCP federation primitive bump PRs \\u2014 carries the `scp-federation` label. The label specific to SCP federation primitive bump PRs is `scp-bump`, which appears only in the packageRule block (`\\\\\\\"labels\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"scp-federation\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"scp-bump\\\\\\\"]`). An operator following step 0 verbatim ('close all scp-federation labeled PRs') during a rollback would close ALL Renovate PRs in the adopter repo, not just the SCP bump PR.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"renovate/default.json root-level: `\\\\\\\"labels\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"scp-federation\\\\\\\"]` \\u2014 applied to all PRs from this preset; renovate/default.json packageRule for jrnb2024/standards-control-plane: `\\\\\\\"labels\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"dependencies\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"scp-federation\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"scp-bump\\\\\\\"]` \\u2014 `scp-bump` is the discriminating label for SCP primitive bump PRs only; ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.5 step 0: 'Close any open Renovate-bot PR that is bumping the wrapper SHA pin (label: `scp-federation` per the preset's packageRules).'\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"During an SCP regression rollback, an operator following the documented step 0 closes every Renovate PR in their repo labeled `scp-federation`, which \\u2014 given the root-level label \\u2014 includes all Renovate-managed dependency updates (npm, pip, go.mod, etc.) that the adopter's Renovate instance generates while extending this preset. This (a) interrupts legitimate dependency updates with no relation to the SCP regression, (b) potentially delays security patches on unrelated dependencies whose Renovate PRs were just closed, and (c) could trigger Renovate rate-limiting on PR recreation. The root label was present at v1.0.0 and the discrepancy was not caught in R1 or R2.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace 'label: `scp-federation`' in \\u00a712.7.5 step 0 with 'label: `scp-bump`', which is the packageRule-specific label exclusive to SCP federation primitive bump PRs. The corrected step 0 becomes: 'Close any open Renovate-bot PR that is bumping the wrapper SHA pin (label: `scp-bump` per the preset's SCP-primitive packageRule; this is distinct from the root-level `scp-federation` label, which appears on all Renovate PRs from this preset).' This scopes the rollback closure to exactly the PR series that poses the risk without disturbing unrelated dependency updates.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R3-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The R2 SAFE-R2-001 MAJ finding's mitigation explicitly requested TWO changes: (1) text in \\u00a712.7.4 requiring dismiss_stale_reviews: true (implemented); and (2) a WARNING in enable-required-check.sh when the preserved existing review config has dismiss_stale_reviews=false or null (NOT implemented). The FIX-ROUND-3 record describes the closure as text-only: 'The enable-required-check.sh helper preserves existing review-shape verbatim and does not set this for adopters; the appendix instructs explicit configuration via the GitHub UI/API.' The helper script at line 311 silently preserves whatever review config it finds (`EXISTING_REVIEWS=\\\\\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$BEFORE_JSON\\\\\\\" | jq '.required_pull_request_reviews // null')\\\\\\\"`), without any check on the dismiss_stale_reviews field value.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"enable-required-check.sh line 311: `EXISTING_REVIEWS=\\\\\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$BEFORE_JSON\\\\\\\" | jq '.required_pull_request_reviews // null')\\\\\\\"` \\u2014 no subsequent check on `.dismiss_stale_reviews // false` value and no conditional WARNING emit; FIX-ROUND-3.md SAFE-R2-001 closure: 'The enable-required-check.sh helper preserves existing review-shape verbatim and does not set this for adopters; the appendix instructs explicit configuration via the GitHub UI/API' \\u2014 no mention of implementing the helper warning; review-safety-r2.json SAFE-R2-001 mitigation part (2): 'Add to enable-required-check.sh: when extracting $existing_reviews, check whether dismiss_stale_reviews is false (or null) and emit a WARNING if so, recommending the adopter set it to true before proceeding.'\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A multi-maintainer adopter who runs the helper on a repo with dismiss_stale_reviews=false (or on a fresh repo with null review config, which the helper preserves as null/no-reviews) receives no signal from the tool itself. The attacker scenario from SAFE-R2-001 partially remains: an adopter who configures branch protection via the helper and then OMITS the follow-up UI step to set dismiss_stale_reviews=true has Gate 1 partially open \\u2014 a contributor could obtain a CODEOWNER review on an initial commit then push bypass-surface content after approval without triggering re-review. The \\u00a712.7.4 text-only mitigation relies entirely on the adopter reading the appendix prior to running the helper; operators who run the helper first and read the docs later (a common pattern) receive no tool-level protection signal.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a dismiss_stale_reviews warning to enable-required-check.sh immediately after line 311 where EXISTING_REVIEWS is extracted: `dismiss_stale_val=\\\\\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$EXISTING_REVIEWS\\\\\\\" | jq -r '.dismiss_stale_reviews // false')\\\\\\\"; if [ \\\\\\\"$dismiss_stale_val\\\\\\\" != 'true' ]; then echo 'WARNING: your current required_pull_request_reviews does not have dismiss_stale_reviews: true. For multi-maintainer SCP adoption, dismiss_stale_reviews: true is required (see ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4). Configure it explicitly via the GitHub branch-protection UI or API after this script completes.' >&2; fi`. The text disclosure in \\u00a712.7.4 remains the normative requirement; the helper warning provides the defence-in-depth layer the R2 mitigation originally specified but which was not implemented in fix-round-3.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R3-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The 2026-05-14 closure deadline for TF-020H3-001 (Regal SHA256 supply-chain gap) is recorded in STATUS.md's 'Tracked-forward items from 020H part 3' section but is ABSENT from the 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table. The follow-ups table is the primary operator surface for time-sensitive action items \\u2014 all five current entries have explicit calendar dates and sources. TF-020H3-001's 2026-05-14 deadline is NOT in that table. An operator who scans the follow-ups table for near-term deadlines (a reasonable operational habit given the table's design) would not see the 2026-05-14 date, reducing the deadline to an item that relies on the operator's memory of the tracked-forward section rather than the time-sensitive prompt the table provides.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"STATUS.md 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table: five entries (D-021 on 2026-05-31, Phase 2 notice on 2026-07-02, bearer-legacy close on 2026-09-30, quarterly review on 2026-07-21, branch-protection review on 2026-07-30) \\u2014 no TF-020H3-001 entry; STATUS.md 'Tracked-forward items from 020H part 3': 'TF-020H3-001: ... Closure deadline: 2026-05-14 (14 days from 020H part 3 merge) OR before v1.0.1 release, whichever comes first. Escalate to user review if deferred beyond this window.' \\u2014 correct deadline present but not in follow-ups table; DISPATCH-NOTE.md TF-020H3-001: 'Closure deadline: before v1.0.1 release OR within 14 calendar days of 020H part 3 merge (whichever comes first); for a 2026-04-30 merge, that is 2026-05-14.' The R2 finding SAFE-R2-004 explicitly requested this concrete deadline, and the deadline WAS added \\u2014 but only to the tracked-forward section, not the follow-ups table.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a row to STATUS.md 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table: `| TF-020H3-001 Regal SHA256 gap \\u2192 slice 020H.2 (Regal SHA256 verification) | 2026-05-14 | docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/DISPATCH-NOTE.md TF-020H3-001 + ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.13 |`. This surfaces the deadline in the operator's primary time-sensitive tracking surface alongside the other dated items, removing the reliance on memory of the tracked-forward section. The 2026-05-14 deadline is the more urgent of the two conditions (14 days from merge vs before v1.0.1 release, whichever is first); the table entry should reflect the concrete date.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R3-005\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4's explanation of dismiss_stale_reviews says 'without it, an approving CODEOWNER review on an initial commit persists across subsequent commits in the same PR.' This is accurate but does not explicitly confirm that dismiss_stale_reviews fires on ALL pushes to the PR branch regardless of which paths are changed \\u2014 including pushes that touch only non-CODEOWNERS paths. An adopter might reasonably wonder whether a new commit that touches only a file not in CODEOWNERS (e.g., a source code file) still triggers stale-review dismissal, or whether the dismissal is path-selective. The current text implies path-agnostic dismissal but does not state it.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4: 'The dismiss_stale_reviews: true setting is non-optional in this mode: without it, an approving CODEOWNER review on an initial commit persists across subsequent commits in the same PR, so an attacker who obtains a clean review can later push commits that add .scp/rule-config.yaml disable: true, change scp_bypass: true, or weaken the wrapper's fork-PR if: \\u2014 and merge without re-review.'; GitHub branch-protection documentation: dismiss_stale_reviews fires when 'someone pushes a new commit' \\u2014 the dismissal is a per-PR-branch event, not a per-path event; it fires regardless of which files the new commit changes.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add one clause to \\u00a712.7.4's dismiss_stale_reviews description: 'Dismissal fires on ANY push to the PR branch regardless of which files the new commit touches \\u2014 it is not path-selective.' This preempts the reasonable adopter question and confirms the control is stronger than a path-scoped protection.\\\"}],\\\"summary\\\":\\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS \\u2014 fixpoint criterion met: zero new CRIT or MAJ findings across a comprehensive adversarial re-examination of all six dispatch question axes. All R1 MAJ closures (SAFE-003: two-mode single/multi breakdown; SAFE-009: rule-config bypass disclosure; SAFE-011: Regal ACE disclosure with TF-020H3-001) verified genuine at post-fix-round-4 state. All R2 closures verified genuine: SAFE-R2-001 (dismiss_stale_reviews: text requirement added, helper limitation disclosed); SAFE-R2-002 (\\u00a711.10 upgraded to normative MUST); SAFE-R2-003 (\\u00a712.7.1 + \\u00a712.7.9 forward-references to \\u00a712.7.13 present); SAFE-R2-004 (concrete 2026-05-14 deadline added to DISPATCH-NOTE and STATUS.md tracked-forward section); SAFE-R2-005 (D-031 item (c) has D-033 amendment annotation). The dismiss_stale_reviews closure (SAFE-R2-001) is robust for the 'review-then-push-malicious-commit' attack: GitHub dismisses ALL reviews on ANY push to the PR branch regardless of which paths are touched, so the path-agnostic property holds. The non-author platform enforcement claim is accurate for all merge types (squash, rebase, merge commit). The de-adoption Case A contexts:[] correctly removes the SCP-only required check; Case B stale-context risk is operational (over-blocking) not a privilege leak. Cross-document consistency (ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7 / DECISIONS.md / STATUS.md / docs/security/branch-protection.md) verified: all four agree on single-operator live state (count=0, codeowner=false, dismiss_stale=true, enforce_admins=true, policy-check/scp/policy-check context). Four MIN residuals surface: (1) SAFE-R3-001: \\u00a712.7.4 Gate 1 'typically' wording could lead an adopter to protect services.yml only, leaving waivers.json uncovered and Gate 1 potentially non-CODEOWNER-specific on bypass PRs; (2) SAFE-R3-002: \\u00a712.7.5 step 0 rollback label uses scp-federation (root-level \\u2014 all Renovate PRs) instead of scp-bump (SCP-primitive-specific) \\u2014 operational footgun during rollback; (3) SAFE-R3-003: enable-required-check.sh does not warn when preserved dismiss_stale_reviews is false/null \\u2014 R2 mitigation component explicitly requested but not implemented; (4) SAFE-R3-004: TF-020H3-001's 2026-05-14 deadline is absent from STATUS.md 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table, leaving it outside the primary operator time-sensitive tracking surface. One nit (SAFE-R3-005): dismiss_stale_reviews description doesn't confirm the dismissal is push-type-agnostic. No findings recommend descoping. All mitigations are additive text or script changes.\\\"}\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3.json:82:  \"stdout_tail\": \"sure in \\u00a712.7.4 remains the normative requirement; the helper warning provides the defence-in-depth layer the R2 mitigation originally specified but which was not implemented in fix-round-3.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"SAFE-R3-004\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"MIN\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The 2026-05-14 closure deadline for TF-020H3-001 (Regal SHA256 supply-chain gap) is recorded in STATUS.md's 'Tracked-forward items from 020H part 3' section but is ABSENT from the 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table. The follow-ups table is the primary operator surface for time-sensitive action items \\u2014 all five current entries have explicit calendar dates and sources. TF-020H3-001's 2026-05-14 deadline is NOT in that table. An operator who scans the follow-ups table for near-term deadlines (a reasonable operational habit given the table's design) would not see the 2026-05-14 date, reducing the deadline to an item that relies on the operator's memory of the tracked-forward section rather than the time-sensitive prompt the table provides.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"STATUS.md 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table: five entries (D-021 on 2026-05-31, Phase 2 notice on 2026-07-02, bearer-legacy close on 2026-09-30, quarterly review on 2026-07-21, branch-protection review on 2026-07-30) \\u2014 no TF-020H3-001 entry; STATUS.md 'Tracked-forward items from 020H part 3': 'TF-020H3-001: ... Closure deadline: 2026-05-14 (14 days from 020H part 3 merge) OR before v1.0.1 release, whichever comes first. Escalate to user review if deferred beyond this window.' \\u2014 correct deadline present but not in follow-ups table; DISPATCH-NOTE.md TF-020H3-001: 'Closure deadline: before v1.0.1 release OR within 14 calendar days of 020H part 3 merge (whichever comes first); for a 2026-04-30 merge, that is 2026-05-14.' The R2 finding SAFE-R2-004 explicitly requested this concrete deadline, and the deadline WAS added \\u2014 but only to the tracked-forward section, not the follow-ups table.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Add a row to STATUS.md 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table: `| TF-020H3-001 Regal SHA256 gap \\u2192 slice 020H.2 (Regal SHA256 verification) | 2026-05-14 | docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/DISPATCH-NOTE.md TF-020H3-001 + ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.13 |`. This surfaces the deadline in the operator's primary time-sensitive tracking surface alongside the other dated items, removing the reliance on memory of the tracked-forward section. The 2026-05-14 deadline is the more urgent of the two conditions (14 days from merge vs before v1.0.1 release, whichever is first); the table entry should reflect the concrete date.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"SAFE-R3-005\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"nit\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\u00a712.7.4's explanation of dismiss_stale_reviews says 'without it, an approving CODEOWNER review on an initial commit persists across subsequent commits in the same PR.' This is accurate but does not explicitly confirm that dismiss_stale_reviews fires on ALL pushes to the PR branch regardless of which paths are changed \\u2014 including pushes that touch only non-CODEOWNERS paths. An adopter might reasonably wonder whether a new commit that touches only a file not in CODEOWNERS (e.g., a source code file) still triggers stale-review dismissal, or whether the dismissal is path-selective. The current text implies path-agnostic dismissal but does not state it.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4: 'The dismiss_stale_reviews: true setting is non-optional in this mode: without it, an approving CODEOWNER review on an initial commit persists across subsequent commits in the same PR, so an attacker who obtains a clean review can later push commits that add .scp/rule-config.yaml disable: true, change scp_bypass: true, or weaken the wrapper's fork-PR if: \\u2014 and merge without re-review.'; GitHub branch-protection documentation: dismiss_stale_reviews fires when 'someone pushes a new commit' \\u2014 the dismissal is a per-PR-branch event, not a per-path event; it fires regardless of which files the new commit changes.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Add one clause to \\u00a712.7.4's dismiss_stale_reviews description: 'Dismissal fires on ANY push to the PR branch regardless of which files the new commit touches \\u2014 it is not path-selective.' This preempts the reasonable adopter question and confirms the control is stronger than a path-scoped protection.\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS \\u2014 fixpoint criterion met: zero new CRIT or MAJ findings across a comprehensive adversarial re-examination of all six dispatch question axes. All R1 MAJ closures (SAFE-003: two-mode single/multi breakdown; SAFE-009: rule-config bypass disclosure; SAFE-011: Regal ACE disclosure with TF-020H3-001) verified genuine at post-fix-round-4 state. All R2 closures verified genuine: SAFE-R2-001 (dismiss_stale_reviews: text requirement added, helper limitation disclosed); SAFE-R2-002 (\\u00a711.10 upgraded to normative MUST); SAFE-R2-003 (\\u00a712.7.1 + \\u00a712.7.9 forward-references to \\u00a712.7.13 present); SAFE-R2-004 (concrete 2026-05-14 deadline added to DISPATCH-NOTE and STATUS.md tracked-forward section); SAFE-R2-005 (D-031 item (c) has D-033 amendment annotation). The dismiss_stale_reviews closure (SAFE-R2-001) is robust for the 'review-then-push-malicious-commit' attack: GitHub dismisses ALL reviews on ANY push to the PR branch regardless of which paths are touched, so the path-agnostic property holds. The non-author platform enforcement claim is accurate for all merge types (squash, rebase, merge commit). The de-adoption Case A contexts:[] correctly removes the SCP-only required check; Case B stale-context risk is operational (over-blocking) not a privilege leak. Cross-document consistency (ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7 / DECISIONS.md / STATUS.md / docs/security/branch-protection.md) verified: all four agree on single-operator live state (count=0, codeowner=false, dismiss_stale=true, enforce_admins=true, policy-check/scp/policy-check context). Four MIN residuals surface: (1) SAFE-R3-001: \\u00a712.7.4 Gate 1 'typically' wording could lead an adopter to protect services.yml only, leaving waivers.json uncovered and Gate 1 potentially non-CODEOWNER-specific on bypass PRs; (2) SAFE-R3-002: \\u00a712.7.5 step 0 rollback label uses scp-federation (root-level \\u2014 all Renovate PRs) instead of scp-bump (SCP-primitive-specific) \\u2014 operational footgun during rollback; (3) SAFE-R3-003: enable-required-check.sh does not warn when preserved dismiss_stale_reviews is false/null \\u2014 R2 mitigation component explicitly requested but not implemented; (4) SAFE-R3-004: TF-020H3-001's 2026-05-14 deadline is absent from STATUS.md 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table, leaving it outside the primary operator time-sensitive tracking surface. One nit (SAFE-R3-005): dismiss_stale_reviews description doesn't confirm the dismissal is push-type-agnostic. No findings recommend descoping. All mitigations are additive text or script changes.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"stop_reason\\\":\\\"end_turn\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"cafdd769-80f6-4414-a3be-aa07a05a2641\\\",\\\"total_cost_usd\\\":1.03842655,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":9,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":121293,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":398256,\\\"output_tokens\\\":30806,\\\"server_tool_use\\\":{\\\"web_search_requests\\\":0,\\\"web_fetch_requests\\\":0},\\\"service_tier\\\":\\\"standard\\\",\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":121293,\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0},\\\"inference_geo\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"iterations\\\":[{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1,\\\"output_tokens\\\":4902,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":101682,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":27482,\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":27482},\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\"}],\\\"speed\\\":\\\"standard\\\"},\\\"modelUsage\\\":{\\\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":1859,\\\"outputTokens\\\":25,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.001984,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000},\\\"claude-sonnet-4-6\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":9,\\\"outputTokens\\\":30806,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":398256,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":121293,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":1.0364425499999999,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000}},\\\"permission_denials\\\":[],\\\"terminal_reason\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"fast_mode_state\\\":\\\"off\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"d20e2de6-778d-4ad7-933e-3978e510d549\\\"}\\n\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3.json:102:        \"claim\": \"The R2 SAFE-R2-001 MAJ finding's mitigation explicitly requested TWO changes: (1) text in \\u00a712.7.4 requiring dismiss_stale_reviews: true (implemented); and (2) a WARNING in enable-required-check.sh when the preserved existing review config has dismiss_stale_reviews=false or null (NOT implemented). The FIX-ROUND-3 record describes the closure as text-only: 'The enable-required-check.sh helper preserves existing review-shape verbatim and does not set this for adopters; the appendix instructs explicit configuration via the GitHub UI/API.' The helper script at line 311 silently preserves whatever review config it finds (`EXISTING_REVIEWS=\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\"$BEFORE_JSON\\\" | jq '.required_pull_request_reviews // null')\\\"`), without any check on the dismiss_stale_reviews field value.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3.json:103:        \"evidence\": \"enable-required-check.sh line 311: `EXISTING_REVIEWS=\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\"$BEFORE_JSON\\\" | jq '.required_pull_request_reviews // null')\\\"` \\u2014 no subsequent check on `.dismiss_stale_reviews // false` value and no conditional WARNING emit; FIX-ROUND-3.md SAFE-R2-001 closure: 'The enable-required-check.sh helper preserves existing review-shape verbatim and does not set this for adopters; the appendix instructs explicit configuration via the GitHub UI/API' \\u2014 no mention of implementing the helper warning; review-safety-r2.json SAFE-R2-001 mitigation part (2): 'Add to enable-required-check.sh: when extracting $existing_reviews, check whether dismiss_stale_reviews is false (or null) and emit a WARNING if so, recommending the adopter set it to true before proceeding.'\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3.json:106:        \"mitigation\": \"Add a dismiss_stale_reviews warning to enable-required-check.sh immediately after line 311 where EXISTING_REVIEWS is extracted: `dismiss_stale_val=\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\"$EXISTING_REVIEWS\\\" | jq -r '.dismiss_stale_reviews // false')\\\"; if [ \\\"$dismiss_stale_val\\\" != 'true' ]; then echo 'WARNING: your current required_pull_request_reviews does not have dismiss_stale_reviews: true. For multi-maintainer SCP adoption, dismiss_stale_reviews: true is required (see ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4). Configure it explicitly via the GitHub branch-protection UI or API after this script completes.' >&2; fi`. The text disclosure in \\u00a712.7.4 remains the normative requirement; the helper warning provides the defence-in-depth layer the R2 mitigation originally specified but which was not implemented in fix-round-3.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3.json:128:    \"summary\": \"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS \\u2014 fixpoint criterion met: zero new CRIT or MAJ findings across a comprehensive adversarial re-examination of all six dispatch question axes. All R1 MAJ closures (SAFE-003: two-mode single/multi breakdown; SAFE-009: rule-config bypass disclosure; SAFE-011: Regal ACE disclosure with TF-020H3-001) verified genuine at post-fix-round-4 state. All R2 closures verified genuine: SAFE-R2-001 (dismiss_stale_reviews: text requirement added, helper limitation disclosed); SAFE-R2-002 (\\u00a711.10 upgraded to normative MUST); SAFE-R2-003 (\\u00a712.7.1 + \\u00a712.7.9 forward-references to \\u00a712.7.13 present); SAFE-R2-004 (concrete 2026-05-14 deadline added to DISPATCH-NOTE and STATUS.md tracked-forward section); SAFE-R2-005 (D-031 item (c) has D-033 amendment annotation). The dismiss_stale_reviews closure (SAFE-R2-001) is robust for the 'review-then-push-malicious-commit' attack: GitHub dismisses ALL reviews on ANY push to the PR branch regardless of which paths are touched, so the path-agnostic property holds. The non-author platform enforcement claim is accurate for all merge types (squash, rebase, merge commit). The de-adoption Case A contexts:[] correctly removes the SCP-only required check; Case B stale-context risk is operational (over-blocking) not a privilege leak. Cross-document consistency (ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7 / DECISIONS.md / STATUS.md / docs/security/branch-protection.md) verified: all four agree on single-operator live state (count=0, codeowner=false, dismiss_stale=true, enforce_admins=true, policy-check/scp/policy-check context). Four MIN residuals surface: (1) SAFE-R3-001: \\u00a712.7.4 Gate 1 'typically' wording could lead an adopter to protect services.yml only, leaving waivers.json uncovered and Gate 1 potentially non-CODEOWNER-specific on bypass PRs; (2) SAFE-R3-002: \\u00a712.7.5 step 0 rollback label uses scp-federation (root-level \\u2014 all Renovate PRs) instead of scp-bump (SCP-primitive-specific) \\u2014 operational footgun during rollback; (3) SAFE-R3-003: enable-required-check.sh does not warn when preserved dismiss_stale_reviews is false/null \\u2014 R2 mitigation component explicitly requested but not implemented; (4) SAFE-R3-004: TF-020H3-001's 2026-05-14 deadline is absent from STATUS.md 'Open scheduled follow-ups' table, leaving it outside the primary operator time-sensitive tracking surface. One nit (SAFE-R3-005): dismiss_stale_reviews description doesn't confirm the dismissal is push-type-agnostic. No findings recommend descoping. All mitigations are additive text or script changes.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3.json:129:    \"target\": \"docs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md \\u00a712.7; .github/workflows/policy-check.yml; scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh; scripts/enable-required-check.sh; renovate/default.json; docs/DECISIONS.md; docs/security/branch-protection.md; STATUS.md\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"Adversarial SAFETY/BYPASS-lens review of WP-SCP-022 slice 020H part 3 (ADOPT-001 §12.7 federation-primitive adopter integration appendix). The slice is documentation-only but the document defines the canonical adopter contract — any safety gap an adopter could open by following this appendix verbatim is a finding. THINK ADVERSARIALLY: an attacker reading this appendix wants to find a way to onboard a malicious wrapper, suppress a deny, or escalate the federation-primitive's privilege. VERIFY EXACTLY: (1) §12.7.1 fork-PR refusal — TF-D1-002 closes by documenting it as MANDATORY. Confirm the wording is unambiguous (no 'optional', 'recommended', 'consider'); confirm the security rationale is named in-line (read-only fork GITHUB_TOKEN cannot post readback; workflow-context-injection surface). Could an adopter read this and conclude the `if:` is opt-out? (2) §12.7.10 'NEVER use `secrets: inherit`' — is the rationale (caller's GITHUB_TOKEN as privilege ceiling) sufficiently strong? Would an adopter who needs deploy-keys for some other workflow misread this and add `secrets:` selectively to the SCP wrapper? (3) §12.7.4 break-glass three-gate model: the three gates are CODEOWNERS approval + sibling D-NNN row + matching waivers.json entry. Could an attacker satisfy all three with a single PR that introduces a permanent backdoor? Specifically: (a) is there a temporal constraint preventing 'D-NNN row added 6 months ago, waiver added today' patterns? (b) does the matching-rule_id check enforce strict equality, or could a wildcard or substring match game it? (c) the 020D2 enforcement makes self-approval impossible on a single-operator account; does the appendix correctly describe the multi-maintainer-vs-single-operator branching for break-glass? (4) §12.7.5 rollback procedure — the 4-hour target. If a bad SCP release is published and the adopter reverts the wrapper `@SHA` pin, what protects against the race where a Renovate-bot-opened PR auto-updates the pin back to the bad SHA before the maintainer can pin to the previous good SHA? Is that race adequately addressed? (5) §12.7.2 — the 'extends:' shape pins to `#renovate/v1.0.0`. The renovate/v* tag series is now protected (per D-034) — is this protection mentioned anywhere in §12.7? Should it be? Is there a documented adopter expectation for verifying the preset's tag is signed/protected before extending? (6) §12.7.3 branch-protection setup — `enable-required-check.sh` requires `administration:write` PAT. Is the appendix clear about the privilege scope an adopter is granting to the operator running the helper? Could an adopter run this thinking it's a low-privilege call? (7) §12.7.9 pre-commit hook — running `scripts/scp-policy-check` from the local SCP clone gives that script execution privileges in the adopter's PR-author shell (not CI). What's the trust model for the SCP clone? If the adopter's clone is stale or tampered with, the local hook output could diverge from CI. Is this risk surfaced? (8) §12.7.11 freshness-warning — currently flagged as 020H.1. Is there a v1.0.0-period gap where adopters with stale pins have NO automated freshness signal? Is the Renovate-preset bump path adequate as the interim signal, or should the appendix recommend a manual quarterly review? (9) The error-code table §12.7.7 — does any code's failure-mode column ('Non-blocking' for SCP-E006) create a bypass surface? An attacker who can disable a rule via `.scp/rule-config.yaml` gets a `::error::` annotation but the merge proceeds — is this expected, or a CRIT? Is the rule-config schema's `expires_at` enforcement sufficient as the mitigation? (10) Privilege boundary: the appendix instructs adopters to add `statuses: write` to their wrapper (§12.7.1). What's the maximum damage an attacker who compromises the reusable workflow's runner could do with `statuses: write` on the caller's repo? Is this scoped correctly per D-029's analysis? Per WP-SCP-022 §4.3. Return SonnetReviewResult JSON. NEVER recommend descoping — propose how to fix.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-package.json:13:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r2.json:32:    \"result\": \"{\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"BLOCKED\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"SAFETY_BYPASS\\\",\\\"reviewer_id\\\":\\\"r2-safety-bypass-020h3\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-022/020h3\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"docs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md \\u00a712.7; .github/workflows/policy-check.yml; scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh; scripts/enable-required-check.sh; renovate/default.json; docs/DECISIONS.md D-031/D-033; docs/security/branch-protection.md\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R2-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4 describes multi-maintainer Gate 1 as 'machine-enforced' via `required_approving_review_count >= 1` + `require_code_owner_reviews: true` + `require_review_from_non_author: true`, but does NOT require `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`. Without stale-review dismissal, an attacker with PR write access can obtain a CODEOWNER review on an initial commit, then push subsequent commits containing bypass-surface changes (e.g., adding `.scp/rule-config.yaml` with `disable: true`, or adding `scp_bypass: true` to the caller wrapper) after the approval is recorded. GitHub's documented behaviour for `dismiss_stale_reviews: false` + `require_code_owner_reviews: true` when a new commit introduces a CODEOWNERS-protected path is ambiguous: the prior approval from a CODEOWNER for earlier files may satisfy the review count even though the reviewer never saw the malicious new file. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper compounds this: its `build_payload` function splices `$existing_reviews` from the adopter's current state into the PUT body, meaning adopters who have never explicitly configured `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` (the GitHub API default is false) will silently retain a vulnerable review shape after running the helper. The 'machine-enforced' claim in \\u00a712.7.4 is therefore conditional on adopters having separately and correctly configured stale-review dismissal \\u2014 a condition the guidance does not require, check for, or warn about.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4 multi-maintainer mode description: 'Gate 1 is machine-enforced \\u2014 a bypass PR cannot merge without a CODEOWNER review from someone other than the author'; enable-required-check.sh build_payload() function (line 269\\u2013291): `required_pull_request_reviews: $reviews` where `$reviews = $(printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$BEFORE_JSON\\\\\\\" | jq '.required_pull_request_reviews // null')` \\u2014 preserves existing review config verbatim without checking or setting dismiss_stale_reviews; docs/security/branch-protection.md SCP self-config: `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` IS set for SCP self \\u2014 but this is not surfaced as a requirement in \\u00a712.7.4's adopter guidance; GitHub branch protection docs on dismiss_stale_reviews: 'If enabled, approving reviews are dismissed when someone pushes a new commit' \\u2014 implied: if disabled, they are not.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An insider or compromised contributor account on a multi-maintainer adopter repo can: (1) open a PR with clean content that passes SCP policy check and gets a CODEOWNER approval; (2) push an additional commit adding `.scp/rule-config.yaml` with `disable: true` for one or more SCP rules after the review; (3) merge without a fresh CODEOWNER review on the new file, depending on GitHub's ambiguous behavior. The SCP-E006 observability annotation emits (non-blocking) but no CODEOWNER approval for the `.scp/rule-config.yaml` change is guaranteed. Gate 1 in multi-maintainer mode is supposed to be the machine-enforcement backstop for the rule-config bypass surface identified in SAFE-009 \\u2014 if Gate 1 can be circumvented by pushing after review, the SAFE-009 closure (CODEOWNERS-protect rule-config) provides weaker protection than \\u00a712.7.4 claims.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"(1) Add to \\u00a712.7.4 multi-maintainer mode description: 'ALSO configure `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` on the adopter branch protection. Without stale review dismissal, an approved PR that receives new commits \\u2014 including additions of `.scp/rule-config.yaml` or modifications to the caller wrapper \\u2014 may not require re-review from a CODEOWNER before merge. Stale-review dismissal is the enforcement complement to `require_code_owner_reviews`.' (2) Add to `enable-required-check.sh`: when extracting `$existing_reviews`, check whether `dismiss_stale_reviews` is false (or null) and emit a `WARNING` if so, recommending the adopter set it to true before proceeding. (3) Optionally, when `$existing_reviews` is null (no existing review config), construct a default that includes `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` rather than passing null.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R2-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4's SAFE-009 closure uses MUST-strength language ('Adopters MUST CODEOWNERS-protect `.scp/rule-config.yaml`') while \\u00a711.10, the declarative section that defines rule-config behavior, retains 'recommendation' language ('CODEOWNERS recommendation: protect `.scp/rule-config.yaml` so a single PR cannot silently expand the bypass surface'). An adopter who reads \\u00a711.10 in isolation \\u2014 which is the natural reading path for adopters configuring the rule-config feature, as \\u00a711.10 is the definitional home \\u2014 would treat CODEOWNERS protection as optional. The \\u00a712.7.4 back-reference ('Adopters MUST CODEOWNERS-protect `.scp/rule-config.yaml` (per \\u00a711.10)') cites \\u00a711.10 as the authority, but \\u00a711.10 itself uses weaker normative language, creating a self-referential inconsistency where the MUST points to a section that says 'recommendation'.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"\\u00a711.10 text: 'CODEOWNERS recommendation: protect `.scp/rule-config.yaml` so a single PR cannot silently expand the bypass surface. Minimum: `.scp/rule-config.yaml @your-team-owners`'; \\u00a712.7.4 final paragraph: 'Adopters MUST CODEOWNERS-protect `.scp/rule-config.yaml` (per \\u00a711.10) so a single developer cannot silently disable a rule.'\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Adopters who read \\u00a711.10 during initial rule-config setup and do not read the full \\u00a712.7 federation appendix will not apply CODEOWNERS protection, leaving the rule-config bypass surface (identified as equivalent to `scp_bypass: true` in \\u00a712.7.4) unguarded. Since \\u00a711.10 is structurally the definitional section for rule-config, it is a realistic first-stop read path. The inconsistency also undermines the normative authority of \\u00a712.7.4's MUST: citing \\u00a711.10 for the 'per \\u00a711.10' justification where \\u00a711.10 says 'recommendation' creates ambiguity about the actual normative force.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Upgrade \\u00a711.10's CODEOWNERS language from 'CODEOWNERS recommendation' to 'CODEOWNERS requirement (MUST)' and add a rationale sentence: 'CODEOWNERS protection on `.scp/rule-config.yaml` is required (MUST), not optional: `.scp/rule-config.yaml` suppresses rule denies without triggering the three-gate break-glass check (see \\u00a712.7.4), making it a bypass-surface equivalent to `scp_bypass: true`. A single developer can silently disable any SCP rule without CODEOWNERS coverage.'\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R2-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.13 (supply-chain posture, v1.0.0 disclosure) discloses the Regal SHA256 gap (TF-020H3-001) but receives no forward-reference from \\u00a712.7.1 (canonical caller wrapper), \\u00a712.7.3 (branch-protection setup), or \\u00a712.7.9 (pre-commit hook). An adopter following the natural setup sequence \\u2014 \\u00a712.7.1 \\u2192 \\u00a712.7.2 \\u2192 \\u00a712.7.3, or the pre-commit hook path at \\u00a712.7.9 \\u2014 would complete adoption without encountering the supply-chain risk disclosure at \\u00a712.7.13. The gap is that a MAJ-severity known gap (Regal binary executing with GITHUB_TOKEN, no SHA256 verification) is disclosed only in the last substantive sub-section of a 13-sub-section appendix, with no signpost from the earlier setup sections.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.1 (canonical wrapper): no mention of \\u00a712.7.13; \\u00a712.7.3 (branch-protection setup): no mention of \\u00a712.7.13; \\u00a712.7.9 (pre-commit hook): references SHA-locked OPA and Conftest binaries ('SHA256 verification is performed') but does not note that Regal in the CI workflow is NOT SHA256-verified, nor forward-reference \\u00a712.7.13; \\u00a712.7.13 text: 'Regal binary downloaded at hardcoded `0.40.0` without SHA256 verification... treat this as a known supply-chain gap until 020H.2 closes it.'\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An adopter who reads through \\u00a712.7.1 to \\u00a712.7.9 to complete setup and does not read \\u00a712.7.13 adopts the federation primitive without knowing that: (a) a binary downloading with GITHUB_TOKEN access is unverified; (b) a fork-and-add workaround exists; (c) the gap is tracked as TF-020H3-001 \\u2192 020H.2. The \\u00a712.7.9 pre-commit hook section specifically mentions SHA-locked binaries in a way that could create a false sense of complete supply-chain coverage, since it does not note that the CI workflow contains an unverified binary. Adopters cannot make an informed risk decision if they don't reach \\u00a712.7.13.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a supply-chain risk forward-reference in \\u00a712.7.1 (canonical wrapper), immediately after the YAML snippet: 'Note: before deploying, review \\u00a712.7.13 for the v1.0.0 supply-chain posture and the known Regal SHA256 gap (TF-020H3-001).' Similarly, add to \\u00a712.7.9 (pre-commit hook), immediately after the SHA-locked binary mention: 'Note: the CI workflow's Regal binary is not yet SHA256-verified (\\u00a712.7.13 discloses this gap; tracked as TF-020H3-001 \\u2192 020H.2).'\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R2-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE TF-020H3-001 records 'closure target = next session' as the deadline for closing the Regal SHA256 supply-chain gap. 'Next session' is not a calendar date, a release milestone, or a dependency condition \\u2014 it is an open-ended commitment that can drift indefinitely as backlog priorities shift. A MAJ-severity gap (unverified binary executing with GITHUB_TOKEN access on every PR in every adopter repo) should have a concrete closure deadline in both DISPATCH-NOTE and STATUS.md, not a session-relative pointer that loses meaning once the current implementation session ends and the note is read weeks later.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md TF-020H3-001 section: 'closure target = next session'; FIX-ROUND-1.md: 'SAFE-011 (MAJ) \\u2014 Reviewed mitigation: \\u00a712.7.13 added... Tracked as TF-020H3-001 \\u2192 slice 020H.2'; DISPATCH-NOTE.md post-merge STATUS.md commitment: 'mark 020H part 3 landed with PR # and commit SHA reference' and 'mark TF-D1-001..003 closed' \\u2014 but TF-020H3-001 is distinct and its STATUS.md entry resolution language is 'next session' not a date.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"If 020H.2 is not opened immediately after 020H part 3 merges \\u2014 due to competing priorities, session boundaries, or backlog pressure \\u2014 TF-020H3-001 persists in STATUS.md 'Post-Threshold-A backlog' without a forcing function. All adopters who have deployed the v1.0.0 federation primitive continue to run an unverified Regal binary with GITHUB_TOKEN access. The fork-and-add mitigation in \\u00a712.7.13 requires per-adopter manual action and breaks Renovate auto-bump, so most adopters will wait for the upstream fix \\u2014 which has no committed date.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace 'closure target = next session' in DISPATCH-NOTE TF-020H3-001 with a concrete deadline: 'closure target = before v1.0.1 release OR within 14 calendar days of 020H part 3 merge (whichever comes first); if deferred beyond this window, escalate to user review.' Update STATUS.md 'Post-Threshold-A backlog' entry for TF-020H3-001 to include the same deadline with an absolute date (e.g., 2026-05-14 if 020H part 3 merges 2026-04-30). This gives the next session a forcing function rather than an open-ended backlog item.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-R2-005\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"D-031 item (c) states `required_approving_review_count = 1` as the anticipated 020D2 configuration for the SCP self-dogfood gate. D-033 corrected D-032's count from 1 to 0 (single-operator self-approval is structurally impossible on GitHub; count=1 locks the sole operator out). D-032's decision text includes a D-033 forward-reference annotation noting this correction. D-031's item (c) lacks a corresponding forward-reference annotation, creating a cross-document state where D-031 and D-033 describe incompatible values (count=1 vs count=0) for the same live configuration parameter without a linking note in D-031.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"D-031 item (c): 'at slice 020D2, branch-protection on `main` is configured with `required_approving_review_count = 1`, `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, and `require_review_from_non_author = false`'; D-033: 'Updated to count=0 + codeowner_reviews=false in single-operator mode... GitHub forbids PR authors from approving their own PRs'; docs/security/branch-protection.md verify section: `review_count: 0` \\u2014 the live state confirms count=0; D-032 has an explicit '**Note (D-033 forward-reference, 2026-04-30 evening)**' annotation covering the same correction, absent from D-031.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An operator consulting D-031 (e.g., when preparing to onboard a second maintainer and needing to understand the current baseline before flipping to count=1 + codeowner=true) will read a stale count=1 and potentially misidentify the delta required. Cross-document inconsistency reduces trust in the decision log as a reliable single source of truth for the live branch-protection shape.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a D-033 forward-reference annotation to D-031 item (c): '**Note (D-033 amendment):** the `required_approving_review_count = 1` anticipated here was corrected to `count = 0` by D-033 (2026-04-30 evening) when the first PR through enforced mode revealed that GitHub forbids PR self-review; count=1 + sole CODEOWNER = operator locks the repo. See D-033 for the live state and the second-maintainer escalation path.'\\\"}],\\\"summary\\\":\\\"BLOCKED \\u2014 1 MAJ finding (SAFE-R2-001) requires resolution before this slice merges. SAFE-R2-001 (MAJ): \\u00a712.7.4's 'machine-enforced' Gate 1 claim for multi-maintainer mode is conditional on adopters having configured `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, which the guidance does not require and the `enable-required-check.sh` helper does not set or warn about. Without stale review dismissal, an attacker with PR write access can obtain a CODEOWNER approval on initial content then push bypass-surface changes (`.scp/rule-config.yaml` disable, caller-wrapper weakening) after the review, exploiting ambiguity in GitHub's CODEOWNERS re-evaluation behavior when `dismiss_stale_reviews: false`. This undermines the SAFE-009 closure's protection guarantee: CODEOWNERS-protecting `.scp/rule-config.yaml` is necessary but not sufficient if the review covering that file can be stale. All R1 SAFE-001..010 and SAFE-012 closures verified genuine at post-fix-round-1 state; SAFE-011 deferral is structurally adequate (\\u00a712.7.13 disclosure present) but TF-020H3-001's 'next session' deadline is insufficiently concrete (SAFE-R2-004 MIN). 4 additional MIN/nit findings: \\u00a711.10 normative inconsistency (SAFE-R2-002 MIN), \\u00a712.7.13 missing forward-reference from \\u00a712.7.1 (SAFE-R2-003 MIN), and D-031/D-033 cross-document staleness on count value (SAFE-R2-005 nit). No findings recommend descoping. All mitigations are additive text changes or helper-script warnings.\\\"}\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r2.json:83:  \"stdout_tail\": \"owing that: (a) a binary downloading with GITHUB_TOKEN access is unverified; (b) a fork-and-add workaround exists; (c) the gap is tracked as TF-020H3-001 \\u2192 020H.2. The \\u00a712.7.9 pre-commit hook section specifically mentions SHA-locked binaries in a way that could create a false sense of complete supply-chain coverage, since it does not note that the CI workflow contains an unverified binary. Adopters cannot make an informed risk decision if they don't reach \\u00a712.7.13.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Add a supply-chain risk forward-reference in \\u00a712.7.1 (canonical wrapper), immediately after the YAML snippet: 'Note: before deploying, review \\u00a712.7.13 for the v1.0.0 supply-chain posture and the known Regal SHA256 gap (TF-020H3-001).' Similarly, add to \\u00a712.7.9 (pre-commit hook), immediately after the SHA-locked binary mention: 'Note: the CI workflow's Regal binary is not yet SHA256-verified (\\u00a712.7.13 discloses this gap; tracked as TF-020H3-001 \\u2192 020H.2).'\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"SAFE-R2-004\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"MIN\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE TF-020H3-001 records 'closure target = next session' as the deadline for closing the Regal SHA256 supply-chain gap. 'Next session' is not a calendar date, a release milestone, or a dependency condition \\u2014 it is an open-ended commitment that can drift indefinitely as backlog priorities shift. A MAJ-severity gap (unverified binary executing with GITHUB_TOKEN access on every PR in every adopter repo) should have a concrete closure deadline in both DISPATCH-NOTE and STATUS.md, not a session-relative pointer that loses meaning once the current implementation session ends and the note is read weeks later.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md TF-020H3-001 section: 'closure target = next session'; FIX-ROUND-1.md: 'SAFE-011 (MAJ) \\u2014 Reviewed mitigation: \\u00a712.7.13 added... Tracked as TF-020H3-001 \\u2192 slice 020H.2'; DISPATCH-NOTE.md post-merge STATUS.md commitment: 'mark 020H part 3 landed with PR # and commit SHA reference' and 'mark TF-D1-001..003 closed' \\u2014 but TF-020H3-001 is distinct and its STATUS.md entry resolution language is 'next session' not a date.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"If 020H.2 is not opened immediately after 020H part 3 merges \\u2014 due to competing priorities, session boundaries, or backlog pressure \\u2014 TF-020H3-001 persists in STATUS.md 'Post-Threshold-A backlog' without a forcing function. All adopters who have deployed the v1.0.0 federation primitive continue to run an unverified Regal binary with GITHUB_TOKEN access. The fork-and-add mitigation in \\u00a712.7.13 requires per-adopter manual action and breaks Renovate auto-bump, so most adopters will wait for the upstream fix \\u2014 which has no committed date.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Replace 'closure target = next session' in DISPATCH-NOTE TF-020H3-001 with a concrete deadline: 'closure target = before v1.0.1 release OR within 14 calendar days of 020H part 3 merge (whichever comes first); if deferred beyond this window, escalate to user review.' Update STATUS.md 'Post-Threshold-A backlog' entry for TF-020H3-001 to include the same deadline with an absolute date (e.g., 2026-05-14 if 020H part 3 merges 2026-04-30). This gives the next session a forcing function rather than an open-ended backlog item.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"SAFE-R2-005\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"nit\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"D-031 item (c) states `required_approving_review_count = 1` as the anticipated 020D2 configuration for the SCP self-dogfood gate. D-033 corrected D-032's count from 1 to 0 (single-operator self-approval is structurally impossible on GitHub; count=1 locks the sole operator out). D-032's decision text includes a D-033 forward-reference annotation noting this correction. D-031's item (c) lacks a corresponding forward-reference annotation, creating a cross-document state where D-031 and D-033 describe incompatible values (count=1 vs count=0) for the same live configuration parameter without a linking note in D-031.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"D-031 item (c): 'at slice 020D2, branch-protection on `main` is configured with `required_approving_review_count = 1`, `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, and `require_review_from_non_author = false`'; D-033: 'Updated to count=0 + codeowner_reviews=false in single-operator mode... GitHub forbids PR authors from approving their own PRs'; docs/security/branch-protection.md verify section: `review_count: 0` \\u2014 the live state confirms count=0; D-032 has an explicit '**Note (D-033 forward-reference, 2026-04-30 evening)**' annotation covering the same correction, absent from D-031.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"An operator consulting D-031 (e.g., when preparing to onboard a second maintainer and needing to understand the current baseline before flipping to count=1 + codeowner=true) will read a stale count=1 and potentially misidentify the delta required. Cross-document inconsistency reduces trust in the decision log as a reliable single source of truth for the live branch-protection shape.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Add a D-033 forward-reference annotation to D-031 item (c): '**Note (D-033 amendment):** the `required_approving_review_count = 1` anticipated here was corrected to `count = 0` by D-033 (2026-04-30 evening) when the first PR through enforced mode revealed that GitHub forbids PR self-review; count=1 + sole CODEOWNER = operator locks the repo. See D-033 for the live state and the second-maintainer escalation path.'\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"BLOCKED \\u2014 1 MAJ finding (SAFE-R2-001) requires resolution before this slice merges. SAFE-R2-001 (MAJ): \\u00a712.7.4's 'machine-enforced' Gate 1 claim for multi-maintainer mode is conditional on adopters having configured `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, which the guidance does not require and the `enable-required-check.sh` helper does not set or warn about. Without stale review dismissal, an attacker with PR write access can obtain a CODEOWNER approval on initial content then push bypass-surface changes (`.scp/rule-config.yaml` disable, caller-wrapper weakening) after the review, exploiting ambiguity in GitHub's CODEOWNERS re-evaluation behavior when `dismiss_stale_reviews: false`. This undermines the SAFE-009 closure's protection guarantee: CODEOWNERS-protecting `.scp/rule-config.yaml` is necessary but not sufficient if the review covering that file can be stale. All R1 SAFE-001..010 and SAFE-012 closures verified genuine at post-fix-round-1 state; SAFE-011 deferral is structurally adequate (\\u00a712.7.13 disclosure present) but TF-020H3-001's 'next session' deadline is insufficiently concrete (SAFE-R2-004 MIN). 4 additional MIN/nit findings: \\u00a711.10 normative inconsistency (SAFE-R2-002 MIN), \\u00a712.7.13 missing forward-reference from \\u00a712.7.1 (SAFE-R2-003 MIN), and D-031/D-033 cross-document staleness on count value (SAFE-R2-005 nit). No findings recommend descoping. All mitigations are additive text changes or helper-script warnings.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"stop_reason\\\":\\\"end_turn\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"07fc223f-176c-4d7c-9301-8ab8603ddfc5\\\",\\\"total_cost_usd\\\":0.7381186499999999,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1528,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":82397,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":260553,\\\"output_tokens\\\":22956,\\\"server_tool_use\\\":{\\\"web_search_requests\\\":0,\\\"web_fetch_requests\\\":0},\\\"service_tier\\\":\\\"standard\\\",\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":82397,\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0},\\\"inference_geo\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"iterations\\\":[{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1,\\\"output_tokens\\\":22106,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":79998,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":10270,\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":10270},\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\"}],\\\"speed\\\":\\\"standard\\\"},\\\"modelUsage\\\":{\\\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":1890,\\\"outputTokens\\\":30,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.00204,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000},\\\"claude-sonnet-4-6\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":1528,\\\"outputTokens\\\":22956,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":260553,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":82397,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.7360786500000001,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000}},\\\"permission_denials\\\":[],\\\"terminal_reason\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"fast_mode_state\\\":\\\"off\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"eefef4e8-e420-4996-96d3-b54c1de82356\\\"}\\n\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r2.json:87:        \"claim\": \"\\u00a712.7.4 describes multi-maintainer Gate 1 as 'machine-enforced' via `required_approving_review_count >= 1` + `require_code_owner_reviews: true` + `require_review_from_non_author: true`, but does NOT require `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`. Without stale-review dismissal, an attacker with PR write access can obtain a CODEOWNER review on an initial commit, then push subsequent commits containing bypass-surface changes (e.g., adding `.scp/rule-config.yaml` with `disable: true`, or adding `scp_bypass: true` to the caller wrapper) after the approval is recorded. GitHub's documented behaviour for `dismiss_stale_reviews: false` + `require_code_owner_reviews: true` when a new commit introduces a CODEOWNERS-protected path is ambiguous: the prior approval from a CODEOWNER for earlier files may satisfy the review count even though the reviewer never saw the malicious new file. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper compounds this: its `build_payload` function splices `$existing_reviews` from the adopter's current state into the PUT body, meaning adopters who have never explicitly configured `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` (the GitHub API default is false) will silently retain a vulnerable review shape after running the helper. The 'machine-enforced' claim in \\u00a712.7.4 is therefore conditional on adopters having separately and correctly configured stale-review dismissal \\u2014 a condition the guidance does not require, check for, or warn about.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r2.json:88:        \"evidence\": \"\\u00a712.7.4 multi-maintainer mode description: 'Gate 1 is machine-enforced \\u2014 a bypass PR cannot merge without a CODEOWNER review from someone other than the author'; enable-required-check.sh build_payload() function (line 269\\u2013291): `required_pull_request_reviews: $reviews` where `$reviews = $(printf '%s' \\\"$BEFORE_JSON\\\" | jq '.required_pull_request_reviews // null')` \\u2014 preserves existing review config verbatim without checking or setting dismiss_stale_reviews; docs/security/branch-protection.md SCP self-config: `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` IS set for SCP self \\u2014 but this is not surfaced as a requirement in \\u00a712.7.4's adopter guidance; GitHub branch protection docs on dismiss_stale_reviews: 'If enabled, approving reviews are dismissed when someone pushes a new commit' \\u2014 implied: if disabled, they are not.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r2.json:91:        \"mitigation\": \"(1) Add to \\u00a712.7.4 multi-maintainer mode description: 'ALSO configure `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` on the adopter branch protection. Without stale review dismissal, an approved PR that receives new commits \\u2014 including additions of `.scp/rule-config.yaml` or modifications to the caller wrapper \\u2014 may not require re-review from a CODEOWNER before merge. Stale-review dismissal is the enforcement complement to `require_code_owner_reviews`.' (2) Add to `enable-required-check.sh`: when extracting `$existing_reviews`, check whether `dismiss_stale_reviews` is false (or null) and emit a `WARNING` if so, recommending the adopter set it to true before proceeding. (3) Optionally, when `$existing_reviews` is null (no existing review config), construct a default that includes `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` rather than passing null.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r2.json:131:    \"summary\": \"BLOCKED \\u2014 1 MAJ finding (SAFE-R2-001) requires resolution before this slice merges. SAFE-R2-001 (MAJ): \\u00a712.7.4's 'machine-enforced' Gate 1 claim for multi-maintainer mode is conditional on adopters having configured `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, which the guidance does not require and the `enable-required-check.sh` helper does not set or warn about. Without stale review dismissal, an attacker with PR write access can obtain a CODEOWNER approval on initial content then push bypass-surface changes (`.scp/rule-config.yaml` disable, caller-wrapper weakening) after the review, exploiting ambiguity in GitHub's CODEOWNERS re-evaluation behavior when `dismiss_stale_reviews: false`. This undermines the SAFE-009 closure's protection guarantee: CODEOWNERS-protecting `.scp/rule-config.yaml` is necessary but not sufficient if the review covering that file can be stale. All R1 SAFE-001..010 and SAFE-012 closures verified genuine at post-fix-round-1 state; SAFE-011 deferral is structurally adequate (\\u00a712.7.13 disclosure present) but TF-020H3-001's 'next session' deadline is insufficiently concrete (SAFE-R2-004 MIN). 4 additional MIN/nit findings: \\u00a711.10 normative inconsistency (SAFE-R2-002 MIN), \\u00a712.7.13 missing forward-reference from \\u00a712.7.1 (SAFE-R2-003 MIN), and D-031/D-033 cross-document staleness on count value (SAFE-R2-005 nit). No findings recommend descoping. All mitigations are additive text changes or helper-script warnings.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r2.json:132:    \"target\": \"docs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md \\u00a712.7; .github/workflows/policy-check.yml; scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh; scripts/enable-required-check.sh; renovate/default.json; docs/DECISIONS.md D-031/D-033; docs/security/branch-protection.md\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"R3 SAFETY/BYPASS-lens fixpoint review of WP-SCP-022 slice 020H part 3 at commit 270823b on feature/wp-scp-022-020h3-adopt-appendix. Prior rounds: R1 safety found 3 MAJ + 8 MIN + 1 nit (closed in fix-round-1 with TF-020H3-001 deferred-with-disclosure); R2 safety found 1 MAJ + 3 MIN + 1 nit (closed in fix-round-3). This R3 review confirms fixpoint — verifies that the closures introduce no new bypass surfaces and that no previously-overlooked safety issues remain. VERIFY adversarially: (1) The fix-round-3 SAFE-R2-001 closure: §12.7.4 multi-maintainer mode now requires dismiss_stale_reviews:true. Re-examine whether this combination (count>=1 + codeowner=true + dismiss_stale_reviews=true + platform-enforced non-author) actually closes the 'review-then-push-malicious-commit' attack. Edge cases: (a) what if the attacker's malicious commit doesn't TOUCH a CODEOWNERS-protected path? Does dismiss_stale_reviews fire only on CODEOWNERS-overlapping diffs or on every push? (b) the 'platform-enforced non-author' claim — verify this is actually enforced by GitHub for all PR types (squash-merge, rebase, etc.). (c) does the helper enable-required-check.sh's 'preserves existing review-shape verbatim' behaviour become a footgun if an adopter has count=0 set today, runs the helper, and assumes it's secure? (2) The fix-round-3 SAFE-R2-002 + R2 completeness COMP-R2-MIN-002 closures: §11.10 MUST + §12.7.4 Gate 1 reword. Does the new wording introduce any ambiguity about WHICH paths the CODEOWNERS coverage must include? An adopter could read 'paths the bypass affects' narrowly. (3) The fix-round-4 §12.7.5 two-case PATCH example: does Case A contexts:[] correctly exit a SCP-only configuration? Does Case B leak any privilege if the listed contexts are stale (e.g., a removed CI check still listed)? (4) The §12.7.13 supply-chain disclosure plus its forward-references from §12.7.1 + §12.7.9: does the disclosure now adequately cover the Regal ACE primitive AND set the 14-day deadline (2026-05-14) into a process that actually fires? Without an automated reminder, does this just rely on operator memory? (5) New attack surfaces from cumulative fix-rounds: (a) §12.7.5 step 0 'close any open scp-federation Renovate PR' — could an attacker mass-close legitimate Renovate PRs to silently rollback adopters? (b) §12.7.4 'SCP source — bus-factor-1 disclosure' — does naming @jrnb2024 publicly create a personal targeting risk that wasn't there before? (c) §12.7.3 'PAT scope is broader than it looks' — does the single-use revocation guidance contradict any default GitHub UI behaviour that auto-creates a long-lived PAT? (6) Cross-document fixpoint: do all four documents (ADOPT-001 §12.7 / DECISIONS.md / STATUS.md / docs/security/branch-protection.md) now describe a single, internally-consistent break-glass + branch-protection model? Any divergence is a finding. (7) Re-read every R1 + R2 safety finding (review-safety.json + review-safety-r2.json) and verify each closure is genuine and not just a textual rewording. Per WP-SCP-022 §4.3. Return SonnetReviewResult JSON. NEVER recommend descoping. Fixpoint criterion: zero new CRIT or MAJ findings.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r3-package.json:18:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r4-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"R4 SAFETY-lens fixpoint sanity check on WP-SCP-022 slice 020H part 3 at commit 72354e5 on feature/wp-scp-022-020h3-adopt-appendix. R3 demonstrated fixpoint (0 CRIT + 0 MAJ across all three lenses). Fix-round-5 closed all 6 MIN + 4 nit R3 findings, including: (a) §12.7.4 Gate 1 reword naming output/findings/waivers.json as MINIMUM-NECESSARY CODEOWNERS path; (b) §12.7.5 step 0 label corrected from scp-federation (root-level) to scp-bump (packageRule-specific); (c) scripts/enable-required-check.sh extended with a dismiss_stale_reviews stderr WARNING block immediately post EXISTING_REVIEWS extraction. This R4 review is narrowly-scoped: confirm fix-round-5 introduces no new BLOCKING (CRIT/MAJ) safety findings. VERIFY: (1) The §12.7.4 Gate 1 reword: does naming waivers.json as the minimum-necessary path actually close the bypass-via-CODEOWNERS-uncovered-paths attack identified in SAFE-R3-001? Verify against scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh — Gate 3 machine-enforces a waivers.json change in every bypass PR (lines 28-31). Therefore CODEOWNERS coverage on waivers.json guarantees Gate 1 fires on every bypass PR, regardless of which OTHER paths the bypass PR touches. Is this argument sound? Are there bypass-path topologies where waivers.json coverage is NOT sufficient? (2) The §12.7.5 step 0 label correction: does naming scp-bump correctly scope rollback-time PR closures to ONLY SCP-primitive bumps? Cross-check against renovate/default.json packageRules — is scp-bump exclusively applied to SCP-federation-primitive PRs, or does it also tag other PR types? (3) The scripts/enable-required-check.sh dismiss_stale_reviews WARNING: read the new block (line 313-329 area, after EXISTING_REVIEWS extraction). Verify: (a) bash syntax is correct; (b) the jq filter handles all three states (null / true / false / absent) without spurious warnings; (c) the WARNING fires BEFORE any mutation of the target repo's state (script must continue to work as a --plan-first tool); (d) the WARNING text correctly identifies single-operator-mode (count=0 per D-033) as legitimately-ignorable; (e) the gh api PATCH command in the WARNING is syntactically correct. Any of these going wrong would make the helper less safe than before fix-round-5. (4) §12.7.3 multi-maintainer paragraph cross-reference to §12.7.4 dismiss_stale_reviews — is the wording strong enough that an adopter following §12.7.3 alone will configure correctly? (5) §12.7.7 SCP-E007 row addition: verify the failure-mode column ('Fail-closed') matches the actual emission semantics in policy-check.yml line 528. (6) Cross-document consistency post fix-round-5: do ADOPT-001 §12.7.4 + scripts/enable-required-check.sh + docs/security/branch-protection.md all agree on the multi-maintainer mode requirements (count >= 1 + codeowner=true + dismiss_stale_reviews=true)? (7) Look adversarially for ANY new attack surface introduced by the cumulative 5 fix-rounds — has the recursive editing introduced an inconsistency, ambiguity, or capability gap that a single fresh-read reviewer (you) catches that no prior round caught? Per WP-SCP-022 §4.3. Return SonnetReviewResult JSON. Fixpoint criterion: zero new CRIT or MAJ findings. NEVER recommend descoping.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety-r4-package.json:13:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-completeness-r1retry.json:32:    \"result\": \"Good \\u2014 `verify-bypass-pairing.sh` exists in `scripts/` (glob confirms it; the grep searched file contents, not names). Now I have all I need. Let me produce the review.\\n\\n{\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"CONDITIONAL_PASS\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"completeness_governance\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-022 020H part 3\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"All eleven plan sub-criteria (i)..(xi) plus the minimal caller wrapper are present in \\u00a712.7.1\\u2013\\u00a712.7.12 with content that matches the plan's specification. The three TF-D1-NNN closure claims are genuine: trailing-dash appears in \\u00a712.7.1 and \\u00a712.7.2 (TF-D1-001), the fork-PR `if:` is labelled mandatory with security rationale (TF-D1-002), and \\u00a712.7 now exists (TF-D1-003). All eight decision-row citations (D-022, D-029, D-030, D-031, D-032, D-033, D-034, D-035) resolve in DECISIONS.md and their characterisations in the \\u00a712.7 reference section match the decision content. Naming is consistent ('020H part 3' throughout; no '020H.3' / 'pt3' variants). Sub-section numbering is sequential \\u00a712.7.1\\u2013\\u00a712.7.12 without gaps, and the DISPATCH-NOTE checklist mirrors the mapping explicitly. Two forward-looking flags (rule-regression issue template and freshness-warning) correctly name 020H.1 which appears in STATUS.md's post-Threshold-A backlog. Seven MAJ findings are raised: the most urgent are (1) \\u00a711.10 now contradicts \\u00a712.7.4 by describing break-glass as a two-gate process, and (2) \\u00a711.10's cross-reference '\\u00a711.x break-glass procedure' is unresolvable \\u2014 both require a patch to \\u00a711.10 in this PR before merge to avoid shipping an internally inconsistent ADOPT-001. The remaining five MAJs concern audit-trail gaps and adopter-onboarding completeness that can be addressed in a fix round within this dispatch. No CRIT findings. No silent descope detected.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MAJ-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a711.10 describes break-glass as a two-gate process ('a paired DECISIONS row + waiver') while \\u00a712.7.4 defines a three-gate model (CODEOWNERS approval + D-NNN row + waivers.json entry). \\u00a711.10 was not updated when \\u00a712.7.4 was authored. After this PR merges, ADOPT-001 contains two inconsistent break-glass descriptions in the same document.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a711.10, 'When not to use rule-config', final bullet: 'One-off PR exceptions \\u2014 use scp_bypass: true on the workflow invocation with a paired DECISIONS row + waiver per the \\u00a711.x break-glass procedure.' This enumerates exactly two gates. ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4 'Break-glass procedure (three-gate model)': 'the PR must satisfy ALL THREE gates simultaneously: (1) Approving review from a member of SCP-CODEOWNERS \\u2026 (2) Sibling D-NNN row \\u2026 (3) Matching waivers.json entry \\u2026 Any miss \\u2192 SCP-E004 \\u2192 merge-blocked.' The CODEOWNERS approval gate (gate 1) is absent from \\u00a711.10.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An adopter reading \\u00a711.10 (the rule-config section they are most likely to encounter first) gets an incomplete picture of break-glass requirements. They prepare a D-NNN row and waivers.json entry, invoke scp_bypass: true, and receive SCP-E004 from the CODEOWNERS gate that \\u00a711.10 never mentioned. This is a documentation defect in a security-enforcement procedure; the cost is a blocked PR that the adopter cannot self-diagnose from the docs they read.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"In this PR, amend \\u00a711.10's break-glass pointer to read: 'One-off PR exceptions \\u2014 use scp_bypass: true on the workflow invocation with a paired DECISIONS row + waiver per the three-gate break-glass procedure in \\u00a712.7.4.' Replace 'paired DECISIONS row + waiver' with the three-gate summary or a direct cross-reference. This is a one-line edit in the same file and should not require a separate PR.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MAJ-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a711.10 contains the cross-reference '\\u00a711.x break-glass procedure' which does not resolve to any section in ADOPT-001. The intended target is now \\u00a712.7.4 (published by this slice), but \\u00a711.10 was not updated. Every adopter following \\u00a711.10's pointer will encounter a dangling reference.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a711.10, 'When not to use rule-config': '\\u2026 per the \\u00a711.x break-glass procedure.' ADOPT-001 contains sections \\u00a711.1 through \\u00a711.10 and \\u00a712.1 through \\u00a712.7. There is no \\u00a711.x sub-section; the label is a placeholder that was not resolved when \\u00a712.7 was authored. The correct target section is \\u00a712.7.4 'Break-glass procedure (three-gate model)'.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 is the canonical onboarding doc for WP-SCP-024 estate cascade. A dangling \\u00a711.x reference in the production version of this doc will confuse every adopter who attempts to follow the break-glass path from \\u00a711.10. The reference is in a 'when not to use' guidance block \\u2014 adopters who comply and need an exception will hit the dangling pointer at exactly the moment they need the procedure.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"In this PR, replace '\\u00a711.x break-glass procedure' in \\u00a711.10 with '\\u00a712.7.4 break-glass procedure (three-gate model)'. This edit fixes both COMP-MAJ-001 and COMP-MAJ-002 simultaneously if combined into a single rewording of the \\u00a711.10 bullet.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MAJ-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The forward-looking SECURITY.md item (\\u00a712.7.8) names 'WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74.1 follow-up SCP-073.sec' as the delivery vehicle, but SCP-073.sec does not appear in STATUS.md's post-Threshold-A backlog or the open scheduled follow-ups table. The audit trail for this deferred item is broken.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.8: 'Adopters concerned about a policy-bypass disclosure should follow the SCP repo's SECURITY.md policy \\u2026 (when published \\u2014 WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74.1 follow-up SCP-073.sec).' DISPATCH-NOTE 'Forward-looking flags': 'SECURITY.md \\u2014 \\u00a712.7.8; lands in WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74.1 follow-up SCP-073.sec.' STATUS.md post-Threshold-A backlog lists: 020G (struck through as presumably landed before 020H part 3), 020H part 3, 020H.1, WP-SCP-022 proposal-queue, WP-SCP-023, WP-SCP-024. SCP-073.sec is not listed. STATUS.md open scheduled follow-ups table also contains no SCP-073.sec entry. WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74.1 deferred table lists 'Follow-up SCP-073.sec | SECURITY.md embargo path' but this is the plan doc, not the live STATUS.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Unlike 020H.1 and rule-regression (both listed in STATUS.md's backlog), SCP-073.sec has no live tracking entry. If this PR merges without adding SCP-073.sec to STATUS.md, the SECURITY.md publication has no durable reminder and may never land. Adopters reading \\u00a712.7.8 are directed to a URL for a document that has no committed delivery path in the live project tracking.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add 'SCP-073.sec: SECURITY.md publication (security disclosure path for policy-bypass reports)' to STATUS.md's post-Threshold-A backlog in this PR. Alternatively, add it to the open scheduled follow-ups table with a target date tied to 020H.1 (since it is a parallel track). Either approach creates a durable trail that the post-merge STATUS.md commitment (which the DISPATCH-NOTE defers to the next slice's PR) cannot omit.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MAJ-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7 provides no 'verify the integration is working' acceptance step. An adopter who completes \\u00a712.7.1 through \\u00a712.7.3 has no documented way to confirm the gate is live before relying on it for production PRs.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.1 documents the wrapper YAML. \\u00a712.7.2 documents the Renovate extends entry. \\u00a712.7.3 documents the enable-required-check.sh helper and mentions '--plan first to see both the current state and the proposed payload.' None of these sub-sections includes an explicit 'open a draft PR with a deliberate violation and confirm the gate blocks merge' or 'run gh api to verify the required-check context is registered' step. The adoption acceptance checklist in \\u00a715 covers the overall SCP onboarding but does not include a federation-primitive-specific verification step (e.g., verify the policy-check / scp/policy-check context appears in branch protection after running the helper).\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Adopters may complete all setup steps and assume they are protected when the required-check context name does not match (a real failure mode documented in D-033: the bare scp/policy-check context does not match the rendered Actions check-run name policy-check / scp/policy-check). Without a verification step, this misconfiguration is invisible until a real violation slips through. The D-033 episode on SCP self demonstrates that the naming issue is non-obvious and requires empirical confirmation.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a \\u00a712.7.3.x (or paragraph within \\u00a712.7.3) titled 'Verify the gate is live' with: (a) the gh api command from docs/security/branch-protection.md \\u00a7How to verify adapted for the adopter's repo (substitute the adopter's rendered context name); (b) instruction to open a test PR with a deliberate SCP-R-001 violation (a services.yml with an invalid auth mode) and confirm the gate blocks merge before relying on it in production. Cross-reference D-033 as the rationale for empirical verification.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MAJ-005\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.5 documents only per-release SHA-pin rollback (revert wrapper @SHA + re-run Renovate). There is no documented path for removing the entire federation-primitive integration. An adopter who decides to de-adopt SCP mid-stream has no documented removal procedure.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.5 'Rollback procedure': '1. Revert the wrapper's @SHA pin to the previous SCP release SHA via PR. 2. Update the # tag: comment to match. 3. Renovate will re-run the bump \\u2026 4. Open an issue using the rule-regression template.' All four steps assume the adopter wants to stay on an older SCP release, not exit the integration entirely. There is no instruction covering: remove .github/workflows/policy-check.yml, remove the extends entry from renovate.json, and run enable-required-check.sh with a removal flag (or manually remove the required-check via gh api) to un-gate the branch protection.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"The plan's \\u00a78 risk row 'Rego false-positive cascade' and \\u00a78's 'SCP-outage blast radius' both assume adopters can revert to a safe state quickly (target 4h, \\u00a712.7.5 iv-e). A false-positive cascade affecting multiple rules simultaneously may make per-release rollback insufficient if the regression was in the workflow infrastructure itself. Without a full-removal path, an adopter in that scenario has no documented escape route. For WP-SCP-024 estate cascade adopters, this gap is systemic across all six target repos.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add \\u00a712.7.5 step 5 (or a separate sub-section '\\u00a712.7.5.1 Full de-adoption'): (a) delete .github/workflows/policy-check.yml via PR; (b) remove the extends entry from renovate.json via the same PR; (c) after merge, run: gh api -X DELETE repos/OWNER/NAME/branches/BRANCH/protection/required_status_checks/contexts -F contexts[]='{rendered-context-name}' or use the GitHub UI to remove the required check; (d) document the de-adoption in a D-NNN row in the repo's DECISIONS equivalent. This documents the escape route without weakening the normal rollback path.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MAJ-006\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4 discloses the single-operator constraint only for adopter-side governance ('Single-operator adopters: \\u2026 self-approval is structurally impossible on GitHub; document this in your repo's analogue of WP-SCP-020 \\u00a78') but does not disclose that SCP's own source \\u2014 the reusable workflow and Renovate preset the adopter is extending \\u2014 is itself operated under an identical single-operator constraint per D-031. An adopter cannot make an informed adoption-risk decision without knowing the supply-chain source is single-operator-gated.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4 break-glass text: 'Single-operator adopters: per WP-SCP-020 \\u00a78 + D-031 (single-operator personal-account closure), self-approval is structurally impossible on GitHub; document this in your repo's analogue of WP-SCP-020 \\u00a78 + 2026-07-21 quarterly review.' This advises adopters about their own single-operator situation. D-031 in DECISIONS.md: 'Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020K on the SCP repository in the personal-account / single-operator mode \\u2026 (a) NO scp-break-glass GitHub team is created \\u2026 CODEOWNERS names @jrnb2024 only.' The SCP-side single-operator constraint is not surfaced to adopters reading \\u00a712.7. The 2026-07-21 quarterly review is mentioned as a reference only for the adopter's own governance, not as SCP's own re-evaluation checkpoint.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An adopter with governance requirements around supply-chain single-operator risk (e.g. a regulated-sector adopter or one with multiple maintainers who want two-person integrity on the gate source) cannot evaluate whether to adopt SCP's federation primitive without knowing the source is @jrnb2024-only. The WP-SCP-024 estate cascade will drive this appendix to six adopters; none of them will have this information unless they independently read D-031.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a 'SCP source \\u2014 bus-factor-1 disclosure' paragraph to \\u00a712.7.4 (or \\u00a712.7, reference section): 'As of v1.0.0, the SCP reusable workflow and Renovate preset (the source this integration depends on) are operated by a single maintainer (@jrnb2024) per D-031 (WP-SCP-020 \\u00a78 bus-factor-1 risk row). The quarterly bus-factor-1 review is scheduled for 2026-07-21. Adopters with multi-maintainer or regulatory requirements for supply-chain diversity should note this constraint and re-evaluate at that checkpoint.' This is a factual disclosure, not a descope.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MAJ-007\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The DISPATCH-NOTE post-merge commitment to 'mark TF-D1-001..003 closed in the tracked-forward section' of STATUS.md is unachievable as stated: STATUS.md's tracked-forward section lists only TF-005..008 (from 020C.1). TF-D1-001..003 do not appear in that section. The commitment requires an implicit add-then-close in a future PR with no current trail in STATUS.md.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE \\u00a7'Post-merge STATUS.md update commitment': 'The Tracked-forward items section: mark TF-D1-001..003 closed (or add a \\\\\\\"Closed in 020H part 3\\\\\\\" annotation row).' STATUS.md \\u00a7'Tracked-forward items from 020C.1': lists TF-005, TF-006, TF-007, TF-008 only. TF-D1-001..003 are not listed anywhere in STATUS.md's tracked-forward section; they appear only in docs/releases/v1.0.0-rc.1.md \\u00a7'Known limitations / tracked-forward items'. The DISPATCH-NOTE itself defers both STATUS.md edits to 'the next slice's PR (likely 020H.1)' \\u2014 meaning 020H part 3 merges without the updates, and if 020H.1 is delayed or scoped differently, the closure evidence evaporates.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Post-merge auditability of TF-D1-001..003 closure depends on a future PR (020H.1) performing a non-obvious compound operation (add entries AND mark closed) on STATUS.md's tracked-forward section. If 020H.1 is dispatched without explicitly including this task, or if a future reviewer reads STATUS.md to confirm TF-D1-002 (fork-PR refusal as mandatory) is closed, they find no entry and cannot resolve the claim. The DISPATCH-NOTE's statement 'Bundle both edits with the next slice's PR to keep the chain auditable' introduces a dependency chain gap rather than eliminating one.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"In this PR, add TF-D1-001, TF-D1-002, TF-D1-003 to STATUS.md's tracked-forward section with status 'Closed in 020H part 3 (PR #NNN)'. If the PR number is not yet known at authorship time, add them as 'Closed \\u2014 see docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/DISPATCH-NOTE.md'. This makes the closure self-evidencing at merge time and removes the dependency on 020H.1 for historical accuracy. The DISPATCH-NOTE's bundling guidance for STATUS.md is acceptable for the backlog-table row (marking 020H part 3 landed), but the tracked-forward closure should be in this PR's diff.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MIN-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7 presents sub-sections in logical order but provides no explicit step-sequenced order-of-operations for a new adopter. A first-time integrator must infer the sequence (add wrapper \\u2192 add Renovate extends \\u2192 run enable-required-check.sh \\u2192 verify gate \\u2192 understand break-glass) from sub-section ordering alone.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.1 (wrapper), \\u00a712.7.2 (Renovate), \\u00a712.7.3 (branch-protection helper) are in correct order but carry no 'Step 1 / Step 2 / Step 3' framing. \\u00a712.7.4 (break-glass) and \\u00a712.7.5 (rollback) are reference procedures, not setup steps, but this distinction is not stated. WP-SCP-024 estate cascade will use this appendix to onboard FLA, PIM, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, and control-tower; operator familiarity with GitHub Actions onboarding varies across those teams.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A new adopter who reads \\u00a712.7 in sequence without framing may attempt to run enable-required-check.sh before adding the wrapper (if \\u00a712.7.3 feels like step 1 independently), or may skip the Renovate preset assuming it is optional. The order matters because the required-check must reference a check that has already been observed (GitHub only accepts a context name that exists in the repo's check history). Wrong order \\u2192 required-check points to a non-existent context \\u2192 permanent 'Expected \\u2014 Waiting'.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a brief 'Setup sequence' paragraph at the top of \\u00a712.7 (before \\u00a712.7.1): 'Perform these steps in order: (1) Add the caller wrapper (\\u00a712.7.1) and merge at least one PR so GitHub registers the check-run context. (2) Add the Renovate preset (\\u00a712.7.2). (3) Run the branch-protection helper (\\u00a712.7.3) \\u2014 only after at least one successful check-run has been observed. (4) Confirm the gate is live (verify step \\u2014 see \\u00a712.7.3). \\u00a712.7.4\\u2013\\u00a712.7.12 are reference sections consulted as needed.' Four sentences; no descoping.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MIN-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The label 'SCP-CODEOWNERS' in \\u00a712.7.4 gate 1 reads as a reference to SCP's own CODEOWNERS file (a specific GitHub file in the jrnb2024/standards-control-plane repo) but the parenthetical clarifies it means 'your repo's CODEOWNERS for paths matching the gate's domain'. This terminology collision will cause confusion for WP-SCP-024 estate adopters unfamiliar with SCP's internal naming.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4, gate 1: 'Approving review from a member of SCP-CODEOWNERS (your repo's CODEOWNERS for paths matching the gate's domain \\u2014 typically services.yml, output/findings/waivers.json, or policies/** if you mirror locally).' The backtick formatting of SCP-CODEOWNERS makes it look like a literal file or group name. An adopter naturally reads 'SCP-CODEOWNERS' as referring to the SCP project's own CODEOWNERS set (i.e., @jrnb2024 must review every break-glass bypass in every adopter repo), which is wrong \\u2014 it is the adopter's own CODEOWNERS configuration that matters.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An adopter who misreads gate 1 as requiring @jrnb2024's review will either (a) conclude the procedure is unworkable (an external party must approve every adopter's bypass) or (b) attempt to contact @jrnb2024 for break-glass approval on their own repo's PRs. Either outcome blocks the adopter's governance process unnecessarily.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Rewrite gate 1 label: 'Approving review from a member listed in your repo's CODEOWNERS for the paths the bypass affects (typically services.yml, output/findings/waivers.json, or your local policy mirror if you maintain one; configure these in your CODEOWNERS analogously to how SCP's break-glass governance is configured per D-031).' Dropping the backtick-wrapped SCP-CODEOWNERS label removes the collision.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-MIN-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The plan \\u00a74 020H part 3 canonical wrapper spec text still shows 'jrnb2024/standards-control-plane' (no trailing dash) in both the uses: line and the Renovate marker comment. TF-D1-001 was correctly resolved in ADOPT-001's \\u00a712.7.1, but the plan spec text is now a permanently misleading authoritative reference. Future authors reading WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 to produce downstream adopter docs (WP-SCP-024 scaffolder, etc.) may propagate the typo.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020H part 3 canonical YAML block: '# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane' and 'uses: jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/.github/workflows/policy-check.yml@\\u2026'. ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.1 correctly shows 'depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane' and 'uses: jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/.github/workflows/\\u2026'. The DISPATCH-NOTE acknowledges TF-D1-001 as closed in ADOPT-001 but does not note that the plan spec text remains wrong. docs/releases/v1.0.0-rc.1.md and the live policy-check-wrapper.yml both correctly use the trailing dash.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-020 is the governing specification document for all federation-primitive work. A scaffolding author who reads \\u00a74 020H part 3's canonical YAML as the template for scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh (per SCP-073-scaffolder) will produce wrappers pointing at the wrong repo name \\u2014 a silent failure because jrnb2024/standards-control-plane (without dash) does not exist and GitHub will return a 404, fail-closing the entire gate infrastructure in adopter repos.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"In the next plan amendment opportunity (020H.1 dispatch or a governance-only PR), update WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020H part 3's canonical YAML block to add the trailing dash to both the depName and the uses: path. Add a note: 'Corrected from plan-v0.6 per TF-D1-001 closure in 020H part 3.' This is a spec-doc amendment, not a code change, and can be bundled with the next planned plan update.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-NIT-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4 break-glass gate 1 parenthetical lists SCP-internal paths (services.yml, output/findings/waivers.json, policies/**) as examples of what CODEOWNERS should cover. Estate adopters (FLA, PIM, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower) have different sensitive artifact paths; the SCP-internal examples will lead adopters to configure CODEOWNERS coverage for non-existent paths.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4 gate 1: '\\u2026 typically services.yml, output/findings/waivers.json, or policies/** if you mirror locally.' services.yml and output/findings/waivers.json are SCP-specific artifact paths. FLA and PIM may have different findings/waivers storage locations; mapp-doc-agent and control-tower have no SCP output/ tree at all unless they adopt one. The 'if you mirror locally' qualifier for policies/** is the only generic-qualified path.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Low \\u2014 the parenthetical is illustrative, not prescriptive, and the 'typically' qualifier partially mitigates the specificity. However, WP-SCP-024 estate cascade operators applying this appendix verbatim will add CODEOWNERS rules that cover paths that do not exist in their repo, producing vacuous CODEOWNERS rules and a false sense of governance coverage.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Generalise the parenthetical to: '\\u2026 typically the paths where your waivers.json, findings, and any locally-mirrored policy files live (for SCP-self: services.yml, output/findings/waivers.json; configure for your repo's equivalent artifact paths).' One sentence addition; no descoping.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-NIT-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.2 states the Renovate preset is 'published at renovate/v1.0.0 (independent of the federation-primitive v* tag series)' without a one-sentence rationale for the independence. An adopter who does not read docs/security/branch-protection.md may conflate a renovate/v1.1.0 preset release with a v1.1.0 workflow release and misinterpret Renovate PRs that bump only the preset ref.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.2: 'The preset is published at renovate/v1.0.0 (independent of the federation-primitive v* tag series; see docs/security/branch-protection.md).' The cross-reference to branch-protection.md is present but that document explains the ruleset mechanics, not the tag-independence rationale. docs/security/branch-protection.md \\u00a7'Tag protection \\u2014 renovate/v* pattern': 'The two rulesets are siblings, not nested. The v* ruleset covers federation-primitive release tags. The renovate/v* ruleset covers preset release tags.' Still no rationale for why they are independent.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Minor. An adopter who receives a Renovate PR bumping renovate/v1.0.0 \\u2192 renovate/v1.1.0 but not the @SHA pin may approve it expecting a workflow rule update and find none, or reject it thinking it is incomplete. The Renovate PR title/body should clarify, but the appendix does not prepare adopters for this scenario.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add one sentence to \\u00a712.7.2 after the independence note: 'The tag series are independent because preset configuration changes (customManager regex adjustments, automerge policy, datasource options) may need to ship without a new workflow release, and vice versa \\u2014 a v1.1.0 Rego rule change ships a v1.1.0 workflow tag without necessarily changing the Renovate preset.' This is a documentation addition, not a scope change.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"COMP-NIT-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.9 pre-commit hook snippet uses the placeholder 'exec <your-local-clone-of-standards-control-plane>/scripts/scp-policy-check' with no guidance on where to maintain the clone, how to keep it updated, or whether the local clone should be pinned to the same SHA as the CI wrapper. The 'optional, recommended' qualifier reduces urgency, but the placeholder is incomplete for a new adopter.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.9: 'cat > .git/hooks/pre-commit <<'EOF' \\u2026 exec <your-local-clone-of-standards-control-plane>/scripts/scp-policy-check'. No subsequent note explains: (a) typical clone location (e.g. ~/src/standards-control-plane- or a submodule); (b) whether the clone's checked-out SHA should match the CI wrapper's pinned SHA for exact parity; (c) that an outdated local clone would evaluate against a different rule version than CI, defeating local reproduction. The local-reproduction guarantee in \\u00a712.7.6 (Python vs Rego parity via conflict-gate) and in scripts/scp-policy-check (same lib/policy_check_invocation.sh) assumes the same-SHA clone constraint, but this is not stated in \\u00a712.7.9.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Trivial for adopters who can infer from context. Non-trivial for WP-SCP-024 estate adopters whose setup is driven by a scaffolding script \\u2014 if the scaffolding script copies \\u00a712.7.9 verbatim, it produces a pre-commit hook with a literal angle-bracket path that fails chmod execution.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add two sentences below the hook snippet: 'Replace <your-local-clone-of-standards-control-plane> with the absolute path to a local clone of the SCP repo. Pin the local clone to the same commit SHA as your CI wrapper's uses: line to guarantee the pre-commit hook evaluates against the same rule version as CI.'\\\"}],\\\"self_reported_concerns\\\":[\\\"COMP-MAJ-001 and COMP-MAJ-002 should be treated as merge-blocking within this dispatch: both require a one-line edit to \\u00a711.10 of the same file (ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md) that this PR modifies. Dispatching a fix round with a targeted \\u00a711.10 amendment before merge is the correct posture under WP-SCP-022 \\u00a74.3 no-silent-descope invariant \\u2014 the inconsistency is observable by any adopter who reads both sections. The remaining five MAJ findings (COMP-MAJ-003 through COMP-MAJ-007) can be fixed in a single targeted fix round without requiring re-review of the plan sub-criteria or decision-row mappings, which are all verified correct.\\\",\\\"I could not verify at review time whether scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh contains the regex pattern '^|\\\\\\\\s*D-[0-9]{3}\\\\\\\\s*|\\\\\\\\s*20[0-9]{2}' (corrected three-digit form per 020K R1 correctness review COR-001) or the older four-digit form. The glob confirms the file exists. The correctness lens should verify the script's regex matches the documented D-NNN pattern.\\\",\\\"The plan \\u00a74 020H part 3 states 'exact minimal caller wrapper text below \\u2014 no line-count claim' \\u2014 ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.1 adds multi-line comments above the if: expression (fork-PR rationale) that are not in the plan's canonical YAML snippet. This is a governed addition (TF-D1-002 closure requires security rationale for the mandatory if:) but it means the 'minimal wrapper' in \\u00a712.7.1 is longer than the plan's literal snippet. This is a legitimate deviation and the DISPATCH-NOTE accounts for it; I flag it only so the correctness reviewer can verify the additions are purely commentary and do not alter the workflow's execution semantics.\\\"]}\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-completeness-r1retry.json:109:        \"evidence\": \"\\u00a712.7.1 documents the wrapper YAML. \\u00a712.7.2 documents the Renovate extends entry. \\u00a712.7.3 documents the enable-required-check.sh helper and mentions '--plan first to see both the current state and the proposed payload.' None of these sub-sections includes an explicit 'open a draft PR with a deliberate violation and confirm the gate blocks merge' or 'run gh api to verify the required-check context is registered' step. The adoption acceptance checklist in \\u00a715 covers the overall SCP onboarding but does not include a federation-primitive-specific verification step (e.g., verify the policy-check / scp/policy-check context appears in branch protection after running the helper).\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-completeness-r1retry.json:117:        \"evidence\": \"\\u00a712.7.5 'Rollback procedure': '1. Revert the wrapper's @SHA pin to the previous SCP release SHA via PR. 2. Update the # tag: comment to match. 3. Renovate will re-run the bump \\u2026 4. Open an issue using the rule-regression template.' All four steps assume the adopter wants to stay on an older SCP release, not exit the integration entirely. There is no instruction covering: remove .github/workflows/policy-check.yml, remove the extends entry from renovate.json, and run enable-required-check.sh with a removal flag (or manually remove the required-check via gh api) to un-gate the branch protection.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-completeness-r1retry.json:140:        \"claim\": \"\\u00a712.7 presents sub-sections in logical order but provides no explicit step-sequenced order-of-operations for a new adopter. A first-time integrator must infer the sequence (add wrapper \\u2192 add Renovate extends \\u2192 run enable-required-check.sh \\u2192 verify gate \\u2192 understand break-glass) from sub-section ordering alone.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-completeness-r1retry.json:143:        \"impact\": \"A new adopter who reads \\u00a712.7 in sequence without framing may attempt to run enable-required-check.sh before adding the wrapper (if \\u00a712.7.3 feels like step 1 independently), or may skip the Renovate preset assuming it is optional. The order matters because the required-check must reference a check that has already been observed (GitHub only accepts a context name that exists in the repo's check history). Wrong order \\u2192 required-check points to a non-existent context \\u2192 permanent 'Expected \\u2014 Waiting'.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety.json:32:    \"result\": \"I have all artifacts. Producing the structured review output now.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"BLOCKED\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"SAFETY_BYPASS\\\",\\n  \\\"reviewer_id\\\": \\\"r1-safety-bypass-020h3\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-022/020h3\\\",\\n  \\\"target\\\": \\\"docs/adoption/ADOPT-001-project-onboarding.md \\u00a712.7; .github/workflows/policy-check.yml; scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh; scripts/enable-required-check.sh; renovate/default.json\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.1 declares the fork-PR `if:` guard mandatory but provides no recommendation to protect the caller wrapper file itself with CODEOWNERS. A developer on the adopter's team can open a PR that removes the `if:`, changes `threshold: deny` to `threshold: warn`, or weakens `permissions:` \\u2014 none of which trigger the break-glass three-gate check.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.1 comment 'mandatory; do not remove this `if:`'; \\u00a711.10 recommends CODEOWNERS for `.scp/rule-config.yaml` (rule-wide bypass file) but makes no equivalent recommendation for `.github/workflows/policy-check.yml` (the caller wrapper carrying the fork guard and threshold). The Reference block at \\u00a712.7 end does not include a CODEOWNERS coverage note for the wrapper.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A rogue developer on a multi-maintainer adopter repo can silently weaken the gate by modifying the caller wrapper in a normal PR that passes review under an unprotected `.github/workflows/` path. The fork-PR `if:` removal in particular is invisible to SCP enforcement \\u2014 SCP only evaluates same-repo PRs and would not detect its own guard being deleted.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a CODEOWNERS recommendation in \\u00a712.7.1 covering the adopter's `.github/workflows/policy-check.yml`, symmetric with \\u00a711.10's recommendation for `.scp/rule-config.yaml`. Example: '`.github/workflows/policy-check.yml @your-team-owners` \\u2014 ensure the caller wrapper cannot be weakened without an owning reviewer approving the change.'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.10 prohibits `secrets: inherit` and gives the correct rationale ('GITHUB_TOKEN is the privilege ceiling'), but omits the forward-compatibility risk. An adopter who observes that the reusable workflow currently declares no `secrets:` block may reason that `secrets: inherit` is a safe no-op and add it anyway. This assumption becomes dangerous if a future SCP version introduces any named `secrets:` declaration \\u2014 all adopters who pre-added `secrets: inherit` would immediately pass that secret to SCP without a new adopter-side change.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.10 text; policy-check.yml (no `secrets:` block at top level \\u2014 correct at v1.0.0); WP-SCP-020 \\u00a78 risk row ('No `secrets:` declared on reusable workflow; adopter forbids `secrets: inherit`').\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"If a future SCP release adds a `secrets:` block (e.g., for an optional signing key or external service token), adopters who silently added `secrets: inherit` would unknowingly pass every secret in their repo to the SCP workflow. The blast radius is bounded only by the caller's secrets store.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add one sentence to \\u00a712.7.10: 'Do not add `secrets: inherit` even if the reusable workflow currently accepts none \\u2014 a future SCP version may declare named secrets, retroactively making your `secrets: inherit` pass all caller secrets to the workflow.'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.4 states 'Single-operator adopters: self-approval is structurally impossible on GitHub' for break-glass Gate 1. This claim was explicitly corrected by D-033 (2026-04-30): D-033 set `required_approving_review_count=0` on SCP itself precisely because count\\u22651 locks a solo operator out entirely, not because self-approval is impossible. With count=0, Gate 1 (CODEOWNERS approval) is a documentation requirement with no machine enforcement \\u2014 a single-operator adopter satisfies all three gates unilaterally. The appendix overstates the security guarantee by describing a three-gate model that collapses to a two-gate model in single-operator mode.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.4 ('self-approval is structurally impossible on GitHub'); D-033 ('Updated to count=0 + codeowner_reviews=false in single-operator mode\\u2026 GitHub forbids PR authors from approving their own PRs, so count=1 + codeowner=true locks the operator out entirely'); docs/security/branch-protection.md (required_approving_review_count=0); WP-SCP-020 \\u00a78 ('bus-factor-1 risk live'). The DISPATCH-NOTE.md confirms this slice closes TF-D1-002 but does not flag D-033's correction as requiring an appendix amendment.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Single-operator adopters reading \\u00a712.7.4 believe the break-glass requires human approval from a CODEOWNER. In practice count=0 means no approval is required, and the operator can self-merge a bypass PR satisfying only Gates 2 (D-NNN row authored by themselves) and 3 (waiver authored by themselves). The appendix misleads adopters about the security posture they're configuring. Multi-maintainer adopters who encounter the single-operator branching text may draw wrong conclusions about the relationship between CODEOWNERS and review enforcement.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Update \\u00a712.7.4 to accurately describe two modes: (a) multi-maintainer: Gate 1 is machine-enforced (required_approving_review_count\\u22651, require_review_from_non_author=true); (b) single-operator: Gate 1 is nominal (count=0 per D-033; GitHub structurally prevents self-review but does not REQUIRE any review), and only Gates 2+3 are machine-checked by verify-bypass-pairing.sh. Remove the phrase 'self-approval is structurally impossible' and replace with: 'Single-operator adopters: per D-033 and WP-SCP-020 \\u00a78, review_count=0 is required (count\\u22651 locks out the sole operator); Gate 1 is therefore documentation-only. This is the accepted bus-factor-1 cost \\u2014 Gates 2 and 3 are the machine-enforced checks.'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-004\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.5 rollback procedure (4-hour target) does not instruct adopters to close or dismiss any open Renovate-generated PR that is already bumping the pin to the bad SHA. An inexperienced maintainer may mistake the open Renovate bump PR for a routine update and merge it during or after the revert, re-pinning to the bad SHA and undoing the rollback.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.5 steps 1-4; renovate/default.json (`automerge: false` \\u2014 auto-merge is disabled, but manual merge remains possible); \\u00a712.7.2 (Renovate bump PRs are labeled `scp-bump` per renovate/default.json packageRules labels).\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A rollback can be silently undone within the 4-hour window if a pending `scp-bump` PR is merged by a team member who is unaware of the rollback context. The bad SHA re-enters the adopter's repo with a clean commit history and no signal that it was the subject of a rollback.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a step 0 to \\u00a712.7.5 before step 1: 'Check for any open pull requests labeled `scp-federation` / `scp-bump` in your repo and close them before beginning the revert. This prevents a pending Renovate bump PR from being accidentally merged to the bad SHA during the rollback window. Re-open or let Renovate recreate the PR only after confirming the SCP-side regression is resolved.'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-005\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.2 directs adopters to extend the preset at `#renovate/v1.0.0` and cross-references `docs/security/branch-protection.md` only via the Reference block at \\u00a712.7's end. The inline text does not state that the `renovate/v*` tag series carries a Repository Ruleset (D-034) preventing force-push and deletion. No adopter-side verification step is documented for confirming the tag's protection state before extending it.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.2 ('see `docs/security/branch-protection.md`' is in the Reference block, not inline); D-034 (ACCEPTED: `scp-tag-protection-renovate-v` ruleset on `refs/tags/renovate/v*`); docs/security/branch-protection.md 'Tag protection \\u2014 renovate/v* pattern' section.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"An adopter who does not follow the cross-reference adopts the preset on trust without knowing the tag is protected. More critically, there is no documented adopter-side verification step. If tag protection were ever disabled (admin override, incident, ruleset misconfiguration), adopters would have no process to detect it. The preset trust chain is fully delegated to the Mend-hosted Renovate Bot with no independent integrity check.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add inline to \\u00a712.7.2: 'The `renovate/v*` tag series is protected by Repository Ruleset `scp-tag-protection-renovate-v` (D-034) \\u2014 deletion and force-push are blocked, including for admins. Verify before extending: `gh api repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/rulesets --jq \\\\\\\".[] | select(.name == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"scp-tag-protection-renovate-v\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") | .enforcement\\\\\\\"` \\u2014 expected: `active`.'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-006\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.3 names the required PAT scope (`administration:write` on the single target repo) and the script emits a WARNING, but neither the appendix nor the warning explains what `administration:write` covers beyond branch-protection configuration. An adopter may grant this scope thinking it is narrowly scoped to status-check and review settings, not realising it also covers webhook creation, environment management, repository transfer initiation, and archival.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.3 step 4 ('Logs a stderr WARNING about required PAT scope (`administration:write` on the single target repo)'); enable-required-check.sh lines 104-108 (WARNING to stderr: 'Recommended: fine-grained PAT scoped to the single target repo.'); GitHub REST API docs: `administration:write` on a fine-grained PAT covers the full administration sub-resource.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"An adopter who authorizes an operator's fine-grained PAT with `administration:write` and then fails to revoke it after onboarding has granted long-lived near-admin write capability to the operator. A compromised operator credential could then configure malicious webhooks or transfer the repo.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add one sentence to \\u00a712.7.3: '`administration:write` covers more than branch protection (webhooks, environments, transfer initiation). Issue a single-use fine-grained PAT scoped to this one repo, run the helper, then immediately revoke or expire the PAT. Do not retain an `administration:write` PAT for routine use.'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-007\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.9 instructs adopters to install a pre-commit hook pointing at `<your-local-clone-of-standards-control-plane>/scripts/scp-policy-check` but does not state that this clone must be at the same commit SHA as the CI wrapper's pin, nor does it warn that a stale or tampered SCP clone produces hook verdicts that diverge from CI. A developer with an out-of-date local clone will see false-pass results for rules that changed between their clone's version and the pinned CI version.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.9 hook snippet (`exec <your-local-clone-of-standards-control-plane>/scripts/scp-policy-check`); policy-check.yml 'Checkout SCP runtime repository' step (pins to `github.action_ref` \\u2014 the caller wrapper's SHA pin); scripts/scp-policy-check.lock (SHA256-locked OPA and Conftest versions, but the local clone itself has no integrity check).\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A developer's pre-commit hook runs old policy rules, producing false-pass results for changes that would be denied at CI. The developer only discovers the divergence when CI runs, defeating the stated purpose of catching denies before pushing. More severely: a tampered local clone can suppress any deny entirely; the developer believes they are clean and the CI catch becomes the only safety layer.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add to \\u00a712.7.9: 'The local SCP clone MUST be checked out at the same commit SHA as your wrapper's `# tag:` pin. Run `git -C <clone-path> fetch && git -C <clone-path> checkout <CI-pin-SHA>` before using the hook, or add a wrapper that reads the SHA from your `.github/workflows/policy-check.yml` and verifies the clone before invoking the script. Divergence from the CI-pinned SHA invalidates the hook's verdicts.'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-008\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.11 documents the freshness-warning (> 2 minor versions behind \\u2192 `::warning::`) as landing in 020H.1 and names the Renovate preset as 'the primary freshness signal' in the interim. It does not recommend a manual quarterly pin-freshness check as a fallback for adopters whose Renovate instance is misconfigured, disabled, or does not have the preset installed.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.11 ('Until 020H.1 ships, the Renovate preset's SHA bump (\\u00a712.7.2) is the primary freshness signal'); \\u00a712.7.2 (Renovate as the sole automation path).\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"An adopter who onboards with the preset but whose Renovate bot is subsequently disabled (credential rotation, app revocation, billing pause) receives no signal that their SCP pin is stale. If a SCP release contains a security fix or a rule correction, the adopter drifts silently with no automated or documented manual fallback during the 020H.1 gap period.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add to \\u00a712.7.11: 'During the 020H.1 gap period, also schedule a manual quarterly check: compare your wrapper's `# tag:` comment against the latest SCP release at `https://github.com/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/releases`. This is the safety net if Renovate is unavailable.'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-009\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.4 presents `scp_bypass: true` (three-gate model) as the mechanism to bypass the federation gate. \\u00a712.7.7 lists SCP-E006 as 'Non-blocking' without noting that disabling a rule via `.scp/rule-config.yaml` achieves the same bypass effect with zero three-gate friction \\u2014 it is not subject to `scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh`. \\u00a712.7 contains no cross-reference to \\u00a711.10's CODEOWNERS recommendation for `.scp/rule-config.yaml`. An adopter reading \\u00a712.7 as the federation integration contract does not learn that rule-config is an alternative, lower-friction bypass surface requiring independent protection.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.4 ('To bypass on a single PR (`scp_bypass: true` on the wrapper), the PR must satisfy ALL THREE gates'); \\u00a712.7.7 (SCP-E006: 'Non-blocking'); policy-check.yml bypass-gate step (condition: `if: inputs.scp_bypass` \\u2014 rule-config disables are NOT routed through the bypass-gate); \\u00a711.10 (CODEOWNERS recommendation for `.scp/rule-config.yaml` \\u2014 not cross-referenced from \\u00a712.7); policy-check.yml 'Validate rule-config against schema' and 'Emit disabled-rule observability records' steps.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"An attacker or careless developer with PR access can open a PR adding `.scp/rule-config.yaml` with `disable: true` on any rule, subject only to: (1) normal PR review (no three-gate check), (2) `expires_at` (time-bounded but up to any future date), and (3) SCP-E006 observability annotation. If `.scp/rule-config.yaml` is not CODEOWNERS-protected \\u2014 which \\u00a712.7 does not require \\u2014 a single developer can suppress any rule's deny with no multi-party approval. The one-release grace period for expired disables extends the bypass window further.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"(1) Add a 'WARNING' box in \\u00a712.7 (after \\u00a712.7.4 or as a \\u00a712.7.4.1) explicitly naming rule-config as an alternative bypass path NOT covered by the three-gate model: 'A rule disabled via `.scp/rule-config.yaml` suppresses a deny without triggering the break-glass pairing check. Treat `.scp/rule-config.yaml` as a bypass-surface equivalent to `scp_bypass: true`. See \\u00a711.10 for the CODEOWNERS requirement.' (2) Elevate the \\u00a711.10 CODEOWNERS recommendation for `.scp/rule-config.yaml` to a MUST in the context of \\u00a712.7 adopter integration. (3) Note that rule-config disables are scoped by `expires_at` and produce SCP-E006, but do not produce a D-NNN row or a waivers.json entry.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-010\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.1 declares `statuses: write` for the readback commit-status post. D-029 scopes the USAGE to the PR head SHA, but the GITHUB_TOKEN with `statuses: write` covers all SHAs in the caller repo. Additionally, the appendix does not warn adopters that misconfiguring the required check to the commit-status context (`scp/policy-check-readback`) instead of the check-run context (`policy-check / scp/policy-check`) would expose the gate to forgery: a compromised runner could post any commit status, but cannot forge a check-run.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"\\u00a712.7.1 (`statuses: write  # required for scp/policy-check-readback per D-029`); D-029 ('posts only to `repos/<caller>/statuses/${{ pull_request.head.sha }}`' \\u2014 this describes the code's behaviour, not the token's scope); policy-check.yml readback step (posts to `scp/policy-check-readback`); docs/security/branch-protection.md (required check = `policy-check / scp/policy-check` \\u2014 a check-run context, not a commit-status context).\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"An adopter who configures `scp/policy-check-readback` (a commit STATUS) as the required check \\u2014 rather than `policy-check / scp/policy-check` (a check-RUN) \\u2014 creates a gate bypassable by any runner with `statuses: write`. The distinction between the two context types is explained in D-033 and docs/security/branch-protection.md, but not in \\u00a712.7 where an adopter makes the branch-protection configuration decision.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a note in \\u00a712.7.1 or \\u00a712.7.3: 'The required status check MUST be configured to the check-run context (`policy-check / scp/policy-check`), NOT to the commit-status context (`scp/policy-check-readback`). The check-run context cannot be forged via `statuses: write`; the commit-status context can. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper defaults to the correct check-run context (D-033).'\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-011\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The Regal linter binary in policy-check.yml is downloaded at a hardcoded version (`REGAL_VERSION=\\\\\\\"0.40.0\\\\\\\"`, line 177) without SHA256 verification. OPA and Conftest are both SHA256-verified via `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock`; Regal is absent from the lockfile and from `scripts/.tool-versions`. A supply-chain attacker who substitutes the Regal binary at the GitHub releases URL executes arbitrary code in every adopter's runner with full access to `GITHUB_TOKEN`.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"policy-check.yml lines 177 (`REGAL_VERSION=\\\\\\\"0.40.0\\\\\\\"`), 273-275 (`curl ... regal ... || emit_infra_failure`; no `verify_sha256` call follows); scripts/scp-policy-check.lock (OPA + Conftest entries only \\u2014 no Regal entry); scripts/.tool-versions (`opa 1.15.2`, `conftest 0.68.2` \\u2014 no Regal entry). Contrast: OPA verification at lines 204-209 (`verify_sha256`), Conftest at lines 267 (`verify_sha256`). Regal is also not monitored by Dependabot or the Renovate `scripts/.tool-versions` watcher.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A compromised Regal binary executes in the adopter's runner (ubuntu-24.04) before the policy evaluation steps. It has access to `GITHUB_TOKEN` (with `statuses: write` and `contents: read`), all checked-out files, and the `RUNNER_TEMP` directory containing the verified OPA/Conftest binaries. Even though Regal's role is linting (not evaluation), its execution provides an arbitrary code execution primitive on every PR in every adopter repo. This is a pre-existing workflow gap, not introduced by \\u00a712.7, but the appendix directs adopters to use this workflow without disclosing the gap.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"This is a workflow fix, not a \\u00a712.7 text fix \\u2014 but \\u00a712.7 should document the supply-chain posture: (1) Add Regal to `scripts/.tool-versions` and `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` with SHA256 entries for linux-x64 and linux-arm64. (2) Add a `verify_sha256 \\\\\\\"${BIN_DIR}/regal\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"${REGAL_SHA256}\\\\\\\"` call immediately after the Regal download, using the same pattern as OPA/Conftest. (3) Consider whether `REGAL_VERSION` should be environment-configured or lockfile-driven, not hardcoded in the workflow. Fix should land before or with the next release; track as a follow-up to this slice.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-012\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"In `verify-bypass-pairing.sh`, Gate 2 (D-NNN row check) uses `grep -F -- \\\\\\\"${rule_id}\\\\\\\"` to find the rule_id anywhere in the added table row. An attacker who controls the bypass PR can author a D-NNN row where the rule_id appears only in the Rationale column for an unrelated purpose, satisfying the content check without a genuine bypass authorization. The temporal constraint (row must be added in this PR's diff) is correct and mitigates reuse of old rows, but the content check is semantically loose.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh line 33: `grep -E '^\\\\+\\\\\\\\|\\\\\\\\s*D-[0-9]{3}...' | grep -F -- \\\\\\\"${rule_id}\\\\\\\"` \\u2014 the second grep is a fixed-string substring search across the entire row, not restricted to a specific column position.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Low practical risk: the attacker still needs to add a NEW D-NNN row in the bypass PR, which is observable and auditable. However, the D-NNN row does not need to explicitly authorize the bypass \\u2014 it just needs to mention the rule_id anywhere. An attacker could add a plausible-sounding decision row (e.g., 'D-NNN: Acknowledge SCP-R-001 is temporarily inapplicable to this repo segment') that provides documentation cover without a genuine governance authorization.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Document the content-check limitation in \\u00a712.7.4: 'Note: the `verify-bypass-pairing.sh` script checks that a D-NNN row containing the bypassed rule_id was added in the same PR, but does not validate that the row's Decision column specifically authorizes the bypass. Human reviewers should verify the D-NNN row content is a genuine bypass authorization, not merely a mention of the rule.' Optionally, tighten the script to require the rule_id in column 3 (the Decision column) specifically.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"BLOCKED \\u2014 3 MAJ findings require resolution before this slice merges. SAFE-003 (MAJ): \\u00a712.7.4 single-operator break-glass description contradicts D-033; the phrase 'self-approval is structurally impossible' is operationally wrong for count=0 mode and overstates Gate 1 enforcement. SAFE-009 (MAJ): \\u00a712.7 never names rule-config as an alternative, lower-friction bypass path not covered by the three-gate model; the \\u00a711.10 CODEOWNERS requirement for `.scp/rule-config.yaml` is not cross-referenced from the federation appendix. SAFE-011 (MAJ): Regal binary download lacks SHA256 verification (absent from lockfile and tool-versions); this is a supply-chain gap in the reusable workflow all adopters are directed to use. SAFE-001 through SAFE-010 (8 MIN) and SAFE-012 (nit) are non-blocking individually but collectively indicate the appendix needs a hardening pass on privilege-scope disclosures, CODEOWNERS coverage, rollback race mitigation, and freshness fallback. No CRIT findings. No findings recommend descoping. All mitigations are additive documentation or workflow fixes.\\\"\\n}\\n```\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-safety.json:80:  \"stdout_tail\": \"licy-check`) would expose the gate to forgery: a compromised runner could post any commit status, but cannot forge a check-run.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"\\u00a712.7.1 (`statuses: write  # required for scp/policy-check-readback per D-029`); D-029 ('posts only to `repos/<caller>/statuses/${{ pull_request.head.sha }}`' \\u2014 this describes the code's behaviour, not the token's scope); policy-check.yml readback step (posts to `scp/policy-check-readback`); docs/security/branch-protection.md (required check = `policy-check / scp/policy-check` \\u2014 a check-run context, not a commit-status context).\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"An adopter who configures `scp/policy-check-readback` (a commit STATUS) as the required check \\u2014 rather than `policy-check / scp/policy-check` (a check-RUN) \\u2014 creates a gate bypassable by any runner with `statuses: write`. The distinction between the two context types is explained in D-033 and docs/security/branch-protection.md, but not in \\u00a712.7 where an adopter makes the branch-protection configuration decision.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Add a note in \\u00a712.7.1 or \\u00a712.7.3: 'The required status check MUST be configured to the check-run context (`policy-check / scp/policy-check`), NOT to the commit-status context (`scp/policy-check-readback`). The check-run context cannot be forged via `statuses: write`; the commit-status context can. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper defaults to the correct check-run context (D-033).'\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SAFE-011\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"MAJ\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The Regal linter binary in policy-check.yml is downloaded at a hardcoded version (`REGAL_VERSION=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0.40.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, line 177) without SHA256 verification. OPA and Conftest are both SHA256-verified via `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock`; Regal is absent from the lockfile and from `scripts/.tool-versions`. A supply-chain attacker who substitutes the Regal binary at the GitHub releases URL executes arbitrary code in every adopter's runner with full access to `GITHUB_TOKEN`.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"policy-check.yml lines 177 (`REGAL_VERSION=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0.40.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`), 273-275 (`curl ... regal ... || emit_infra_failure`; no `verify_sha256` call follows); scripts/scp-policy-check.lock (OPA + Conftest entries only \\u2014 no Regal entry); scripts/.tool-versions (`opa 1.15.2`, `conftest 0.68.2` \\u2014 no Regal entry). Contrast: OPA verification at lines 204-209 (`verify_sha256`), Conftest at lines 267 (`verify_sha256`). Regal is also not monitored by Dependabot or the Renovate `scripts/.tool-versions` watcher.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"A compromised Regal binary executes in the adopter's runner (ubuntu-24.04) before the policy evaluation steps. It has access to `GITHUB_TOKEN` (with `statuses: write` and `contents: read`), all checked-out files, and the `RUNNER_TEMP` directory containing the verified OPA/Conftest binaries. Even though Regal's role is linting (not evaluation), its execution provides an arbitrary code execution primitive on every PR in every adopter repo. This is a pre-existing workflow gap, not introduced by \\u00a712.7, but the appendix directs adopters to use this workflow without disclosing the gap.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"This is a workflow fix, not a \\u00a712.7 text fix \\u2014 but \\u00a712.7 should document the supply-chain posture: (1) Add Regal to `scripts/.tool-versions` and `scripts/scp-policy-check.lock` with SHA256 entries for linux-x64 and linux-arm64. (2) Add a `verify_sha256 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${BIN_DIR}/regal\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${REGAL_SHA256}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` call immediately after the Regal download, using the same pattern as OPA/Conftest. (3) Consider whether `REGAL_VERSION` should be environment-configured or lockfile-driven, not hardcoded in the workflow. Fix should land before or with the next release; track as a follow-up to this slice.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SAFE-012\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"nit\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"In `verify-bypass-pairing.sh`, Gate 2 (D-NNN row check) uses `grep -F -- \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${rule_id}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` to find the rule_id anywhere in the added table row. An attacker who controls the bypass PR can author a D-NNN row where the rule_id appears only in the Rationale column for an unrelated purpose, satisfying the content check without a genuine bypass authorization. The temporal constraint (row must be added in this PR's diff) is correct and mitigates reuse of old rows, but the content check is semantically loose.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh line 33: `grep -E '^\\\\\\\\+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*D-[0-9]{3}...' | grep -F -- \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${rule_id}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` \\u2014 the second grep is a fixed-string substring search across the entire row, not restricted to a specific column position.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Low practical risk: the attacker still needs to add a NEW D-NNN row in the bypass PR, which is observable and auditable. However, the D-NNN row does not need to explicitly authorize the bypass \\u2014 it just needs to mention the rule_id anywhere. An attacker could add a plausible-sounding decision row (e.g., 'D-NNN: Acknowledge SCP-R-001 is temporarily inapplicable to this repo segment') that provides documentation cover without a genuine governance authorization.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document the content-check limitation in \\u00a712.7.4: 'Note: the `verify-bypass-pairing.sh` script checks that a D-NNN row containing the bypassed rule_id was added in the same PR, but does not validate that the row's Decision column specifically authorizes the bypass. Human reviewers should verify the D-NNN row content is a genuine bypass authorization, not merely a mention of the rule.' Optionally, tighten the script to require the rule_id in column 3 (the Decision column) specifically.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ],\\\\n  \\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"BLOCKED \\u2014 3 MAJ findings require resolution before this slice merges. SAFE-003 (MAJ): \\u00a712.7.4 single-operator break-glass description contradicts D-033; the phrase 'self-approval is structurally impossible' is operationally wrong for count=0 mode and overstates Gate 1 enforcement. SAFE-009 (MAJ): \\u00a712.7 never names rule-config as an alternative, lower-friction bypass path not covered by the three-gate model; the \\u00a711.10 CODEOWNERS requirement for `.scp/rule-config.yaml` is not cross-referenced from the federation appendix. SAFE-011 (MAJ): Regal binary download lacks SHA256 verification (absent from lockfile and tool-versions); this is a supply-chain gap in the reusable workflow all adopters are directed to use. SAFE-001 through SAFE-010 (8 MIN) and SAFE-012 (nit) are non-blocking individually but collectively indicate the appendix needs a hardening pass on privilege-scope disclosures, CODEOWNERS coverage, rollback race mitigation, and freshness fallback. No CRIT findings. No findings recommend descoping. All mitigations are additive documentation or workflow fixes.\\\\\\\"\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\",\\\"stop_reason\\\":\\\"end_turn\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"55c75351-12cb-4d62-9e91-46aeac570cff\\\",\\\"total_cost_usd\\\":0.9047607500000001,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":7,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":101631,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":269365,\\\"output_tokens\\\":29388,\\\"server_tool_use\\\":{\\\"web_search_requests\\\":0,\\\"web_fetch_requests\\\":0},\\\"service_tier\\\":\\\"standard\\\",\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":101631,\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0},\\\"inference_geo\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"iterations\\\":[{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1,\\\"output_tokens\\\":6936,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":108610,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":892,\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":892},\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\"}],\\\"speed\\\":\\\"standard\\\"},\\\"modelUsage\\\":{\\\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":1844,\\\"outputTokens\\\":30,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.001994,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000},\\\"claude-sonnet-4-6\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":7,\\\"outputTokens\\\":29388,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":269365,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":101631,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.9027667500000001,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000}},\\\"permission_denials\\\":[],\\\"terminal_reason\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"fast_mode_state\\\":\\\"off\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"cf88e97d-f37c-421e-9b15-cffeaa54e559\\\"}\\n\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r2-package.json:6:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r3.json:32:    \"result\": \"I have all the evidence I need. The critical MAJ finding is at line 343: `FAIL=0` in the verify section resets `FAIL=1` set by the apply-phase APPLY_FAIL trap (lines 322, 329) \\u2014 this is a regression introduced by fix-round-2.\\n\\n{\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"R3-SAFETY\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"020G\\\",\\\"work_package\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-022\\\",\\\"round\\\":3,\\\"lens\\\":\\\"safety_bypass\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"NEEDS_REVISION\\\",\\\"r2_closure_verification\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-001\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"PARTIALLY-CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"The APPLY_FAIL trap pattern is present (lines 312\\u2013331): PUT failure sets APPLY_FAIL=1 \\u2192 FAIL=1 (line 322); POST failure sets APPLY_FAIL=1 \\u2192 FAIL=1 (line 329); both emit diagnostic stderr; script falls through to log emission. However, the verify section immediately follows with `FAIL=0` at line 343, which unconditionally resets FAIL regardless of whether the apply phase set it to 1. If the subsequent GET succeeds and all verification checks pass (because the pre-existing protection state already satisfies them), the script exits 0 and emits 'verification passed \\u2713' \\u2014 the apply-phase failure is silently laundered. The SAF-R2-001 fix introduced the trap correctly but failed to preserve the FAIL=1 state across the verify-phase initialization. Raised as SAF-R3-001 (MAJ).\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-002\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"Lines 354\\u2013366: 'verification passed \\u2713' is now inside the `else` branch of `if [ \\\\\\\"${FAIL:-0}\\\\\\\" -ne 0 ]`. When FAIL=1 (set by verification checks), the success message is suppressed and the failure path fires instead. Closure verified for the verify-phase case; the apply-phase FAIL reset is a separate regression (SAF-R3-001).\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-007\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"CORR2-003 closure confirmed at lines 181\\u2013185: `if [ \\\\\\\"$ACK_ADMIN_BYPASS\\\\\\\" -eq 1 ] && [ \\\\\\\"$ENFORCE_ADMINS\\\\\\\" = \\\\\\\"true\\\\\\\" ]` exits 2 with an explicit error. An operator with the ack flag pre-filled from history but without `--no-enforce-admins` gets a hard error rather than silent bypass. The fix correctly closes the history-preload path; it does not shorten the friction on the canonical bypass path (both flags present, which still requires the 5-second sleep). CLOSED.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-003\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"ACKNOWLEDGED-DEFERRED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"TF-020G-003 still open. `readlink -f \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\"` at line 379 remains; macOS BSD readlink exits non-zero, fallback to bare `$0` is in place. Not addressed in fix-round-2; tracked forward per dispatch note.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-004\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"ACKNOWLEDGED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"SIGINT inheritance gap not addressed; acknowledged as non-blocking per FIX-ROUND-2.md. The 5-second sleep remains unguarded by a local `trap - INT`.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-005\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"ACKNOWLEDGED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"CI guard breadth gap (Jenkins/Azure/Bamboo) not addressed; tracked as TF-020G-002 per dispatch note.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-006\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"ACKNOWLEDGED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"BEFORE_STATUS env-var collision not addressed; acknowledged as non-blocking nit per FIX-ROUND-2.md. BEFORE_STATUS still assigned only in the `||` branch (line 276) with `:-0` defaulting (line 277).\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-008\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"ACKNOWLEDGED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"Arg-validation order acknowledged as cosmetic per FIX-ROUND-2.md. The ack-flag check (lines 181\\u2013206) still fires before the regex/case validation (lines 213\\u2013224).\\\"}],\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R3-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The verify-section `FAIL=0` initialization at line 343 unconditionally resets `FAIL=1` set by the apply-phase APPLY_FAIL handler (lines 322 and 329), causing PUT or POST failures to be silently laundered as 'verification passed' when the pre-existing branch protection state satisfies all verification checks. This is a regression introduced by fix-round-2: the SAF-R2-001 trap correctly sets FAIL=1 in the apply phase, but the FAIL=0 on line 343 \\u2014 which was already present as the verify-phase initializer before fix-round-2 \\u2014 now clobbers it.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 322: `FAIL=1` set when PUT fails (APPLY_FAIL=1). Line 329: `FAIL=1` set when POST fails (APPLY_FAIL=1). Both paths emit diagnostic stderr and are designed to fall through to log emission. Line 343: `FAIL=0` unconditionally reinitializes FAIL before verification checks run. Lines 344\\u2013347: verification checks set FAIL=1 only if the GET-after returns bad state. If the GET succeeds and the pre-existing state satisfies strict=true, the configured contexts include REQUIRED_CONTEXT, enforce_admins matches, and required_signatures is already enabled \\u2014 all four checks pass, FAIL remains 0. Lines 354\\u2013362: `if [ \\\\\\\"${FAIL:-0}\\\\\\\" -ne 0 ]` is false; 'verification passed \\u2713' is emitted to stdout. Lines 435\\u2013437: `if [ \\\\\\\"${FAIL:-0}\\\\\\\" -ne 0 ]` is false; script exits 0. The APPLY_FAIL variable is never consulted in the final exit check. Concrete scenario: operator runs 020G to update the required-check context name on a repo already protected with the old context string; a transient network timeout on the PUT causes APPLY_FAIL=1 \\u2192 FAIL=1; the subsequent GET succeeds and returns the old (still-valid) protection state; if the old context name is a substring match of the new REQUIRED_CONTEXT under `grep -q -F`, or if old and new context are identical (re-run with same args), FAIL stays 0; script exits 0 with 'verification passed \\u2713'; operator pastes a false-success log entry into branch-protection-log.md; branch protection is not updated.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"The audit integrity guarantee of WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020G(iii) \\u2014 that every invocation, including failed applies, is accurately recorded \\u2014 is violated. An operator on a re-run or update invocation receives a false-success exit code and a 'verification passed \\u2713' stdout line despite the apply having failed. The markdown log entry does not record the failure. The stderr diagnostic messages from lines 320\\u2013321 or 327\\u2013328 are the only indication of failure, but these are lost in any context that discards stderr (log-scraping, piping to a file without 2>&1). The branch protection silently remains in the pre-update state with no audit trail.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Change line 343 from `FAIL=0` to `[ \\\\\\\"${APPLY_FAIL:-0}\\\\\\\" -ne 0 ] || FAIL=0`. This preserves any FAIL=1 set in the apply phase while still initializing FAIL for the verify phase when the apply succeeded. Alternatively, use a separate `VERIFY_FAIL=0` for the four check lines (344\\u2013347), then set `FAIL=1` if `VERIFY_FAIL` or the existing `FAIL` is non-zero before the if/else block. Either approach ensures that an apply-phase FAIL=1 propagates to the final exit check and is reflected in the log emission and exit code.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R3-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"REQUIRED_CONTEXT (from `--required-check` or `SCP_REQUIRED_CONTEXT`) has no character or length validation. Backticks or embedded newlines in the context name corrupt the emitted markdown log entry. Specifically: (a) the 'Required check' inline code span at the heredoc line embeds `${REQUIRED_CONTEXT}` directly inside backtick delimiters \\u2014 a context value containing a backtick or a newline breaks the span; (b) a context value containing an actual newline character causes the `printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$REQUIRED_CONTEXT\\\\\\\" | grep -q -F` verification check to split across lines in a way that is functionally benign but surprising.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 113: `REQUIRED_CONTEXT=\\\\\\\"${SCP_REQUIRED_CONTEXT:-policy-check / scp/policy-check}\\\\\\\"`. Line 160: `REQUIRED_CONTEXT=\\\\\\\"$2\\\\\\\"; shift 2`. No validation block follows for REQUIRED_CONTEXT format. In the heredoc at approximately lines 408\\u2013409, the emitted markdown contains `- **Required check:** \\\\\\\\`${REQUIRED_CONTEXT}\\\\\\\\`` where REQUIRED_CONTEXT is interpolated directly between backtick delimiters. If REQUIRED_CONTEXT is `my-check \\\\\\\\`injected\\\\\\\\``, the rendered line is `- **Required check:** \\\\\\\\`my-check \\\\\\\\`injected\\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`` which closes the first code span at 'my-check ' and opens a second, garbling the field. If REQUIRED_CONTEXT contains an embedded newline (e.g., passed via `$'foo\\\\\\\\nbar'`), the inline code span is split across two lines. The JSON fenced block (PUT payload applied) is safe because jq `--arg` JSON-encodes the value, serializing newlines as `\\\\\\\\n` escape sequences and leaving backticks as literals inside a quoted JSON string \\u2014 the resulting output is a single-line string field that cannot close the inner ``` fence.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Log integrity is compromised: the emitted markdown block, when pasted into branch-protection-log.md, renders incorrectly or contains injected markdown rather than the literal context string. An operator who pastes a malformed log entry without rendering it may not notice the corruption. Since the operator controls the REQUIRED_CONTEXT value, no privilege escalation is possible; the risk is audit-log legibility rather than unauthorized access.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a REQUIRED_CONTEXT validation block after line 160 (and at the top-level after defaults are set): reject any value containing `\\\\\\\\n`, `\\\\\\\\r`, or backtick characters. A permissive rule that mirrors real GitHub check-name grammar: `if ! printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$REQUIRED_CONTEXT\\\\\\\" | grep -qP '^[^\\\\\\\\x00-\\\\\\\\x1f\\\\\\\\x60]+$'; then echo \\\\\\\"error: --required-check contains control characters or backticks\\\\\\\" >&2; exit 2; fi`. Simpler alternative: add a length cap (GitHub check names have a practical limit) and a backtick/newline rejection via `case`.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R3-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The fix-round-2 CORR2-004 closure added `gh api -X POST .../required_signatures` to configure-020d2-required-check.sh (line 117) without the diagnostic error message that 020G emits for the same failure path. If the PUT at line 81 succeeds but the POST at line 117 fails, `set -e` aborts with only the bare gh CLI stderr output \\u2014 no '[020D2] ERROR: PUT applied but signatures NOT set \\u2014 re-run to retry' context message. The FIX-ROUND-2.md CORR2-004 closure note claims the POST 'is itself idempotent' but does not address the diagnostic quality asymmetry.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh line 81: `gh api -X PUT ... >/dev/null` \\u2014 bare, no trap. Line 117: `gh api -X POST \\\\\\\"repos/${REPO}/branches/${DEFAULT_BRANCH}/protection/required_signatures\\\\\\\" >/dev/null` \\u2014 bare, no trap. With `set -euo pipefail` (line 28), a non-zero exit from line 117 aborts immediately. The operator sees only gh CLI stderr (e.g., `gh: Error: HTTP 403: Resource not accessible by integration`) with no script-level message identifying the partial state. Compare: enable-required-check.sh lines 327\\u2013328 emit `ERROR: required_signatures POST failed; status-checks/admins/reviews are set but signatures are NOT` before falling through to log emission. 020D2 has no log-emission section and no comparable diagnostic. The idempotency claim (PUT + POST both safe to re-run) is correct \\u2014 GitHub returns 204 No Content for an already-enabled required_signatures POST, which gh treats as success \\u2014 but the operator must know to re-run; the bare abort gives no such guidance.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a trap for the POST failure in configure-020d2-required-check.sh line 117: `gh api -X POST \\\\\\\"repos/${REPO}/branches/${DEFAULT_BRANCH}/protection/required_signatures\\\\\\\" >/dev/null || { echo \\\\\\\"[020D2] ERROR: required_signatures POST failed; unified PUT was applied but signatures are NOT enabled \\u2014 re-run this script to retry the POST\\\\\\\" >&2; exit 1; }`. This matches the diagnostic quality of 020G for the same partial-state scenario. Bus-factor-1 / SCP-self-only scoping is accepted; the mitigation improves operator recovery UX without requiring a log-emission section.\\\"}],\\\"dispatch_probes_addressed\\\":[{\\\"probe\\\":1,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"APPLY_FAIL trap and partial gh stdout\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"NON-FINDING. When gh exits 0 (HTTP 200) on a malformed PUT response, APPLY_FAIL remains 0. The verification GET at line 336 is a fresh, independent API call that reads actual server state regardless of what the PUT response body contained. Any deviation from intended state (e.g., GitHub accepted the request but applied it inconsistently) would be caught by the four verification checks at lines 344\\u2013347. The trade-off \\u2014 relying on verification rather than PUT response parsing \\u2014 is acceptable and matches standard GitHub API client practice. No finding.\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":2,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"020D2 POST-after-PUT lacks APPLY_FAIL trap\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"FINDING raised as SAF-R3-003 (MIN). Asymmetry confirmed: 020G has explicit diagnostic and fall-through on POST failure; 020D2 has bare set -e abort. Idempotency is correct (204 No Content on re-run confirmed). Acceptable for bus-factor-1 SCP-self-only scope but diagnostic quality should match 020G.\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":3,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"Case-statement *..*  over-reject of legitimate repo names\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"NON-FINDING. GitHub's repository naming rules explicitly prohibit consecutive dots (`..`) in repository names. GitHub organization/user names additionally prohibit all dot characters (only `[a-zA-Z0-9-]` are allowed). The `*..*` pattern in the case statement at lines 213\\u2013217 therefore cannot reject any name that GitHub itself would accept. The guard is correctly scoped.\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":4,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"Fix-round-2 shortening of ack-flag friction\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"NON-FINDING. Fix-round-2 CORR2-003 only added the error-on-ack-flag-without-trigger case (lines 181\\u2013185). The existing friction path \\u2014 long flag name, 5-second sleep, verbose stderr warning block (lines 198\\u2013206) \\u2014 is completely unchanged. No friction reduction introduced.\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":5,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"PUT payload REQUIRED_CONTEXT markdown injection in log block\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"PARTIAL FINDING raised as SAF-R3-002 (MIN). The JSON fenced block is safe: jq `--arg` encodes REQUIRED_CONTEXT as a JSON string, serializing newlines as `\\\\\\\\n` escapes and leaving backticks as quoted string content, which python3 json.tool preserves on a single line. The ``` fence cannot be closed by jq-encoded content. However, the bare `${REQUIRED_CONTEXT}` interpolation in the 'Required check' markdown inline code span (heredoc, not the JSON block) is vulnerable to backtick and newline injection. Log integrity issue, not a security boundary.\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":6,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"020D2 POST idempotency on already-enabled required_signatures\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"NON-FINDING. GitHub's REST API returns HTTP 204 No Content when POST /protection/required_signatures is called on a branch where required_signatures is already enabled. The gh CLI treats any 2xx response as success; `>/dev/null` suppresses the empty body. Re-running configure-020d2-required-check.sh on a repo where required_signatures is already enabled is idempotent.\\\"}],\\\"self_reported_concerns\\\":[],\\\"summary\\\":\\\"One new MAJ regression introduced by fix-round-2 (SAF-R3-001): the `FAIL=0` initialization at line 343 that was added to enable the SAF-R2-002 conditional 'verification passed' gate now unconditionally clobbers the `FAIL=1` set by the SAF-R2-001 apply-phase trap. The two fixes in combination create a false-success window: PUT or POST failure \\u2192 FAIL=1 (SAF-R2-001); FAIL=0 reset (line 343); verify checks pass on pre-existing state \\u2192 FAIL stays 0; exit 0 with 'verification passed \\u2713'. The SAF-R2-001 intent is inverted. Fix: change `FAIL=0` at line 343 to `[ \\\\\\\"${APPLY_FAIL:-0}\\\\\\\" -ne 0 ] || FAIL=0`. Two MIN findings: REQUIRED_CONTEXT has no validation and can corrupt the emitted markdown log entry via backtick or newline injection (SAF-R3-002; no API injection risk, log integrity only); configure-020d2-required-check.sh's new POST step lacks the diagnostic error message 020G emits for the same failure, producing a bare set-e abort with no operator guidance on partial state (SAF-R3-003; idempotent re-run is the repair, but asymmetric diagnostic quality). All six dispatch probes addressed: APPLY_FAIL partial-stdout (non-finding \\u2014 independent verify GET is the safety net), 020D2 POST asymmetry (SAF-R3-003 MIN), *..*  over-reject (non-finding \\u2014 GitHub forbids `..` in names), ack-flag friction (non-finding \\u2014 fix-round-2 only added the error case), PUT payload markdown injection (SAF-R3-002 MIN on bare ${REQUIRED_CONTEXT} interpolation; JSON block safe), 020D2 POST idempotency (non-finding \\u2014 GitHub returns 204 on re-run, gh treats 204 as success). Verdict: NEEDS_REVISION on SAF-R3-001. The SAF-R3-001 fix is a one-line change; SAF-R3-002 and SAF-R3-003 are straightforward additions. A fix-round-3 pass and R4 targeted re-verification is required before merge.\\\"}\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r3.json:80:  \"stdout_tail\": \" fails, `set -e` aborts with only the bare gh CLI stderr output \\u2014 no '[020D2] ERROR: PUT applied but signatures NOT set \\u2014 re-run to retry' context message. The FIX-ROUND-2.md CORR2-004 closure note claims the POST 'is itself idempotent' but does not address the diagnostic quality asymmetry.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh line 81: `gh api -X PUT ... >/dev/null` \\u2014 bare, no trap. Line 117: `gh api -X POST \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"repos/${REPO}/branches/${DEFAULT_BRANCH}/protection/required_signatures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >/dev/null` \\u2014 bare, no trap. With `set -euo pipefail` (line 28), a non-zero exit from line 117 aborts immediately. The o