onse .enabled field correctly referenced.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"sec_12_7_3_required_signatures\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection/required_signatures \\u2014 VALID sub-resource endpoint. Response .enabled field correctly referenced.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"sec_12_7_5_get_contexts\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection/required_status_checks --jq '.contexts' \\u2014 VALID.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"sec_12_7_5_patch_contexts\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"PATCH /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection/required_status_checks with body {strict:boolean, contexts:string[]} \\u2014 VALID. The contexts field is the correct parameter for this endpoint (legacy form; still supported by GitHub API). PATCH performs full array replacement as documented in the warning text.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"sec_12_7_2_renovate_ruleset\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/rulesets --jq '.[] | select(.name == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"scp-tag-protection-renovate-v\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") | .enforcement' \\u2014 VALID. /repos/{owner}/{repo}/rulesets returns an array of ruleset objects with name and enforcement fields.\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\"findings\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CORR-R3-MIN-001\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"MIN\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"SCP-E007 is emitted by policy-check.yml but absent from \\u00a712.7.7 error-code table, leaving the table incomplete relative to its stated scope.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"policy-check.yml line 528 (inside 'Validate rule-config against schema' step): print(f'::error file={rule_config_path},title=SCP-E007::rule-config schema validation failed: {exc}'). \\u00a712.7.7 table ends at SCP-E006. DISPATCH-NOTE acceptance row (vi) states 'codes verified emitted by .github/workflows/policy-check.yml and conflict-gate.yml' \\u2014 this claim is incomplete since SCP-E007 is a live emission site in policy-check.yml.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Adopters who submit a malformed .scp/rule-config.yaml (e.g. missing required justification field, invalid expires_at format) will see SCP-E007 in CI annotations with no documented interpretation or remediation guidance in ADOPT-001. The appendix's stated purpose of documenting 'what to expect when SCP cuts a new release' and error codes is partially unmet for the rule-config validation path.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Add SCP-E007 row to \\u00a712.7.7 table: SCP-E007 | Rule-config schema validation failure (.scp/rule-config.yaml does not conform to schemas/rule-config.schema.json \\u2014 missing required field, invalid expires_at format, or additionalProperties violation) | Fail-closed \\u2014 workflow exits non-zero before policy evaluation. This is a documentation-only edit requiring no workflow changes.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CORR-R3-nit-001\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"nit\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\u00a712.7.13 parenthetical references VERSIONING.md as if it is a current document when it is a forward-looking artefact that ships in 020H.1.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\u00a712.7.13 line 1032: 'mirroring the `020H.1` naming used for VERSIONING.md'. \\u00a712.7.11 explicitly states: 'The freshness-warning emit and the published version-manifest.json both land in slice 020H.1 (post-v1.0.0)'. VERSIONING.md does not exist at v1.0.0.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Minor reader confusion: an adopter who follows the VERSIONING.md reference at v1.0.0 will not find the document. Low-impact since the reference is informational context for the 020H.2 naming convention, not an operative instruction.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Rephrase to: 'mirroring the 020H.1 dot-N naming convention (020H.1 being the post-Threshold-A VERSIONING.md + versioning-policy slice)' \\u2014 removes the forward-reference to a not-yet-published document while preserving the naming-system explanation.\\\\\\\"}]}\\\",\\\"stop_reason\\\":\\\"end_turn\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"59bff45a-f882-4012-9469-8a0c1fcfe65e\\\",\\\"total_cost_usd\\\":0.701796,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":6,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":79492,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":115000,\\\"output_tokens\\\":24471,\\\"server_tool_use\\\":{\\\"web_search_requests\\\":0,\\\"web_fetch_requests\\\":0},\\\"service_tier\\\":\\\"standard\\\",\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":79492,\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0},\\\"inference_geo\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"iterations\\\":[{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1,\\\"output_tokens\\\":23489,\\\"cache_read_input_tokens\\\":60699,\\\"cache_creation_input_tokens\\\":26664,\\\"cache_creation\\\":{\\\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\\\":26664},\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\"}],\\\"speed\\\":\\\"standard\\\"},\\\"modelUsage\\\":{\\\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":1993,\\\"outputTokens\\\":25,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.002118,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000},\\\"claude-sonnet-4-6\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":6,\\\"outputTokens\\\":24471,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":115000,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":79492,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.699678,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000}},\\\"permission_denials\\\":[],\\\"terminal_reason\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"fast_mode_state\\\":\\\"off\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"642ea989-fa00-4b30-903c-9672d5481842\\\"}\\n\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-correctness-r3.json:127:      \"5c_section_references\": \"PASS \\u2014 All \\u00a712.7.N cross-references resolve to existing sub-sections. \\u00a712.7.1 \\u2192 \\u00a712.7.13: present. \\u00a712.7.4 \\u2192 \\u00a711.10: present. \\u00a712.7.4 \\u2192 \\u00a712.7.7: present. \\u00a712.7.9 \\u2192 \\u00a712.7.13: present. Reference block D-022/D-029/D-030/D-031/D-032/D-033/D-034/D-035 all present in DECISIONS.md. scripts/verify-bypass-pairing.sh referenced in \\u00a712.7.4 exists at repo HEAD. scripts/enable-required-check.sh referenced in \\u00a712.7.3 exists at repo HEAD.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-package.json:4:  \"instruction\": \"Adversarial review of WP-SCP-022 slice 020G (enable-required-check.sh adopter helper) at safety/bypass lens. The script's blast radius is significant — it modifies adopter-repo branch protection. Hunt for ways the script could be misused or weakened. SPECIFIC ATTACK SURFACES TO PROBE: (1) Argument injection: the --required-check, --repo, --branch flags accept arbitrary strings. Could a tainted value (e.g. shell metachars in --branch) lead to command injection? Verify all user-supplied strings are passed via jq --arg (data-typed) or quoted positional substitution. (2) Plan-only escape hatch: --plan exits before the PUT call. Does it also exit before the before-state capture (which makes a GET call)? Yes — verify. (3) --no-enforce-admins escape hatch: emits a stderr warning but still proceeds. The flag is documented as 'NOT recommended; emits a warning'. Is the warning loud enough? Should the script require an explicit --i-understand-this-is-not-canonical confirmation flag? (4) PAT scope: the script doesn't introspect token scope. If the operator runs with a token that lacks administration:write, the PUT will fail with HTTP 403, but the FAILED apply might leave the adopter's branch in a partial state (some fields set, others not — actually no, GitHub's PUT is atomic). Verify the failure mode is clean. (5) Script-SHA capture: `git log -1 --format=%H -- scripts/enable-required-check.sh`. If the script is run from a clone where the file has uncommitted changes, the SHA reflects the last committed version, NOT the version actually run. This is a logging integrity issue. Recommend a check: warn if git status reports uncommitted changes to the script. (6) Operator capture: `gh api user --jq '.login'`. If gh's auth context differs from the operator's GitHub identity (e.g., a service-account PAT), the log records the wrong identity. Acceptable? Document? (7) Markdown injection in log block: the operator pastes an emitted markdown block. If the adopter repo's name contains markdown metacharacters (e.g. underscores in 'repo_with_underscores'), would they be misinterpreted in the log? Verify. (8) The invocation-log workflow says 'commit on a feature branch, open PR, merge'. The merge of the log commit goes through the SCP gate, which means the log entry needs to pass scp/policy-check. Verify there's nothing in a typical log entry that would trigger a deny (e.g., the `before` JSON field showing waivers.json contents). (9) The script applies branch protection to a single named branch (--branch). Could an operator accidentally protect the wrong branch by typo'ing --branch? The --plan flag mitigates but doesn't prevent. Acceptable? (10) The script's PUT payload sets required_pull_request_reviews:null. This OVERRIDES whatever the adopter currently has — including any review-enforcement they had. Is this acceptable? Should the script preserve existing review-enforcement, or make this an explicit decision via flag? Any bypass surface, weakening, or unintended consequence is a finding. Per WP-SCP-022 §4.3. Return SonnetReviewResult JSON.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-package.json:6:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-correctness-r2.json:32:    \"result\": \"I now have all the evidence needed. Producing the R2 correctness review JSON.\\n\\n{\\\"review_id\\\":\\\"COR2-020H3-2026-04-30\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"correctness\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"020H part 3\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"R2 correctness review of ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.1\\u2013\\u00a712.7.13 post fix-round-1 (commit 6907a8b). All five R1 correctness fixes (CORR-MIN-001 through CORR-MIN-004 + CORR-nit-001) are confirmed present and accurate in the current document. \\u00a712.7.13 supply-chain disclosure cross-checks out: OPA and Conftest SHA256 verification is accurately described (lockfile fields read at lines 200\\u2013202 of policy-check.yml, verify_sha256 called at lines 215/229/267); Regal SHA256 gap is accurately described (version hardcoded at 0.40.0 on line 177; curl + smoke-test only at lines 273\\u2013275; no verify_sha256 call; no regal entry in scp-policy-check.lock). \\u00a712.7.4 D-033 corrective language is present (count=0 + codeowner_reviews=false); verify-bypass-pairing.sh has no approving-review check (confirmed: the script only gates on DECISIONS.md diff, waivers.json diff, D-NNN row, and waiver entry expiry). \\u00a712.7.2 ruleset name matches configure-020f-renovate-tag-protection.sh default. \\u00a712.7.5 scp-federation label is accurate (preset packageRule labels include scp-federation). \\u00a712.7.10 secrets-block claim is accurate (policy-check.yml workflow_call block contains only inputs:; no secrets: declared). \\u00a712.7.9 git syntax is valid. \\u00a712.7.11 releases URL format is correct. Four new findings at MIN/nit level: (1) DISPATCH-NOTE acceptance checklist row (xi) retains the pre-fix ~2,400 figure after CORR-MIN-004 was applied; (2) require_review_from_non_author is not a documented field in GitHub's legacy protected-branches REST API; (3) '020H.2' as the TF-020H3-001 closure target is ambiguous against the established '020H part N' naming convention; (4) the 'expired-rule-config grace ramp' claim in \\u00a712.7.4 cites plan \\u00a74 020C.1(v) whose text addresses deprecated-rule removal warnings, not expired disable-entry handling \\u2014 TF-005 (which the 020C.1 tracked-forward index attributes this to) was deferred and its v1.0.0 implementation status is unconfirmed by the available files. None of these findings block approval.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MIN-R2-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md acceptance checklist row (xi) still reads '~2,400 PR runs/month headroom on GitHub Free with 2,000-minute monthly budget' after CORR-MIN-004 was applied to ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.12, creating an internal contradiction within the same document.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md lines 65\\u201368: '- [x] **(xi)** \\u00a712.7.12 \\u2014 Actions-billing note; ~30s warm-start per PR run; cold-start adds 10\\u201315s for binary download + SHA verification; ~2,400 PR runs/month headroom on GitHub Free with 2,000-minute monthly budget.' ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.12 (post-fix) correctly reads: 'GitHub bills Actions in whole minutes per job, rounded up \\u2014 a ~30\\u201345s job counts as 1 billed minute ... should therefore expect ~2,000 PR runs/month before the budget caps.' The fix-round-1 closure table at DISPATCH-NOTE.md line 138 records CORR-MIN-004 as '\\u00a712.7.12 corrected \\u2014 GitHub bills whole minutes per job', confirming the substantive doc was updated. The acceptance checklist (lines 25\\u201368) was not simultaneously updated and now contradicts both the closure table and the document it describes.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A future reviewer auditing the DISPATCH-NOTE acceptance checklist against \\u00a712.7.12 finds ~2,400 in the checklist and ~2,000 in the document and must determine independently which is correct and why they differ. The contradicting value could also be picked up by automated checks that parse the dispatch note's billing claim. No adopter behavior is affected since the authoritative text is in ADOPT-001, not the dispatch note.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Update DISPATCH-NOTE.md line 67 from '~2,400 PR runs/month headroom on GitHub Free with 2,000-minute monthly budget' to '~2,000 PR runs/month on GitHub Free (2,000-minute budget \\u00f7 1 billed min/run)' to match the corrected ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.12 and the closure table entry.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MIN-R2-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4 multi-maintainer configuration lists 'require_review_from_non_author: true' as a branch-protection parameter, but this is not a documented field in GitHub's legacy protected-branches REST API (/repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection). The API's required_pull_request_reviews object exposes required_approving_review_count, require_code_owner_reviews, dismiss_stale_reviews, and require_last_push_approval \\u2014 not require_review_from_non_author.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4 text (ADOPT-001 lines 881): 'Configure branch protection with required_approving_review_count >= 1, require_code_owner_reviews: true, and require_review_from_non_author: true.' The same term appears in plan \\u00a74 020B(viii-c): 'the caller's main branch protection must have required_approving_review_count >= 1 + dismiss_stale_reviews: true + require_review_from_non_author: true'; in 020K: 'branch-protection require_review_from_non_author=false at 020D2'; and in D-033: 'flip count=1 + codeowner=true + non-author=true'. GitHub's branch protection API for required_pull_request_reviews does not include a require_review_from_non_author field; GitHub prevents PR self-approval at the platform level implicitly regardless of this configuration. The term does not appear in configure-020f-renovate-tag-protection.sh, and enable-required-check.sh was not available for direct verification in this review pass.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Multi-maintainer adopters following \\u00a712.7.4 literally and constructing their branch-protection PUT payload via the API will find no require_review_from_non_author field to set. The underlying security behavior (non-self-approval) is platform-enforced regardless, so no security regression results. However, the documentation describes a configuration step that cannot be carried out as written, eroding adopter confidence and potentially causing support questions. Note: the same terminology is used consistently throughout the WP-SCP-020 plan and all prior decision records \\u2014 this appears to be accepted shorthand in the plan corpus, not an error introduced by this slice \\u2014 but the adopter-facing \\u00a712.7.4 is the external contract document and should use accurate API terms.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace 'require_review_from_non_author: true' in \\u00a712.7.4 with: 'Note: GitHub prevents PR authors from approving their own PRs at the platform level; there is no explicit API field required for this \\u2014 configure required_approving_review_count >= 1 and require_code_owner_reviews: true, and GitHub enforces the non-self-approval constraint automatically.' If the term is intentionally carried over from the plan for consistency, add a parenthetical: '(require_review_from_non_author: true \\u2014 platform-enforced; not a REST API field but used as shorthand in the WP-SCP-020 plan corpus)'.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MIN-R2-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"TF-020H3-001 closure path references 'slice 020H.2' but the established WP-SCP-022 slice naming convention is '020H part N' (three existing slices: 020H part 1, 020H part 2, 020H part 3). '020H.2' is ambiguous: it parses naturally as '020H part 2', which has already merged per WP-SCP-020 \\u00a73 sequence. Neither DISPATCH-NOTE.md nor \\u00a712.7.13 explains that '020H.2' designates a new fourth slice in the sub-series.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md lines 157\\u2013168: 'Closure path: open slice 020H.2 as a workflow-change PR...' ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.13: 'lockfile + tool-versions parity with OPA + Conftest is tracked as TF-020H3-001 \\u2192 slice 020H.2'. WP-SCP-020 \\u00a73 sequence (plan lines 82\\u2013101) uses 'part N' notation throughout: '020H part 1 Cut v1.0.0-rc.1', '020H part 2 Promote v1.0.0-rc.1 \\u2192 v1.0.0', '020H part 3 ADOPT-001 \\u00a712 appendix published', '020H.1 policies/VERSIONING.md + rule-RFC process + rollback detection'. The plan itself uses '020H.1' (with a period) for the post-Threshold-A slice, and '020H part N' for the pre-Threshold-A sub-slices. DISPATCH-NOTE.md TF-D1-001..003 items use 'closed in 020H part 3' (lines 74\\u201383). No definition of '020H.2' appears in the plan, STATUS.md reference, or this DISPATCH-NOTE.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A contributor or automated STATUS.md parser reading 'TF-020H3-001 \\u2192 020H.2' will look for slice '020H part 2' in the STATUS.md backlog table and find it already marked closed \\u2014 preventing them from correctly identifying the intended new slice and deferring closure tracking to the next session. If STATUS.md uses dotted notation (020H.1 for the post-Threshold-A slice), '020H.2' may intend a second post-Threshold-A sub-slice, but this interpretation is not stated.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"In both DISPATCH-NOTE.md (lines 161, 167) and ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.13, replace '020H.2' with an unambiguous designator: either '020H part 4' (next in the pre-Threshold-A 'part N' series, consistent with naming of current and prior slices) or '020H.2 (a new workflow-change slice, not 020H part 2)' with an explanatory parenthetical. Align with STATUS.md's post-Threshold-A backlog naming convention when opening the slice.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MIN-R2-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.4 describes 'the expired-rule-config grace ramp (one-release ::warning:: window before suppression refusal at release-tag time)' as an existing v1.0.0 safety mechanism, but plan \\u00a74 020C.1(v) \\u2014 the cited source \\u2014 reads 'Deprecated rules emit one-release warning before removal', which refers to SCP removing a rule from its own library, not to an adopter's disable entry in rule-config reaching its expires_at date. TF-005 (which the project_020c1_tracked_forward memory attributes to the rule-config expiry grace ramp) was deferred. No expired-entry handling was found in lib/policy_check_invocation.sh lines 85\\u2013134 reviewed in this pass.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"ADOPT-001 \\u00a712.7.4 (line 889): 'The expired-rule-config grace ramp (one-release ::warning:: window before suppression refusal at release-tag time) is the safety-valve, not a substitute for CODEOWNERS coverage.' Plan \\u00a74 020C.1(v): 'Caller-side .scp/rule-config.yaml override with rule-level disable: <SCP-R-NNN> + justification: string + expires_at: date. Deprecated rules emit one-release warning before removal.' The 'Deprecated rules' sentence is in the context of SCP's own rule library lifecycle (a rule being deprecated by SCP), not in the context of adopter config expires_at semantics. project_020c1_tracked_forward memory: 'TF-005..TF-008 deferred to 020D2 / WP-SCP-023 / opportunistic / v1.1'. lib/policy_check_invocation.sh lines 85\\u2013134 show SCP-E002 emit paths for missing/wrong-type rule-config but no expired-entry grace-ramp logic. If expired entries are silently honored indefinitely at v1.0.0 (no suppression refusal), the described grace ramp does not exist yet and the \\u00a712.7.4 sentence misleads adopters about a present safety mechanism.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An adopter who lets their disable entry expire believing 'there is a one-release warning before suppression refusal' will be surprised if the workflow either (a) silently continues to disable the rule indefinitely (no warning, no refusal) or (b) immediately refuses to run without any prior warning. The mitigation guidance 'treat .scp/rule-config.yaml as a bypass-surface equivalent' and 'CODEOWNERS-protect it' is correct and valuable regardless \\u2014 the misleading part is attributing an unimplemented safety valve to the grace ramp. No adopter is immediately blocked, but the documentation creates false confidence in a behavioral guarantee that may not be live.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace 'the expired-rule-config grace ramp (one-release ::warning:: window before suppression refusal at release-tag time) is the safety-valve' with: 'Note: a grace-ramp behavior for expired disable entries (::warning:: window before suppression refusal) is tracked as a post-v1.0.0 capability (TF-005); at v1.0.0, an expired disable entry may be silently honored or immediately refused depending on implementation state \\u2014 do not rely on the grace ramp as a safety valve at this version. CODEOWNERS protection is the correct control.' Then verify TF-005 status and update once the grace ramp lands.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-nit-R2-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.3 states that administration:write 'also enables webhook creation, environment management, repository-transfer initiation, and archival.' Repository environment management is governed by a separate fine-grained PAT permission (environments: write) in GitHub's model, not by administration:write.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"\\u00a712.7.3 (ADOPT-001 line 865): 'administration:write covers more than branch-protection settings \\u2014 it also enables webhook creation, environment management, repository-transfer initiation, and archival.' GitHub's fine-grained PAT repository permissions include a distinct 'Environments' permission (read / read-and-write) separate from 'Administration'. Repository webhooks are typically under 'Webhooks' as a distinct permission at the repository level, though some sources include them under Administration. Transfer and archival are standard Administration capabilities. The 'environment management' claim is the most likely inaccuracy: GitHub environments (for deployment secrets/protection rules) use the environments permission, not administration.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Adopters issuing a single-use administration:write PAT per \\u00a712.7.3 guidance for branch-protection configuration will not need environments access for the enable-required-check.sh operation (the script applies branch protection only). The practical risk is zero for the script's stated purpose. However, if an adopter reads this as a full inventory of what their one-time PAT grants and later uses it expecting environments access, they may be surprised when it lacks that capability. The inaccuracy slightly overstates the blast radius of the PAT, which works in the security-conservative direction.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace 'environment management' with a more accurate term or caveat: 'administration:write covers repository-level branch protection, some webhook operations, repository-transfer initiation, and archival \\u2014 note that repository environments (deployment secrets/protection rules) require a separate environments: write permission and are NOT included in administration:write.'\\\"}],\\\"criteria_verification\\\":{\\\"r1_fix_corr_min_001\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 \\u00a712.7.7 SCP-E002 description now reads 'Policy-bundle or invocation pre-condition failure \\u2014 policy directory missing/wrong type, changed-files manifest absent, waivers/rule-config data preparation error, manifest-file checkout missing, or Conftest invocation failure (Rego compilation, missing helper, etc.)'. This covers all emission paths identifiable in lib/policy_check_invocation.sh (rule-config not-a-file check line 97, policy-dir not-a-directory check line 102, waivers/rule-config data wrap errors, manifest-file checkout missing, Conftest invocation failure). R1 fix confirmed present.\\\",\\\"r1_fix_corr_min_002\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 \\u00a712.7.7 SCP-E006 failure-mode column now reads 'Non-blocking \\u2014 step exits 0 and merge proceeds. Note: GitHub renders the ::error:: annotation as a red X icon in the PR Files-Changed tab; this is observability noise, not a hard failure.' R1 fix confirmed present.\\\",\\\"r1_fix_corr_min_003\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 \\u00a712.7.1 YAML block statuses: write comment reads '# required for scp/policy-check-readback per D-029 / 020C.1(vi)'. Matches self-dogfood wrapper. R1 fix confirmed present.\\\",\\\"r1_fix_corr_min_004\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 \\u00a712.7.12 reads '~2,000 PR runs/month' with correct derivation: 'GitHub bills Actions in whole minutes per job, rounded up \\u2014 a ~30\\u201345s job counts as 1 billed minute.' R1 fix confirmed present in ADOPT-001. (DISPATCH-NOTE acceptance checklist not updated \\u2014 CORR-MIN-R2-001.)\\\",\\\"r1_fix_corr_nit_001\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 \\u00a712.7.1 YAML comment reads 'Closes WP-SCP-020 020D1 R1 review TF-D1-002.' No trailing ')'. R1 fix confirmed present.\\\",\\\"supply_chain_opa_conftest\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 scp-policy-check.lock contains sha256 fields for OPA on all four platforms (linux-x64/arm64, darwin-arm64/x64) and sha256 + archive_sha256 for Conftest on all four platforms. policy-check.yml lines 200\\u2013202 read OPA_SHA256, CONFTST_BINARY_SHA256, CONFTST_ARCHIVE_SHA256 from lockfile. verify_sha256 is called for OPA at resolve_opa lines 215/219, for Conftest archive at resolve_conftest lines 229/233, and for extracted Conftest binary at line 267. \\u00a712.7.13 claims accurate.\\\",\\\"supply_chain_regal_gap\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 policy-check.yml line 177 hardcodes REGAL_VERSION='0.40.0' (matches \\u00a712.7.13 claim). Lines 273\\u2013275: curl download + chmod + smoke-test only; no verify_sha256 call for Regal. scp-policy-check.lock has no regal entry. \\u00a712.7.13 gap disclosure is accurate.\\\",\\\"safe_003_two_mode_consistency\\\":\\\"PARTIAL PASS with CORR-MIN-R2-002 \\u2014 Logical internal consistency of two-mode description is PASS: multi-maintainer (count >= 1 + CODEOWNER + machine-enforced Gate 1) and single-operator (count = 0 + documentation-only Gate 1) are internally coherent and mutually exclusive. D-033 ACCEPTED text contains the corrective language for count=0 ('Updated to count=0 + codeowner_reviews=false in single-operator mode'). verify-bypass-pairing.sh confirmed: no approving-review check anywhere in 109-line script; only checks DECISIONS.md diff, waivers.json diff, D-NNN row, waiver expiry. Partial flag: require_review_from_non_author is not a recognized GitHub API field (CORR-MIN-R2-002).\\\",\\\"safe_009_rule_config_bypass\\\":\\\"PARTIAL PASS with CORR-MIN-R2-004 \\u2014 The rule-config-as-bypass-surface description is accurate: .scp/rule-config.yaml disable entries suppress rule evaluation without triggering verify-bypass-pairing.sh; only SCP-E006 observability annotation is emitted. SCP-E006 step exits 0 (policy-check.yml confirmed). CODEOWNERS recommendation is correct. Grace ramp claim is unverified against implementation (CORR-MIN-R2-004).\\\",\\\"safe_004_step_0_label\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 renovate/default.json packageRule for jrnb2024/standards-control-plane has labels ['dependencies', 'scp-federation', 'scp-bump']; root preset also has labels ['dependencies', 'scp-federation']. The scp-federation label IS present on federation-primitive bump PRs. \\u00a712.7.5 step 0 label claim is accurate.\\\",\\\"d034_ruleset_name\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 configure-020f-renovate-tag-protection.sh line 28: RULESET_NAME='${SCP_RENOVATE_RULESET_NAME:-scp-tag-protection-renovate-v}'. \\u00a712.7.2 verification command uses 'select(.name == \\\\\\\"scp-tag-protection-renovate-v\\\\\\\")'. Names match.\\\",\\\"pat_scope_claims\\\":\\\"PARTIAL PASS with CORR-nit-R2-001 \\u2014 webhook creation, repository-transfer initiation, archival are generally covered by administration:write. 'environment management' likely requires a separate environments permission (CORR-nit-R2-001).\\\",\\\"clone_pinning_syntax\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 'git -C <path> fetch' and 'git -C <path> checkout <SHA>' are valid git syntax. The suggestion to extend the hook to read the SHA from .github/workflows/policy-check.yml is workable: the wrapper file contains the line 'uses: jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/.github/workflows/policy-check.yml@<SHA>' parseable with grep -oP '(?<=@)[0-9a-f]{40}'.\\\",\\\"secrets_inherit_workflow_sig\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 policy-check.yml on: workflow_call: block (lines 1\\u201344) contains only inputs: with seven input definitions. No secrets: block declared. \\u00a712.7.10 claim 'The SCP reusable workflow does not declare any secrets:' is accurate against the live workflow.\\\",\\\"releases_url\\\":\\\"PASS \\u2014 \\u00a712.7.11 manual fallback URL 'https://github.com/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/releases' matches the repo name pattern used throughout \\u00a712.7 (trailing dash confirmed). Standard GitHub releases URL format.\\\",\\\"tf_020h3_001_naming\\\":\\\"FAIL \\u2014 see CORR-MIN-R2-003. '020H.2' is ambiguous against the established '020H part N' convention; '020H part 2' has already merged per WP-SCP-020 \\u00a73 sequence. DISPATCH-NOTE.md and \\u00a712.7.13 both use the ambiguous notation without clarification.\\\",\\\"dispatch_note_closure_table\\\":\\\"PARTIAL PASS with CORR-MIN-R2-001 \\u2014 Fix-round-1 closure table (DISPATCH-NOTE.md lines 134\\u2013153): all 17 finding rows match the actual ADOPT-001 diff in terms of what was changed. SAFE-003 closure description matches two-mode \\u00a712.7.4 rewrite. SAFE-009 closure matches final paragraph addition. SAFE-011 tracked-forward description matches \\u00a712.7.13 content. CORR-MIN-004 closure correctly records the billing math correction. The acceptance checklist row (xi) at lines 65\\u201368 was not updated after CORR-MIN-004 fix (CORR-MIN-R2-001).\\\"}}\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-correctness-r2.json:114:        \"evidence\": \"\\u00a712.7.4 text (ADOPT-001 lines 881): 'Configure branch protection with required_approving_review_count >= 1, require_code_owner_reviews: true, and require_review_from_non_author: true.' The same term appears in plan \\u00a74 020B(viii-c): 'the caller's main branch protection must have required_approving_review_count >= 1 + dismiss_stale_reviews: true + require_review_from_non_author: true'; in 020K: 'branch-protection require_review_from_non_author=false at 020D2'; and in D-033: 'flip count=1 + codeowner=true + non-author=true'. GitHub's branch protection API for required_pull_request_reviews does not include a require_review_from_non_author field; GitHub prevents PR self-approval at the platform level implicitly regardless of this configuration. The term does not appear in configure-020f-renovate-tag-protection.sh, and enable-required-check.sh was not available for direct verification in this review pass.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/review-correctness-r2.json:140:        \"impact\": \"Adopters issuing a single-use administration:write PAT per \\u00a712.7.3 guidance for branch-protection configuration will not need environments access for the enable-required-check.sh operation (the script applies branch protection only). The practical risk is zero for the script's stated purpose. However, if an adopter reads this as a full inventory of what their one-time PAT grants and later uses it expecting environments access, they may be surprised when it lacks that capability. The inaccuracy slightly overstates the blast radius of the PAT, which works in the security-conservative direction.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r2.json:32:    \"result\": \"{\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"R2-SAFETY\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"020G\\\",\\\"work_package\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-022\\\",\\\"round\\\":2,\\\"lens\\\":\\\"safety_bypass\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"NEEDS_REVISION\\\",\\\"r1_closure_verification\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-001\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"SHA256 of the executing file's content captured via `shasum -a 256 \\\\\\\"$SCRIPT_PATH\\\\\\\"` (lines 331-332). Correctly reflects the running artefact regardless of uncommitted changes. Git SHA retained as secondary identifier with explicit `not-in-git-clone` fallback. New residual surface for macOS raised as SAF-R2-003.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-002\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"EXISTING_REVIEWS extracted from BEFORE_JSON via `jq '.required_pull_request_reviews // null'` (line 266) and spliced into PUT payload through `build_payload` (lines 224-246, `--argjson reviews`). Verified: when BEFORE_JSON is the no-protection-yet fallback `{\\\\\\\"_note\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"no protection currently configured\\\\\\\"}`, jq `.required_pull_request_reviews // null` yields `null`; build_payload emits `\\\\\\\"required_pull_request_reviews\\\\\\\": null`, which is the correct no-reviews posture and does not destroy a pre-existing review shape. Probe (4) from dispatch note confirmed correct.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-003\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"ACK_ADMIN_BYPASS=1 is required alongside --no-enforce-admins (lines 174-188). Without it, script exits 2 with explanation. With it, a visible stderr warning block plus `sleep 5` interposes. Residual SIGINT-inherited-ignore concern raised as SAF-R2-004.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-004\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"Plan mode (lines 270-278) prints both BEFORE_JSON and PAYLOAD via `jq .` before exiting. Operator can inspect full current-vs-intended diff.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-005\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"Addressed via SAF-006 path-traversal regex on REPO and BRANCH. REQUIRED_CONTEXT is passed to `jq --arg` (shell-safe) and rendered inside markdown backticks in the heredoc (no shell execution path). Heredoc delimiters are single-quoted (`<<'EOF'` inner reference lines) only where needed; the outer cat block uses unquoted `<<EOF` but all interpolated values are either operator-supplied at identical trust level or come from `gh api` responses.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-006\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"REPO validated against `^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$` (line 193). BRANCH rejects empty, slash-containing, `..`-containing, and leading-dot values (lines 197-199). Probe (7) re: dotted owner names (`actions.github.io`): the regex allows dots in both owner and name segments and `gh` CLI handles dotted names in URL paths without interpretation, so no path-confusion surface. Confirmed non-finding.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-007\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"ACKNOWLEDGED-DEFERRED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"By design. `--plan` is the documented pre-apply confirmation step. No interactive prompt added. The dispatch note and FIX-ROUND-1.md both record the deliberate deferral. Accepted.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-008\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"ACKNOWLEDGED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"Service-account identity documented in bootstrap-only header (lines 38-43); fine-grained single-repo PAT recommendation present. Log records gh API identity (`gh api user`) alongside script SHA256 + git SHA to close any ambiguity. Accepted as process control.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-010\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"PARTIALLY-CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"The post-verify FAIL=1 path falls through to log emission and exits non-zero (lines 306-313, 383-385). However, apply-phase `set -e` aborts \\u2014 specifically when `gh api -X POST .../required_signatures` returns non-zero (line 288) \\u2014 bypass the log-emission block entirely. SAF-010 fix covers only the verify-failure path; the apply-failure path is unprotected. Residual gap raised as SAF-R2-001 (MAJ).\\\"}],\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"A non-zero exit from the required_signatures POST (e.g. HTTP 403 \\u2014 PAT lacks administration:write on the signatures sub-resource, or the branch does not yet have base protection) causes `set -e` to abort the script before verification and log emission, leaving the adopter repo in a partial protection state with no audit trail.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 50: `set -euo pipefail`. Line 288: `gh api -X POST \\\\\\\"repos/${REPO}/branches/${BRANCH}/protection/required_signatures\\\\\\\" >/dev/null` \\u2014 no error handler, no `|| true`, no captured exit code. If this POST exits non-zero, bash aborts immediately. The verify block (lines 293-304) and log-emission block (cat <<EOF, lines 349-378) are never reached. The SAF-010 fix that falls through to log emission (lines 306-313) is only reachable after the POST succeeds; it handles verify-phase failures, not apply-phase failures. Scenario is realistic: the GitHub REST API treats `required_signatures` as a separate sub-resource; a fine-grained PAT scoped to `contents: read, administration: write` on the repo may still lack the `branches` administration scope required for the signatures endpoint. A 403 here is a plausible day-one operator error.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"The adopter repo has the unified PUT applied (status checks + enforce_admins) but required_signatures disabled \\u2014 a partial security posture that does not match the canonical SCP federation primitive defined in D-035. No markdown log entry is produced; the operator's only signal is a non-zero shell exit and gh CLI's stderr error text. WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020G(iii) mandates that every apply \\u2014 including failed applies \\u2014 is recorded in the invocation log. This invariant is violated. Recovery requires manual diagnosis, manual POST, and manual log entry.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace line 288 with an error-captured form: `gh api -X POST \\\\\\\"repos/${REPO}/branches/${BRANCH}/protection/required_signatures\\\\\\\" >/dev/null || { echo \\\\\\\"required_signatures POST failed (see above); branch is in partial state\\\\\\\" >&2; FAIL=1; }`. Remove the bare `set -e` exit path from this step by using the explicit `FAIL` sentinel. This matches the pattern already used in the verify block (lines 301-304) and allows the fall-through to log emission (lines 306-313) to fire for both verify-phase and apply-phase failures. The emitted log entry should include a `**Partial-apply:**` field distinguishing POST failure from verify failure.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"`log \\\\\\\"verification passed \\u2713\\\\\\\"` on line 315 is unconditional \\u2014 it executes whether FAIL is 0 or 1, emitting a false success message to stdout when verification has failed.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 300-318: FAIL is conditionally set to 1 on lines 301-304; the `if [ \\\\\\\"$FAIL\\\\\\\" -ne 0 ]` block (lines 306-313) writes failure context to stderr with `# fall through to log emission`; then line 315 unconditionally executes `log \\\\\\\"verification passed \\u2713\\\\\\\"` \\u2014 the `log` function (lines 201-203) writes to stdout. The intended control flow leaves no `else` branch before the success message. In a terminal, an operator sees both `verify FAIL:` on stderr and `[020G] verification passed \\u2713` on stdout, interleaved at flush time. In a stdout-only log-scraping scenario (`./script > out.txt 2>/dev/null`) or in a CI wrapper that separates streams, `out.txt` records a clean `[020G] verification passed \\u2713` with no indication of failure.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Misleading terminal output undermines the operator's confidence in the verification signal. Any monitoring or log-aggregation path that observes only stdout (grep on CI job log output, log-scraping dashboards, shell pipelines) receives a false PASS. Weakens the SAF-010 fix's stated intent to produce a clear audit record of failures. The markdown log entry itself does not explicitly label outcome, so an operator copy-pasting the block into branch-protection-log.md loses the failure context.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Gate the success message on FAIL: `if [ \\\\\\\"$FAIL\\\\\\\" -eq 0 ]; then log \\\\\\\"verification passed \\u2713\\\\\\\"; else log \\\\\\\"verification FAILED \\u2014 see stderr for details\\\\\\\"; fi`. Lines 316-318 (the detailed value lines) can remain unconditional \\u2014 they provide informational value in both success and failure paths.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"On stock macOS (the primary operator platform for this bootstrap script), `readlink -f` is not available without GNU coreutils; the fallback to bare `$0` makes the SHA256 self-hash unreliable when the script is invoked via a PATH-resolved bare name.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 331: `SCRIPT_PATH=\\\\\\\"$(readlink -f \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\"`. macOS ships BSD readlink which does not accept `-f`; it exits non-zero, and `2>/dev/null` suppresses the error, causing the fallback `printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\"` to execute. When the script is installed on PATH and invoked as `enable-required-check.sh` (bare name), `$0` is `enable-required-check.sh` with no directory component. `shasum -a 256 \\\\\\\"enable-required-check.sh\\\\\\\"` then searches the current working directory \\u2014 not the script's actual location. If a file named `enable-required-check.sh` exists in CWD (possibly an older version, a modified copy, or a symlink), its hash is recorded in the audit log. If no such file exists in CWD, shasum exits non-zero and `set -e` aborts the script after mutations have already been applied (though the mutation-then-abort sequence is the same as SAF-R2-001).\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"The audit log records a hash that may not correspond to the script that actually executed. The SAF-001 closure intended SHA256 to be the primary reliable identifier that supersedes the potentially-stale git SHA; the macOS readlink edge case degrades it to best-effort. An operator who runs the script by its bare name from a repo root that contains a different version in the current directory will record the wrong artefact hash.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace `readlink -f \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\"` with the portable shell idiom: `SCRIPT_PATH=\\\\\\\"$(cd \\\\\\\"$(dirname \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\" && pwd -P)/$(basename \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\"`. This resolves one level of symlinks, handles relative paths, and works on macOS without GNU coreutils. Alternatively: `command -v greadlink >/dev/null 2>&1 && SCRIPT_PATH=\\\\\\\"$(greadlink -f \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\" || SCRIPT_PATH=\\\\\\\"$(cd \\\\\\\"$(dirname \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\" && pwd -P)/$(basename \\\\\\\"$0\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\"`.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"If the script is called from a context where SIGINT disposition is SIG_IGN (inherited from the parent process), the `sleep 5` interposition on the --no-enforce-admins bypass path cannot be interrupted by Ctrl-C, eliminating the escape-hatch intent of the SAF-003 pause.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 185-187: `echo \\\\\\\"... 5-second pause to allow Ctrl-C...\\\\\\\" >&2; sleep 5`. POSIX specifies that when a process forks+execs a child, if a signal is SIG_IGN in the parent, it is SIG_IGN in the child unless the child explicitly resets it. If an outer shell or orchestration wrapper runs `trap '' INT` before invoking this script, both the bash process and its `sleep 5` child inherit SIGINT=SIG_IGN. Terminal Ctrl-C sends SIGINT to the foreground process group; if ignored at the process level, neither the sleep nor the script terminates. Realistic scenario: the script is wrapped inside a larger provisioning shell script that suppresses SIGINT to prevent mid-run interruption (`trap '' INT; ... ./enable-required-check.sh ...; trap - INT`).\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An operator in a shared-account or wrapped-invocation context who realises the bypass flag was autocompleted, pasted unintentionally, or included in a template command without review cannot abort during the 5-second window. The pause degrades from an interruptible safety checkpoint to a mandatory delay. The acknowledgement flag's deterrent value (probe (2)) is further reduced because the only operative gate \\u2014 the sleep \\u2014 is no longer an escape hatch.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add `trap - INT TERM` near the top of the script (before argument parsing) to reset SIGINT and SIGTERM to default in this process, regardless of the caller's signal dispositions. This is safe and standard practice for scripts that import interactive pause logic. Alternatively, in the sleep block specifically: `trap 'echo \\\\\\\"Aborted by Ctrl-C\\\\\\\" >&2; exit 130' INT; sleep 5; trap - INT`.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-005\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The CI bootstrap guard covers only `CI=true` and `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true`; CI systems that do not set these variables (Jenkins, Azure Pipelines, Bamboo) would pass the guard and allow the script to run in a non-attended automated context.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 54-57: `if [ \\\\\\\"${CI:-}\\\\\\\" = \\\\\\\"true\\\\\\\" ] || [ \\\\\\\"${GITHUB_ACTIONS:-}\\\\\\\" = \\\\\\\"true\\\\\\\" ]`. Jenkins sets `BUILD_NUMBER` and `JENKINS_URL` but does not set `CI=true` by default. Azure DevOps sets `TF_BUILD=True` (capital T) but not `CI=true`. Bamboo sets `bamboo.buildNumber` (env export style varies by version) but not `CI=true`. GitLab CI sets both `GITLAB_CI=true` and `CI=true`; CircleCI sets both `CIRCLECI=true` and `CI=true` \\u2014 those systems are covered. The comment on line 27 states \\\\\\\"the two standard CI env vars\\\\\\\" which is accurate for GitHub Actions but overstates coverage for the full CI landscape.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An operator at a Jenkins- or Azure-backed estate who accidentally includes this script in a pipeline step (e.g., as part of an onboarding automation) would not be blocked. The script would attempt to apply branch protection changes in a non-attended context against the operator's PAT credentials, potentially mass-mutating adopter repos if the pipeline is parameterised over a repo list. This is the bootstrap-only invariant's principal adversary.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add the common non-CI=true signals: `|| [ -n \\\\\\\"${BUILD_NUMBER:-}\\\\\\\" ] || [ \\\\\\\"${TF_BUILD:-}\\\\\\\" = \\\\\\\"True\\\\\\\" ] || [ -n \\\\\\\"${BAMBOO_BUILD_NUMBER:-}\\\\\\\" ]`. Or, given the guard is already documented as incomplete for edge cases, update the comment and the `--help` text to explicitly warn that the guard targets GitHub Actions + standard `CI=true` CI systems and does not cover Jenkins or Azure DevOps. The documentation update is lower-risk than adding guards that may have false positives on developer laptops with those env vars set.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-006\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"BEFORE_STATUS is populated only in the `||` error branch (line 251), then defaulted via `${BEFORE_STATUS:-0}` (line 252). A pre-existing non-zero `BEFORE_STATUS` in the caller's environment causes a successful `gh api` GET to be misclassified as a failure.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 251: `BEFORE_RAW=\\\\\\\"$(gh api -X GET \\\\\\\"repos/${REPO}/branches/${BRANCH}/protection\\\\\\\" 2>&1)\\\\\\\" || BEFORE_STATUS=$?`. Line 252: `BEFORE_STATUS=\\\\\\\"${BEFORE_STATUS:-0}\\\\\\\"`. If `BEFORE_STATUS=128` is exported in the environment before invocation and `gh api` succeeds (exit 0), the `||` branch does not execute, `BEFORE_STATUS` retains its inherited value of `128`, and the `:-0` default is not triggered because the variable is already set (set -u treats it as defined). Line 254 enters the failure branch; grep finds no \\\\\\\"Branch not protected\\\\\\\" pattern in valid protection JSON; script aborts with \\\\\\\"cannot proceed safely\\\\\\\" before any mutation.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Effect is a harmless abort \\u2014 no mutations occur, no security regression. In practice the variable name `BEFORE_STATUS` is uncommon enough that collision is unlikely. Noted as an encapsulation gap.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"`local BEFORE_STATUS=0` is not usable in bash outside functions; instead, either explicitly `unset BEFORE_STATUS` at line 251 before the assignment, or use a more unique variable name (`_020G_BEFORE_STATUS`) that is unlikely to collide with caller environment.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-007\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Shell history and tab-completion can pre-fill `--i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate` on any account with a prior bypass invocation, reducing the friction the deliberately long flag name is intended to impose.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"SAF-003 closure added the flag to make bypass intent explicit. In a shared shell account (or any account whose history includes a previous bypass invocation), Ctrl-R reverse-search or zsh/fish autocomplete surfaces the full prior command including the flag. The flag as written is correct by intent: it is friction, not a cryptographic gate. The operative gate remains the 5-second `sleep 5` and the visible warning block. Probe (2) from the dispatch note: realistic in multi-tenant shared-account scenarios; not realistic as a bypass of the sleep (which requires SIGINT handling, see SAF-R2-004).\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"The long flag's deterrent value is marginally lower for operators with bypass history. Not a meaningful security regression since the 5-second window and visible warning are the real gates. The flag's purpose is deliberate intent expression, not entropy-based forgery prevention.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"No code change required. Consider a documentation note in the `--help` text that the flag is an intent-expression mechanism, not a secret, and that the operative escape hatch is the 5-second Ctrl-C window (which should be protected via SAF-R2-004's signal-reset fix). Acceptable as-is.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R2-008\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Argument validation applies the `--no-enforce-admins` acknowledgement check and 5-second pause (lines 174-188) before REPO/BRANCH format validation (lines 193-199), causing the operator to experience the bypass warning and wait through the pause before seeing a bad-argument error.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Parsing loop (lines 143-161) sets all flags. Lines 163-167 check that REPO and BRANCH are non-empty. Lines 174-188 check `ENFORCE_ADMINS != true` and fire the ack-flag gate + sleep. Lines 193-199 then check the regex and case patterns. An invocation like `--no-enforce-admins --i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate --repo invalid@@format --branch main` waits 5 seconds and displays the bypass warning before failing the regex check.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Cosmetic UX confusion only. The script still exits without applying any mutations. No security regression \\u2014 argument validation order does not affect the safety property.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Move REPO/BRANCH non-empty check (lines 163-167) and the regex/case validation (lines 193-199) to immediately after the parsing loop, before the enforce-admins ack-flag block. This ensures format errors surface before the operator waits through the bypass pause.\\\"}],\\\"summary\\\":\\\"R1 closures verified for SAF-001 through SAF-010 with one material exception: SAF-010 is only partially closed. The verify-failure fall-through path is correctly implemented (lines 306-313), but apply-phase `set -e` aborts \\u2014 specifically a non-zero exit from the `required_signatures` POST on line 288 \\u2014 skip the log-emission block entirely. This residual gap is raised as SAF-R2-001 (MAJ): partial protection state (PUT succeeded, POST failed) with no audit trail, violating the WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020G(iii) invocation-log mandate. A secondary MIN finding (SAF-R2-002) surfaces that `log \\\\\\\"verification passed \\u2713\\\\\\\"` on line 315 is unconditional \\u2014 any stdout-only observer (log scraper, CI wrapper) receives a false success message when FAIL=1. Two further MIN findings probe the SAF-001 SHA self-hash reliability on stock macOS (SAF-R2-003: `readlink -f` unavailability; bare `$0` fallback can hash the wrong file in CWD) and the SIGINT-inherited-ignore degradation of the 5-second escape hatch (SAF-R2-004: `trap '' INT` in a calling context silently disables Ctrl-C). One MIN on CI guard coverage gaps for Jenkins and Azure DevOps (SAF-R2-005) and two nits (BEFORE_STATUS env-var collision SAF-R2-006; history pre-fill of ack flag SAF-R2-007; arg-validation order SAF-R2-008) complete the round. All dispatch-note probes addressed: SHA race (not material \\u2014 attacker needs execute access; macOS readlink is the more realistic surface), BEFORE_JSON null fallback (verified correct \\u2014 jq yields null cleanly for the no-protection case), SIGINT interruptibility (MIN finding), dotted owner names (non-finding \\u2014 gh CLI handles without URL interpretation), arg-order (nit). Verdict: NEEDS_REVISION on SAF-R2-001 and SAF-R2-002; SAF-R2-003 and SAF-R2-004 are non-blocking but warrant a fix pass before merge.\\\"}\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r2.json:103:        \"evidence\": \"Line 331: `SCRIPT_PATH=\\\"$(readlink -f \\\"$0\\\" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' \\\"$0\\\")\\\"`. macOS ships BSD readlink which does not accept `-f`; it exits non-zero, and `2>/dev/null` suppresses the error, causing the fallback `printf '%s' \\\"$0\\\"` to execute. When the script is installed on PATH and invoked as `enable-required-check.sh` (bare name), `$0` is `enable-required-check.sh` with no directory component. `shasum -a 256 \\\"enable-required-check.sh\\\"` then searches the current working directory \\u2014 not the script's actual location. If a file named `enable-required-check.sh` exists in CWD (possibly an older version, a modified copy, or a symlink), its hash is recorded in the audit log. If no such file exists in CWD, shasum exits non-zero and `set -e` aborts the script after mutations have already been applied (though the mutation-then-abort sequence is the same as SAF-R2-001).\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r2.json:111:        \"evidence\": \"Lines 185-187: `echo \\\"... 5-second pause to allow Ctrl-C...\\\" >&2; sleep 5`. POSIX specifies that when a process forks+execs a child, if a signal is SIG_IGN in the parent, it is SIG_IGN in the child unless the child explicitly resets it. If an outer shell or orchestration wrapper runs `trap '' INT` before invoking this script, both the bash process and its `sleep 5` child inherit SIGINT=SIG_IGN. Terminal Ctrl-C sends SIGINT to the foreground process group; if ignored at the process level, neither the sleep nor the script terminates. Realistic scenario: the script is wrapped inside a larger provisioning shell script that suppresses SIGINT to prevent mid-run interruption (`trap '' INT; ... ./enable-required-check.sh ...; trap - INT`).\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness-r4-package.json:6:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r3-package.json:6:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:24:1. **`enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode** proves invariant 7's <30-min rollback SLO is real, not aspirational. Operator-interactive demo at step 7 is merge-blocking AC and is recorded through the placeholder evidence path until the operator completes the real-repo round-trip.\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:34:| `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode | `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` (modify) | New `--restore <pre-state.json>` flag reverses prior branch-protection mutation per plan-doc invariant 7. Existing forward-mode unchanged. **Key behaviours per extras-parking lessons:** (a) GET-shape JSON transformed to PUT-shape via Python helper (strips `_links`, `url`, `checks`, `required_signatures`); (b) `required_signatures` reset via dedicated DELETE/POST sub-resource calls; (c) APPLY_FAIL=0 wrap pattern (failed PUT continues to log emission); (d) posture-degradation flags `--i-understand-restore-removes-admin-enforcement` + `--i-understand-restore-removes-required-checks` required when before-state has `enforce_admins.enabled=false` or empty `required_status_checks.contexts`; (e) restore log block captures actual pre-restore state via `gh api -X GET` BEFORE PUT (not just the target good-state). |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:35:| Forward-mode safety check | `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` (modify) | Refuses forward-mode invocation against repo with no successful `policy-check` workflow run on default branch in last 60 days. **Critical (R8 + R9 lessons):** workflow lookup by `path == \".github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml\"` (NOT by display name); query workflow-runs API WITHOUT `&branch=` filter (PR-triggered runs have head-branch = feature branch, not default; branch filter defeats gate). `--i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci` flag for cold-start. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:36:| `--expected-wrapper-sha` flag | `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` (modify) | Validates supplied SHA against actual git tags via `gh api repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/git/ref/tags/<tag>` (NOT just 40-char shape match). Used by Gate 3 of break-glass. **Mutex with `--i-understand-this-repo-has-no-prior-green-ci`** (R7 SAFE-MAJ-001 closure). |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:42:| **Step 7 operator-interactive demo evidence** | `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/restore-roundtrip-evidence-PLACEHOLDER.md` | **MERGE-BLOCKING.** Operator-interactive after R-fixpoint reached. Real-repo round-trip on throw-away test repo: create test repo → invoke `enable-required-check.sh` forward-mode → capture pre-state → invoke `--restore` mode → verify branch-protection API response matches captured before-state byte-for-byte → commit evidence. NOT a dry-run mock. The placeholder path is the handoff target until the operator completes the demo. Closes plan-doc invariant 7 SLO + 024A R1 MAJ-SAFE-003. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/CONTINUATION-PROMPT-2026-05-04-am.md:47:- `enable-required-check.sh --restore <pre-state.json>` mode — rollback path per invariant 7. **MUST be demonstrated functional via real-repo round-trip on a throw-away test repo** (NOT a dry-run mock) before 024C opens.\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/CONTINUATION-PROMPT-2026-05-04-am.md:66:- `enable-required-check.sh` invocation (operator-run from local).\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r4-package.json:6:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020h3/FIX-ROUND-3.md:17:| SAFE-R2-001 | MAJ | §12.7.4 multi-maintainer mode now requires `dismiss_stale_reviews: true` with rationale: without it, an attacker can obtain a CODEOWNER review on initial commit, then push subsequent malicious commits (rule-config disable, scp_bypass=true, fork-PR `if:` removal) without re-review. The `enable-required-check.sh` helper preserves existing review-shape verbatim and does not set this for adopters; the appendix instructs explicit configuration via the GitHub UI/API. |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/FIX-ROUND-1.md:52:- `scripts/enable-required-check.sh` — substantial rewrite: bootstrap-only CI guard; PAT-scope warning; argument validation; required_signatures via dedicated endpoint; required_pull_request_reviews preservation; --no-enforce-admins acknowledgement-flag gate; SHA256 self-hash; nested-fence fix; null-safe jq.\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r3-archive/r1-correctness.json:1:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":881612,\"duration_api_ms\":882474,\"num_turns\":15,\"result\":\"Now I have all the information needed. Let me produce the structured review.\\n\\n{\\\"review_id\\\":\\\"r2-correctness\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-024/024B-core\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"correctness\\\",\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"sonnet-r2-correctness\\\",\\\"date\\\":\\\"2026-05-09\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"CHANGES_REQUESTED\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"All four R1 findings (CORR-CRIT-001 two-pass bypass, CORR-MAJ-001 unreachable not-applicable branch, CORR-MAJ-002 run_enable_case residual, CORR-MAJ-003 missing bypass test) have been resolved. The single-pass re.findall regex eliminates the indented-not-applicable bypass; the not-applicable case branch is now reachable via ([^\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n]+?); the enable-required-check.sh reference and run_enable_case helper are gone; case 20 covers the indented-not-applicable-before-onboarded scenario. The slice correctly implements bootstrap-only guard, 40-char lowercase hex SHA validation, system-path denylist with macOS /private/* canonicalization via readlink -f, 4-file emission with SHA256 MANIFEST audit, template with Renovate marker immediately before uses: line, least-privilege permissions (contents:read + statuses:write only), fork-PR refusal if: clause, five-path cascade-status state machine with exactly-one-match assertion, adopter-slug canonicalization, SAFE-003 log-entry exactly-one assertion, D-044 correctly dated and scoped, STATUS.md 2026-05-09 PM chain entry present, and plan-doc §6 split-amendment with FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 gate preserved. Two MAJ findings remain: the YAML lint check's pyyaml-absent handler exits code 1 under set -euo pipefail (turns a skip into a hard abort), and the --pr mode test lacks onboarded-operator-bump and blocked-on-adopter-conflict coverage (the blocked-on-adopter-conflict negative log-modification assertion traverses gh pr diff --name-only, a code path never exercised by run_pr_case). Two MIN findings: --diff-base path lacks error handling for invalid DIFF_BASE (inconsistency with --pr path's 2>/dev/null || true), and the 024B-core DISPATCH-NOTE uses backtick-prose form for cascade-status rather than the bare line-start format that check-invocation-log-entry.sh requires.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MAJ-001-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh YAML lint check handles pyyaml ImportError via raise SystemExit(str), which Python resolves to exit code 1. set -euo pipefail then aborts the entire test runner, turning an intended graceful skip into a hard FAIL that masks all subsequent test cases.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:132-136 — except ImportError as exc: raise SystemExit('SKIP: pyyaml not installed — install it to run YAML lint checks') from exc. Python 3: SystemExit(non-int) exits code 1. set -euo pipefail at line 2 causes the script to abort on the non-zero python3 exit.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Any CI or dev environment without pyyaml installed sees the entire scaffolder test suite report FAIL rather than a targeted SKIP. Tests after line 138 (arg-validation, CI-guard, denylist, non-HEAD-warn, default-branch variants) are never reached, producing a false-negative that masks real regressions.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace raise SystemExit('SKIP: ...') from exc with: print('SKIP: pyyaml not installed — install it to run YAML lint checks', file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(0). This preserves the informational message while correctly exiting 0 so set -e does not abort the runner.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MAJ-002-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The --pr mode test (run_pr_case) is exercised only for cascade-status=onboarded (case16 happy path, case17 SAFE-003 phantom). No run_pr_case test exists for onboarded-operator-bump or blocked-on-adopter-conflict. The blocked-on-adopter-conflict negative log-modification assertion uses changed_files from gh pr diff --name-only — a distinct code path from git diff --name-only — and is entirely untested in PR mode.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh — grep of run_pr_case shows calls only at case16 (line ~552) and case17 (line ~609). check-invocation-log-entry.sh:168 — changed_files via gh pr diff; line 171 — via git diff. The branch_log_modified=0 assertion for blocked-on-adopter-conflict is tested only via --diff-base (case10).\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A regression in the gh pr diff --name-only parsing path (e.g., unexpected path prefix, trailing whitespace, or gh CLI version difference) could cause blocked-on-adopter-conflict slices that DID modify branch-protection-log.md to pass PR-mode CI enforcement. The negative safety assertion is the core of invariant-2's blocked-on-adopter-conflict guarantee.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add run_pr_case test for blocked-on-adopter-conflict where fake gh --name-only returns branch-protection-log.md path; expect exit 1 with 'ERROR: branch-protection-log.md was modified'. Add run_pr_case for onboarded-operator-bump where fake gh --name-only returns the log file path and --patch returns the log entry; verify STATUS.md TF-row grep still exercises local-file path (unchanged) and expect exit 0.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MIN-001-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"In --diff-base mode, git diff at line 171 has no error guard, while the --pr path at line 169 uses 2>/dev/null || true. An invalid DIFF_BASE ref causes set -euo pipefail to abort at line 171 with a raw git error rather than a script-controlled diagnostic, and no test covers this failure mode.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh:171 — branch_log_patch=$(git diff --unified=0 ${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD -- $BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL) — no error guard. Line 169 — || true present. test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh — no test passes an invalid --diff-base value.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Operators running the script manually with an incorrect base ref (e.g. a typo or a force-pushed-away ref) receive an opaque git error with no context about which argument was invalid, making diagnosis harder. The asymmetry also obscures a latent failure mode where gh fails silently in --pr mode while --diff-base fails loudly.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Wrap line 171: branch_log_patch=$(git diff --unified=0 \\\\\\\"${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD\\\\\\\" -- \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL\\\\\\\") || die \\\\\\\"ERROR: could not compute diff for diff-base '${DIFF_BASE}'; is this a valid git ref?\\\\\\\" 2. Add a test that passes an invalid --diff-base value and expects exit 2.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-MIN-002-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The 024B-core DISPATCH-NOTE records cascade-status in backtick-prose form (`cascade-status: not applicable` — line 9) rather than as a bare line-start field. check-invocation-log-entry.sh requires ^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+ at column 0 (re.M). If the CI enforcement workflow (landing in 024B-extras) is ever applied repo-wide or to historical DISPATCHNOTEs, 024B-core's DISPATCH-NOTE would produce 'expected exactly one cascade-status match, found 0' and fail.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/DISPATCH-NOTE.md:9 — `cascade-status: not applicable` — this slice... (backtick-wrapped, embedded in prose). check-invocation-log-entry.sh:127 — re.findall(r'^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+...', text, re.M) requires field at column 0. test case15b shows the correct machine-parseable form: cascade-status: not applicable as a bare line.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"No runtime defect for 024B-core because the CI enforcement workflow is deferred to 024B-extras. However, the format inconsistency mismatches what future tooling-slice DISPATCHNOTEs should use, and any repo-wide integrity sweep using check-invocation-log-entry.sh against the 024B-core DISPATCH-NOTE would produce a false CI failure.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a bare cascade-status: not applicable line at the top of the DISPATCH-NOTE header block (the existing backtick-prose sentence can remain as prose context). This makes the DISPATCH-NOTE self-consistent with the enforcement contract and safe for future repo-wide sweeps.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-nit-001-R2\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"validate_manifest helper in test_scaffold_downstream.sh hardcodes expected adopter_repo='jrnb2024/test-adopter' and scorecard_emit=False. The helper is called for the happy-path and non-HEAD-SHA cases but not for the master or scorecard-emit=true variants. If a future test case passes different values and reuses validate_manifest, the hardcoded assertions will unexpectedly fail.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:61-67 — if payload['adopter_repo'] != 'jrnb2024/test-adopter': raise SystemExit('unexpected adopter_repo'); if payload['scorecard_emit'] is not False: raise SystemExit. Lines 139, 182 — validate_manifest called for main happy path and non-HEAD-SHA variant only; master/develop/scorecard-emit=true variants do not call validate_manifest.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"No current defect. Brittle for future test expansion — adding a new call with different fixture values would require modifying validate_manifest rather than just calling it.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Parameterise validate_manifest to accept expected_adopter_repo and expected_scorecard_emit arguments rather than hardcoding. Update call sites to pass the current fixture values explicitly.\\\"}]}\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"cdb14333-9eb7-4b96-91ce-f629aa722c40\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.4142677,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":9,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":132498,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":409594,\"output_tokens\":52794,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":132498,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":16627,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":136704,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":3175,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":3175},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":2445,\"outputTokens\":28,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.002585,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":9,\"outputTokens\":52794,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":409594,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":132498,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.4116827,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"2f476711-ee4e-453f-bafa-a1398d769e91\"}\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r4.json:32:    \"result\": \"{\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"R4-SAFETY\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"020G\\\",\\\"work_package\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-022\\\",\\\"round\\\":4,\\\"lens\\\":\\\"safety_bypass\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"CONDITIONAL\\\",\\\"r3_closure_verification\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R3-001\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"enable-required-check.sh line 365: `FAIL=\\\\\\\"${FAIL:-0}\\\\\\\"` \\u2014 the default-preserving initialisation is present and correctly positioned. Verified no-FAIL-write path between apply-phase (lines 329\\u2013348) and line 365: lines 352\\u2013364 are exclusively the verify-section GET (`AFTER_JSON`) and four jq extractions (`CHECKS_STRICT`, `CHECKS_CONTEXTS`, `APPLIED_ADMINS`, `SIGS_ENABLED`). None assign to FAIL. APPLY_FAIL=1 at line 334 (PUT failure) \\u2192 FAIL=1 at line 339; or APPLY_FAIL=1 at line 342 (POST failure) \\u2192 FAIL=1 at line 347. Both are preserved intact by `${FAIL:-0}` at line 365. The false-success laundering window introduced by fix-round-2 is closed. CLOSED.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R3-002\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"enable-required-check.sh lines 233\\u2013241: case statement `*'\\\\\\\\`'*|*$'\\\\\\\\n'*|*$'\\\\\\\\r'*)` rejects any REQUIRED_CONTEXT value containing a backtick, LF, or CR; `[ \\\\\\\"${#REQUIRED_CONTEXT}\\\\\\\" -gt 200 ]` applies 200-char cap. Evidence from probe-2 analysis confirms: LF/CR rejection prevents ~~~ outer-fence-close injection (a newline is required to open a line starting with `~~~`); backtick rejection prevents inline code-span corruption; jq `--arg` encodes REQUIRED_CONTEXT for the payload (no injection surface there); `grep -q -F` is fixed-string (no regex injection). Square brackets, parens, and hyphens cannot close a fenced block and are correctly not rejected. Validation set is correct and minimal. CLOSED.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R3-003\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"CLOSED\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh lines 118\\u2013122: POST is wrapped in `if ! gh api -X POST ... >/dev/null; then echo \\\\\\\"ERROR: required_signatures POST failed; status-checks/admins/reviews are set but signatures are NOT\\\\\\\" >&2; echo \\\\\\\"partial branch-protection state on ${REPO}@${DEFAULT_BRANCH}; re-run after fixing PAT scope\\\\\\\" >&2; exit 1; fi`. Explicit error message names the partial state and advises the operator to re-run. Asymmetric diagnostic quality vs bare set-e is resolved for the POST. CLOSED.\\\"}],\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R4-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh does not validate SCP_REQUIRED_CONTEXT before interpolating it into the heredoc PUT body at line 87. Fix-round-3 added backtick/CR/LF/length-cap validation to enable-required-check.sh (SAF-R3-002 closure) but the symmetric fix was not applied to configure-020d2-required-check.sh, which uses REQUIRED_CONTEXT from the same env-var source and interpolates it raw into a bash heredoc JSON string.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh line 41: `REQUIRED_CONTEXT=\\\\\\\"${SCP_REQUIRED_CONTEXT:-policy-check / scp/policy-check}\\\\\\\"` \\u2014 no validation block follows. Lines 83\\u2013105: heredoc JSON contains `\\\\\\\"contexts\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"${REQUIRED_CONTEXT}\\\\\\\"]` (line 87); REQUIRED_CONTEXT is expanded in the heredoc at shell evaluation time, not escaped by jq. A value containing a double-quote closes the JSON string early (e.g., `foo\\\\\\\" }` yields `\\\\\\\"contexts\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"foo\\\\\\\" }\\\\\\\"...`), producing malformed JSON that the GitHub API rejects. A crafted value of the form `x\\\\\\\"], \\\\\\\"enforce_admins\\\\\\\": false, \\\\\\\"required_status_checks\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"strict\\\\\\\": true, \\\\\\\"contexts\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"x` would attempt to inject a duplicate `enforce_admins: false` field before the canonical `enforce_admins: true` on line 89; whether GitHub's parser honours the first or last instance of a duplicate key determines whether the gate is bypassed. Contrast: enable-required-check.sh lines 233\\u2013241 contains the validation block added in fix-round-3. configure-020d2-required-check.sh has no such block.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Malformed JSON scenarios safe-fail (gh exits non-zero; set -e aborts). The residual risk is a crafted duplicate-key injection that passes JSON parsing and causes GitHub to honour an attacker-controlled value for enforce_admins or contexts. This requires operator-level access to the execution environment (ability to set SCP_REQUIRED_CONTEXT). The gate-relevant field is enforce_admins: an injected false value would disable admin enforcement without the --i-understand-this-bypasses-the-gate friction path required by enable-required-check.sh. Probability: low (operator-level prerequisite). Consequence: enforcement gap on the SCP self-repo policy gate. The primary risk is inconsistency: R3 established that validation is required; 020D2 is now silently out of conformance with its own sister script.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add the following validation block to configure-020d2-required-check.sh immediately after line 41 (the REQUIRED_CONTEXT assignment): `case \\\\\\\"$REQUIRED_CONTEXT\\\\\\\" in *'\\\\\\\\`'*|*$'\\\\\\\\n'*|*$'\\\\\\\\r'*) echo \\\\\\\"error: SCP_REQUIRED_CONTEXT contains markdown-corrupting chars (backtick, CR, LF)\\\\\\\" >&2; exit 1 ;; esac; if [ \\\\\\\"${#REQUIRED_CONTEXT}\\\\\\\" -gt 200 ]; then echo \\\\\\\"error: SCP_REQUIRED_CONTEXT is ${#REQUIRED_CONTEXT} chars; max 200\\\\\\\" >&2; exit 1; fi`. This mirrors enable-required-check.sh lines 233\\u2013241 exactly, closing the asymmetry. Optionally also reject double-quote (`*'\\\\\\\"'*`) to directly block heredoc JSON injection, though the LF/CR/backtick set is the minimal R3-established standard.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAF-R4-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh PUT step (lines 81\\u2013105) has no custom diagnostic on failure. Fix-round-3 added an explicit error message to the POST step (SAF-R3-003 closure) but the PUT step still relies solely on bare `set -euo pipefail` + gh CLI stderr output, with no script-level message naming the partial state or advising the operator.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh lines 81\\u2013105: `gh api -X PUT ... >/dev/null` \\u2014 no `|| { echo ERROR; exit 1; }` wrapper. Lines 118\\u2013122: POST uses `if ! ...; then echo ERROR >&2; exit 1; fi` (SAF-R3-003 closure). With `set -euo pipefail` (line 28), a PUT failure causes immediate exit with gh CLI stderr only (e.g., `gh: HTTP 403: Resource not accessible by integration`) \\u2014 no script-level message like `[020D2] ERROR: unified PUT failed; signatures POST was not reached`. Compare: enable-required-check.sh lines 332\\u2013339 \\u2014 PUT failure sets APPLY_FAIL=1, emits `ERROR: unified PUT failed; partial branch-protection state may exist`. Asymmetric within 020D2 itself (POST has diagnostic, PUT does not) and with 020G.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Not a bypass \\u2014 exits non-zero. Operator diagnostic quality is reduced: on PUT failure the operator sees gh output with no script-level context identifying which step failed or what partial state to expect. Operationally inconsistent with the SAF-R3-003 POST fix applied one level up in the same function.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Wrap the PUT call in an explicit failure handler: `gh api -X PUT ... >/dev/null || { echo \\\\\\\"[020D2] ERROR: unified PUT failed; required_signatures POST was not reached; no protection changes applied\\\\\\\" >&2; exit 1; }`. Not a blocking merge issue.\\\"}],\\\"dispatch_probes_addressed\\\":[{\\\"probe\\\":1,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"FAIL preservation path: any write between apply-phase trap and verify-section FAIL initialisation\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"NON-FINDING. Confirmed clean. Lines 352\\u2013364 (the gap between apply-phase and `FAIL=\\\\\\\"${FAIL:-0}\\\\\\\"` at line 365) contain exactly: one `log` call, one GET assignment (`AFTER_JSON`), and four jq extractions (`CHECKS_STRICT`, `CHECKS_CONTEXTS`, `APPLIED_ADMINS`, `SIGS_ENABLED`). None assign to FAIL. The FAIL=1 set at line 339 (PUT failure) or line 347 (POST failure) is unconditionally preserved by `${FAIL:-0}`. No bypass path exists between the trap and the initialiser.\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":2,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"REQUIRED_CONTEXT minimal rejection set: are backtick/CR/LF sufficient?\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"NON-FINDING for enable-required-check.sh. The minimal set is correct and sufficient: (a) backtick rejection prevents inline code-span close in the emitted `\\\\\\\\`${REQUIRED_CONTEXT}\\\\\\\\`` log field; (b) LF/CR rejection prevents ~~~ outer-fence-close injection (a new line starting with `~~~` is the only way to close the CommonMark tilde fence \\u2014 no newline means no line boundary means no fence-close); (c) jq `--arg` encodes the PUT payload value (no injection surface at the API call level); (d) `grep -q -F` is fixed-string (no regex injection surface); (e) square brackets, parens, and other markdown chars cannot close a fenced block or a backtick code span without a backtick or newline. The set {backtick, LF, CR, length>200} is minimal and complete for the stated threat surface. FINDING raised as SAF-R4-001 (MIN) for configure-020d2 which lacks this validation.\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":3,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"configure-020d2 POST diagnostic: clear, names partial state\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"NON-FINDING. configure-020d2-required-check.sh lines 118\\u2013122: error message `ERROR: required_signatures POST failed; status-checks/admins/reviews are set but signatures are NOT` plus `partial branch-protection state on ${REPO}@${DEFAULT_BRANCH}; re-run after fixing PAT scope`. Clear, names both the failed step and the surviving partial state. SAF-R3-003 CLOSED.\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":4,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"Cross-script symmetry: 020G APPLY_FAIL trap vs 020D2 if-not POST guard; 020D2 PUT set-e feedback quality\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"ASYMMETRIC \\u2014 two sub-findings. (a) PUT diagnostic asymmetry: 020D2 PUT (lines 81\\u2013105) uses bare set-e; 020G PUT uses APPLY_FAIL trap with explicit error message. 020D2 IS protected by set -euo pipefail and exits non-zero on PUT failure; the gh CLI stderr output is sufficient for diagnosis. Not a bypass; raised as SAF-R4-002 (nit). (b) REQUIRED_CONTEXT validation asymmetry: the fix-round-3 validation added to enable-required-check.sh was not applied to configure-020d2, which also interpolates REQUIRED_CONTEXT into a heredoc. Raised as SAF-R4-001 (MIN). The asymmetry between the APPLY_FAIL pattern (020G) and the if-not-POST pattern (020D2) is acceptable given the different operational contexts (020G is an attended adopter helper that must emit an audit log even on failure; 020D2 is an idempotent self-targeting script).\\\"},{\\\"probe\\\":5,\\\"topic\\\":\\\"020D2 dispatch-note post-merge correction: audit-trail integrity\\\",\\\"finding\\\":\\\"NON-FINDING. The COMP-R3-001 closure added a 'Post-merge correction' section to the 020D2 DISPATCH-NOTE.md that names PR #63 as the source of the latent required_signatures bug, dates the correction to 2026-04-30 evening (fix-round-3), and scopes the change to the POST-only path without retroactively altering prior round records. Audit-trail integrity is preserved: the correction is additive (new section), cites the corrective fix path, and does not edit or remove prior round findings or decisions.\\\"}],\\\"self_reported_concerns\\\":[],\\\"summary\\\":\\\"All three R3 safety findings confirmed closed: SAF-R3-001 (FAIL preservation) closed via `FAIL=\\\\\\\"${FAIL:-0}\\\\\\\"` at line 365 with verified no-write gap in lines 352\\u2013364; SAF-R3-002 (REQUIRED_CONTEXT injection) closed via case-statement rejection of backtick/CR/LF + 200-char cap in enable-required-check.sh lines 233\\u2013241; SAF-R3-003 (020D2 POST diagnostic) closed via if-not wrapper at configure-020d2 lines 118\\u2013122. Fix-round-3 introduces one new MIN finding (SAF-R4-001): the REQUIRED_CONTEXT validation added to enable-required-check.sh was not applied symmetrically to configure-020d2-required-check.sh, which interpolates the same env-var directly into a heredoc JSON PUT body. The injection risk requires operator-level access and most payloads safe-fail as malformed JSON; the residual concern is a crafted duplicate-key injection that could set enforce_admins=false without the ack-flag friction path. Fix is one validation block mirroring lines 233\\u2013241. One new nit (SAF-R4-002): 020D2 PUT step has no custom diagnostic; fix-round-3 corrected the POST but not the PUT. Not a bypass; operator still sees gh CLI stderr and non-zero exit. Verdict: CONDITIONAL on SAF-R4-001. SAF-R4-002 is non-blocking. No new CRIT or MAJ surface from fix-round-3.\\\"}\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-safety-r4.json:80:  \"stdout_tail\": \"nimal R3-established standard.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"SAF-R4-002\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"nit\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"claim\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh PUT step (lines 81\\u2013105) has no custom diagnostic on failure. Fix-round-3 added an explicit error message to the POST step (SAF-R3-003 closure) but the PUT step still relies solely on bare `set -euo pipefail` + gh CLI stderr output, with no script-level message naming the partial state or advising the operator.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"configure-020d2-required-check.sh lines 81\\u2013105: `gh api -X PUT ... >/dev/null` \\u2014 no `|| { echo ERROR; exit 1; }` wrapper. Lines 118\\u2013122: POST uses `if ! ...; then echo ERROR >&2; exit 1; fi` (SAF-R3-003 closure). With `set -euo pipefail` (line 28), a PUT failure causes immediate exit with gh CLI stderr only (e.g., `gh: HTTP 403: Resource not accessible by integration`) \\u2014 no script-level mes