\\\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":1246,\\\"outputTokens\\\":34,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":0,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.001416,\\\"contextWindow\\\":200000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000},\\\"claude-sonnet-4-6\\\":{\\\"inputTokens\\\":8,\\\"outputTokens\\\":17304,\\\"cacheReadInputTokens\\\":180173,\\\"cacheCreationInputTokens\\\":53410,\\\"webSearchRequests\\\":0,\\\"costUSD\\\":0.5139234,\\\"contextWindow\\\":600000,\\\"maxOutputTokens\\\":32000}},\\\"permission_denials\\\":[],\\\"terminal_reason\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"fast_mode_state\\\":\\\"off\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"e4c88271-588c-42f8-ae2e-5987c894df4c\\\"}\\n\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness-r4.json:88:        \"evidence\": \"enable-required-check.sh lines 234: `*$'\\\\n'*|*$'\\\\r'*` in case pattern. Shebang is `#!/usr/bin/env bash` (line 1), which is correct; ANSI-C quoting is a bash extension and works correctly in bash. ShellCheck under --shell=bash emits no warning; under --shell=sh (or via global shellcheck.rc defaulting to sh) it would flag SC3009.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness-r4.json:100:        \"evidence\": \"enable-required-check.sh line 365: `FAIL=\\\"${FAIL:-0}\\\"`. Apply-phase trap emits FAIL=1 at line 339 (PUT failure branch) and line 346 (POST failure branch). Default-to-0 only fires when FAIL is unset, preserving any prior 1. Both FAIL=1 assignments confirmed in the APPLY_FAIL handlers via grep output.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness-r4.json:109:        \"evidence\": \"enable-required-check.sh line 353: `AFTER_JSON=\\\"$(gh api -X GET ...)\\\"` \\u2014 naked call, no `|| AFTER_STATUS=$?` fallback. set -euo pipefail in effect.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness-r4.json:128:        \"result\": \"PASS \\u2014 enable-required-check.sh lines 233\\u2013237. Pattern syntax is valid bash: ANSI-C quoting `$'\\\\n'` expands to literal newline before pattern matching. A REQUIRED_CONTEXT containing an actual CR or LF character would match the pattern. A backtick is matched by `*'\\\\`'*`. Validation fires before the value reaches the log heredoc (line 427 onward), so injection via newline cannot corrupt the outer `~~~markdown` fence. The validation is correct for its stated purpose.\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r3-archive/r1-completeness_governance.json:1:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":450447,\"duration_api_ms\":451094,\"num_turns\":12,\"result\":\"{\\\"lens\\\":\\\"COMPLETENESS_GOVERNANCE\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"CHANGES_REQUESTED\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"024B-core scope split is well-governed: SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md carries empirical R6→R7→R8 trajectory, an explicit why-not-descope section, and a carry-forward reference. The D-044 decision row cites the scope-correction doc, is scoped to core deliverables, and is filed ACCEPTED. STATUS.md split-decision entry documents the extras-parking branch and three outcome rows. The plan-doc §6 amendment gates 024C on both halves plus FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 (captured in the split note). VERSIONING.md alignment is clean — scaffolder, template, and CI script are internal tooling, no version bump required. Anti-descoping posture is sound; no extras-scope content leaked into core files. Code coverage of plan-doc invariant 2 is complete across all five cascade-status check-paths including the fail-closed default and adopter-slug canonicalisation. One MAJ is filed: the DISPATCH-NOTE declares cascade-status: not applicable inside a backtick markdown span (line starts with the backtick delimiter, not cascade-status:), so the enforcement script's Python regex ^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+ finds zero matches and would exit with an error if run against this file. This breaks machine-parseability of the DISPATCH-NOTE per plan-doc invariant 2 and will cause CI self-dogfood failure when 024B-extras wires the enforcement workflow. Four lower-severity findings cover the scorecard-emit true permissions gap in the template, asymmetric negative-case test coverage for the blocked-on-adopter-conflict regex path, missing manifest validation for the scorecard-emit true variant, and a stale invocation-log convention claim in the DECISIONS.md reservation header note.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MAJ-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE cascade-status field is backtick-wrapped so the enforcement script regex cannot parse it, finding 0 matches instead of 1.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md line 9: starts with backtick character — `cascade-status: not applicable` — not with cascade-status:. Python regex ^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+([^\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n]+?)\\\\\\\\s*$ with re.M anchors to line-start and finds 0 matches, triggering the exactly-one-match error exit. test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh case 15b uses a bare cascade-status: not applicable at line start as the passing fixture, confirming required format.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"When 024B-extras ships the CI enforcement workflow, any PR that checks the 024B-core DISPATCH-NOTE exits non-zero. Also establishes a formatting precedent that would silently break cascade-slice self-dogfood if copied.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a bare cascade-status: not applicable line at the start of a line in the DISPATCH-NOTE (no backtick prefix), replacing or preceding the backtick-formatted inline-code span, so the regex finds exactly one match.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MIN-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Template adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl has no inline comment warning that scorecard-emit: true requires additional permissions not present in the template.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Template lines 9-12: permissions block emits only contents: read + statuses: write regardless of {{SCORECARD_EMIT}}. TF-023E-002 documents that attestations: write + id-token: write are required for the attest-scorecard job when scorecard-emit: true. DISPATCH-NOTE notes 'opt-in adopters add attestations:write + id-token:write per ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 step 2' but the template file itself is silent.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Operator using --scorecard-emit true receives a syntactically valid but functionally broken wrapper; adopter CI fails at startup_failure with no in-file indication of the root cause. Failure occurs silently at scaffolder run time.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add an inline comment in the template adjacent to the permissions block noting that scorecard-emit: true additionally requires attestations: write + id-token: write per ADOPT-001 §12.7.15 step 2 and TF-023E-002 before enabling.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MIN-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh negative TF-format cases (ex3–ex6) are only exercised on the onboarded-operator-bump path; blocked-on-adopter-conflict path has no parallel negative-format tests.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Cases 4c–4f test bare-prefix, empty-description, whitespace, and too-short TF formats via STATUS.md for the operator-bump path. Case 11 tests missing TF reference for the conflict path but includes no format-validation negatives. DISPATCH-NOTE AC claims '6 worked-example matrix cases from plan-doc §2 invariant 2 (2 ✅ + 4 ❌)' covered, but the 4 ❌ cases test only the operator-bump path.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Shared regex means logic is correct; however test asymmetry leaves conflict-path TF-format enforcement unproven. A future regex regression specific to the conflict path would not be caught by the test suite.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add cases mirroring 4c–4f for the blocked-on-adopter-conflict path: bare-prefix stub, empty description, all-whitespace description, and too-short description in the DISPATCH-NOTE TF conflict reference.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MIN-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"validate_manifest in test_scaffold_downstream.sh only asserts scorecard_emit is False; the --scorecard-emit true variant has no manifest validation.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh lines 67-68: if payload['scorecard_emit'] is not False: raise SystemExit. The master-variant test (lines 186-197 using --scorecard-emit true) only checks wrapper file content and does not call validate_manifest. The manifest's scorecard_emit boolean field is never asserted for the true case.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A regression that emits scorecard_emit: false in the manifest when --scorecard-emit true is passed would go undetected by the test suite.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Parameterise validate_manifest to accept an expected_scorecard_emit argument and call it in the scorecard-emit true variant test block with expected value True.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-MIN-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"DECISIONS.md reservation header note for D-044 still references 'scaffolder writes a rendered-template log entry alongside enable-required-check.sh's branch-protection log', which was not implemented.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DECISIONS.md lines 29-46: reservation note retains 'Invocation-log convention extension: scaffolder writes a rendered-template log entry alongside enable-required-check.sh's branch-protection log.' Filed D-044 row describes actual implementation as MANIFEST.json SHA256 audit to output dir only. Plan-doc §5.3 also specifies only MANIFEST.json.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Operators reading the reservation note before the filed D-044 row expect a scaffolder-to-branch-protection-log write path that does not exist; could cause confusion at 024B-extras authoring or future adopter onboarding time.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Update the D-044 reservation header note to replace the stale invocation-log-extension language with the actual implementation: MANIFEST.json SHA256 audit emitted to output directory.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-nit-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Plan-doc §6 024C table row acceptance criteria does not inline the 024B-extras and FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 gate conditions.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"docs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md §6 024C row AC: 'PIM policy-check / scp/policy-check required-check live; ≥1 Renovate-bump-propagation cycle merged clean; D-045 filed.' Split note at line 213 captures both prerequisites but the 024C row itself does not enumerate them, requiring cross-referencing two locations.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A future operator reading only the 024C row risks missing the FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 gate; cohort onboarding could start before ACC install precondition is closed.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a prerequisites note to the 024C AC column: 'Prerequisites: 024B-core + 024B-extras merged + FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closed (per split note).'\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-R1-nit-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE Status line is stale: reads 'DRAFT v0.1 — awaiting Codex T3 surgical-clean dispatch' although Phase 1 Codex work is already complete.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md final line: **Status:** DRAFT v0.1 — awaiting Codex T3 surgical-clean dispatch. DISPATCH-NOTE §Sequencing Phase 0 is marked 'done'; Phase 1 Codex work appears to have landed given the slice files exist. Status was not updated after Phase 1 completed.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Cosmetic; reviewer sees a stale phase label and must infer actual progress from elsewhere.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Update Status line to reflect review-in-progress state, e.g. 'R1 review in progress (2026-05-09)'.\\\"}]}\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"a11a42f4-6dca-4c5b-b0f8-42dc6bc3a28e\",\"total_cost_usd\":0.8611840499999999,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":5607,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":97383,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":146456,\"output_tokens\":28909,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":97383,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":28038,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":84683,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":20081,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":20081},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1475,\"outputTokens\":26,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.0016049999999999999,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":5607,\"outputTokens\":28909,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":146456,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":97383,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.8595790499999999,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"b5f2c347-3a43-4554-a80c-f6b076b6cabc\"}\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness.json:32:    \"result\": \"{\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"Sonnet-R1-CORRECTNESS\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"CORRECTNESS\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-022/020G\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"NEEDS_REVISION\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Nested fenced code blocks in the emitted log entry are structurally broken: the outer triple-backtick markdown fence (line 243) is closed prematurely by the first bare triple-backtick line (line 253), which GFM parses as the closing fence of the outer block. The 'After' JSON section (lines 254\\u2013256) and the final closing fence (line 257) therefore render outside the outer code block.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 243\\u2013257 of scripts/enable-required-check.sh. The heredoc emits: (a) \\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`markdown on line 243 opening the outer fence; (b) '- **Before:** \\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`json' on line 251 \\u2014 this is safe (not a fence because it does not begin at column 0); (c) a bare \\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\` on line 253 \\u2014 per GFM \\u00a74.5 a closing fence is 3+ backticks with no other content, and this line qualifies, so it closes the outer block; (d) '- **After:** \\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`json' at line 254 and its \\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\` at line 256 are now outside the outer block and render as naked markdown prose. The outer closing fence at line 257 is orphaned. Bash heredoc escaping (\\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\` \\u2192 literal triple-backtick) is correct; the defect is at the GFM level, not the shell level.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"The markdown block the operator pastes into docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md renders incorrectly in every GFM renderer (GitHub, VS Code preview). The before-state is enclosed in the outer code block; the after-state is not, and multi-line JSON in the after section is partially parsed as markdown (bold, lists, etc.). Audit fidelity is degraded for every invocation.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Use a 4-backtick outer fence (````markdown \\u2026 ````) so that the inner 3-backtick fences do not satisfy the closing-fence rule. Alternatively, restructure to avoid inline fence tags on bullet lines by placing before/after JSON in separate labelled 3-backtick blocks at the top level of the entry (not nested inside an outer markdown fence).\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"SCRIPT_SHA is silently empty \\u2014 not 'unknown' \\u2014 when the script is invoked from a working directory that is not the SCP repo, because `git log` exits 0 with empty stdout when the path scripts/enable-required-check.sh has no commits in the current repo. The `|| echo unknown` fallback fires only on non-zero exit; it never fires here.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 227: SCRIPT_SHA=\\\\\\\"$(git -C \\\\\\\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || pwd)\\\\\\\" log -1 --format=%H -- scripts/enable-required-check.sh 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)\\\\\\\". The inner `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` resolves to the CWD repo root (which may be the adopter repo, not the SCP repo). `git log -- <path>` on a path absent from that repo's history exits 0 and emits nothing. SCP_REPO_ROOT (line 232) is a separate variable defined after line 227 and is never used for the SCRIPT_SHA lookup.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"The emitted log entry contains '**Script SHA:** ``' (empty backtick pair) rather than either a real SHA or the 'unknown' sentinel. A future audit cannot correlate the applied invocation with the script source. The WP-SCP-020 \\u00a74 020G(iii) requirement ('script SHA at invocation time') is unmet in the normal case where the operator runs from their adopter repo's root.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Move SCP_REPO_ROOT assignment before line 227 and use it in the git log call: `git -C \\\\\\\"${SCP_REPO_ROOT}\\\\\\\" log -1 --format=%H -- scripts/enable-required-check.sh`. Additionally add an explicit non-empty guard: `[ -n \\\\\\\"$SCRIPT_SHA\\\\\\\" ] || SCRIPT_SHA=unknown`. Document SCP_REPO_ROOT as a required env var when the operator is not running from the SCP repo root.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Including `required_signatures: true` in the unified PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/{branch}/protection payload relies on undocumented GitHub API behaviour. The required_signatures sub-resource is managed via its own dedicated endpoints (POST/DELETE \\u2026/protection/required_signatures) and is not a documented parameter of the unified PUT. If the field is silently ignored \\u2014 the expected behaviour for unknown fields \\u2014 the script's verification check (line 213) will fail on any adopter repo that does not already have required_signatures enabled, making the script non-functional as a standalone adopter onboarding tool.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 174 (`required_signatures: true` in jq payload) and 207/213 (SIGS_ENABLED extracted and asserted). GitHub REST API v3 documentation for PUT branch protection lists required_status_checks, enforce_admins, required_pull_request_reviews, restrictions, required_linear_history, allow_force_pushes, allow_deletions, block_creations, required_conversation_resolution, lock_branch, allow_fork_syncing \\u2014 required_signatures is absent. The same pattern exists in configure-020d2-required-check.sh line 104, but SCP-self had required_signatures enabled by the dedicated 020J apply (plan line 76: 'required_signatures live') before 020D2 ran, masking the issue there.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Any adopter repo invoking the script for the first time \\u2014 the primary use case for this adopter helper \\u2014 will see 'verify FAIL: required_signatures=false' at line 213 and exit 1 after the PUT has already mutated branch protection. The log entry is never emitted. The operator cannot determine whether the main protection was applied. The WP-SCP-022 slice 020G acceptance criterion (iv) bootstrap-only posture is satisfied, but the tool is broken for its stated audience.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a dedicated required_signatures enablement call after the PUT: `gh api -X POST \\\\\\\"repos/${REPO}/branches/${BRANCH}/protection/required_signatures\\\\\\\" -H 'Accept: application/vnd.github+json' >/dev/null`. Remove `required_signatures: true` from the jq payload (it has no documented effect). The verification check on SIGS_ENABLED can then remain as-is.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The jq expression `.required_status_checks.contexts | join(\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\")` at line 205 has no null guard on the contexts field. If the GitHub API returns a response where required_status_checks is absent or contexts is null \\u2014 possible if the API shape changes or if the PUT silently omits the field \\u2014 jq exits non-zero with 'null cannot be iterated' and the script exits via set -e with no actionable diagnostic.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 205: CHECKS_CONTEXTS=\\\\\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$AFTER_JSON\\\\\\\" | jq -r '.required_status_checks.contexts | join(\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\")')\\\\\\\". Compare line 204 (CHECKS_STRICT uses `// false` fallback) and line 206 (APPLIED_ADMINS uses `// false` fallback); contexts is the only verification extraction without a null default.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"If the API response is missing required_status_checks.contexts, the script exits silently after a successful PUT. The operator cannot distinguish this from a real verification failure without inspecting AFTER_JSON manually.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Change to: `jq -r '.required_status_checks.contexts // [] | join(\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\")'`. This mirrors the null-guard pattern used on the adjacent fields.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-005\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"The before-state capture at line 190 silently converts all gh API failures \\u2014 including auth errors (HTTP 401), forbidden (HTTP 403), and network timeouts \\u2014 into the fallback JSON object '{\\\\\\\"_note\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"no protection currently configured\\\\\\\"}'. Only HTTP 404 (no branch protection) is the intended fallback; other errors indicate a real problem.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 190: BEFORE_JSON=\\\\\\\"$(gh api -X GET \\u2026 2>/dev/null || echo '{\\\\\\\"_note\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"no protection currently configured\\\\\\\"}')\\\\\\\". The `2>/dev/null` suppresses all stderr, and the `||` catches all non-zero exits regardless of cause. Contrast with line 202 (AFTER_JSON) which has no fallback and lets failures propagate.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"If the operator's PAT lacks administration:read scope or the network is partitioned, BEFORE_JSON logs as 'no protection configured' rather than erroring. The audit trail in branch-protection-log.md will contain a false before-state; post-hoc audit will be unable to determine what protection was actually in place. The PUT at line 195 will subsequently fail on auth errors and surface the real problem, so this is not a silent full failure, but the before-state entry is permanently corrupted.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Capture exit code and stderr separately, or use `gh api \\u2026 2>&1; rc=$?` and check `[ $rc -eq 0 ] || [[ \\\\\\\"$captured\\\\\\\" == *'Not Found'* ]]` to distinguish 404 from other errors. A simpler approach: remove `2>/dev/null`, let real errors surface to stderr, and only suppress the exit code for the 404 case.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-006\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Passing a flag that requires a value without providing it (e.g., `--repo` as the last argument) causes `shift 2` to fail with bash's internal 'shift: can't shift that many' error rather than the usage-aware diagnostic the script emits for other input errors.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 122\\u2013124: `--repo) REPO=\\\\\\\"$2\\\\\\\"; shift 2 ;;`. With `set -e` and only one argument remaining, `shift 2` exits the script via set -e after printing bash's own error to stderr. No `usage` invocation, no exit 2.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Poor operator experience; confusing error message inconsistent with the rest of the script's argument-error path.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Guard each value-consuming case: `if [ $# -lt 2 ]; then echo \\\\\\\"error: $1 requires an argument\\\\\\\" >&2; usage; exit 2; fi` before the `REPO=\\\\\\\"$2\\\\\\\"` assignment.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CORR-007\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"python3 is used for JSON pretty-printing in the log entry heredoc (lines 252, 255) but is not declared as a required command via require_cmd. The fallback (`|| printf '%s'`) is functional but the dependency is silent.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 252 and 255 call `python3 -m json.tool` inside the heredoc; lines 61\\u201363 declare `require_cmd gh`, `require_cmd jq`, `require_cmd git` but not `require_cmd python3`.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"On systems without python3 (Alpine containers, minimal CI images) the fallback fires silently and the log entry contains compact single-line JSON, which is harder to diff in PR review but not incorrect.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add `require_cmd python3` to the header precondition block, or replace with `jq .` (already required) as the pretty-printer: `printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$BEFORE_JSON\\\\\\\" | jq .`.\\\"}],\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Three MAJ findings require fixes before merge. CORR-001 (malformed nested fences) produces broken markdown on every invocation. CORR-002 (SCRIPT_SHA silently empty from non-SCP CWD) corrupts the audit trail in the primary operator scenario. CORR-003 (required_signatures in unified PUT body not a documented API parameter) will cause verification failure and exit 1 on every fresh adopter repo, making the adopter onboarding tool non-functional for its stated audience. Two MIN findings (null guard on jq join, before-state error masking) and two nits. Shell syntax, set -euo pipefail discipline, gh version parsing (including future major versions), argument rejection for unknown flags, ENFORCE_ADMINS argjson handling, restrictions=null, required_pull_request_reviews=null, and the branch-protection-log.md format document are all correct.\\\"}\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness.json:88:        \"evidence\": \"Lines 243\\u2013257 of scripts/enable-required-check.sh. The heredoc emits: (a) \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`markdown on line 243 opening the outer fence; (b) '- **Before:** \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`json' on line 251 \\u2014 this is safe (not a fence because it does not begin at column 0); (c) a bare \\\\`\\\\`\\\\` on line 253 \\u2014 per GFM \\u00a74.5 a closing fence is 3+ backticks with no other content, and this line qualifies, so it closes the outer block; (d) '- **After:** \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`json' at line 254 and its \\\\`\\\\`\\\\` at line 256 are now outside the outer block and render as naked markdown prose. The outer closing fence at line 257 is orphaned. Bash heredoc escaping (\\\\`\\\\`\\\\` \\u2192 literal triple-backtick) is correct; the defect is at the GFM level, not the shell level.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness.json:95:        \"claim\": \"SCRIPT_SHA is silently empty \\u2014 not 'unknown' \\u2014 when the script is invoked from a working directory that is not the SCP repo, because `git log` exits 0 with empty stdout when the path scripts/enable-required-check.sh has no commits in the current repo. The `|| echo unknown` fallback fires only on non-zero exit; it never fires here.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness.json:96:        \"evidence\": \"Line 227: SCRIPT_SHA=\\\"$(git -C \\\"$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || pwd)\\\" log -1 --format=%H -- scripts/enable-required-check.sh 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)\\\". The inner `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` resolves to the CWD repo root (which may be the adopter repo, not the SCP repo). `git log -- <path>` on a path absent from that repo's history exits 0 and emits nothing. SCP_REPO_ROOT (line 232) is a separate variable defined after line 227 and is never used for the SCRIPT_SHA lookup.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness.json:99:        \"mitigation\": \"Move SCP_REPO_ROOT assignment before line 227 and use it in the git log call: `git -C \\\"${SCP_REPO_ROOT}\\\" log -1 --format=%H -- scripts/enable-required-check.sh`. Additionally add an explicit non-empty guard: `[ -n \\\"$SCRIPT_SHA\\\" ] || SCRIPT_SHA=unknown`. Document SCP_REPO_ROOT as a required env var when the operator is not running from the SCP repo root.\",\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/020g/review-correctness-r3-package.json:6:    \"scripts/enable-required-check.sh\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
